What's going to happen? CSSD doesn't know either Voldemort or pastry in Prague.
The new representatives of CSSD at the town hall of the capital will apparently have to overcome a real language barrier to understand each other at the level of the municipal council with their old and new colleagues of the ODS.
The daily Actualne.cz has "tested" the new members of the municipal council belonging to CSSD, i.e. whether they know the scoung that was born in recent years at the town hall, when it was the partners of the current coalition who ran in Prague.
The coded vocabulary that Prague’s political cream started during Mayor Pavel Bem’s previous era describes some of the city’s personalities, situations, and causes.
It is with surprise that it has been discovered that the newly elected officials do not very well understand known terms.
At least they say that.
"Who's Valdemort."
“I really don’t know.”
"I'm a younger in Prague politics reacted Lukas Kaucky, the chosen one in culture on the test of the vocabulary of the "godfather".
And as far as the political veteran is concerned, elected official Karel Brezina reacted in the same way.
For example the meaning of "golf hole".
"That's the only expression you're suggesting I use"
Otherwise I don't use scout.
Vocabulary, of which Aktualne.cz published the expressions in June of this year ahead of the pre-éĺectoral Congress of the ODS, the writing has chosen 6 examples, primarily those related to the Prague City Hall.
The socialists had to explain the meaning of "Voldemort", "the largest golf hole in Prague", go to the pastry", "beef nerve", "betrayal of the capital" and "enter".
Three of the elected officials agreed for the quiz, the fourthieth – Antonin Weinert, who is due to lead education in Prague in the future – initially promised an interview but then did not pick up the phone.
The fifth elected Petr Dolinek rejected the test altogether.
"I don't deal with these things at all and how the representatives of citizen democracy have fun: I won't give my opinion."
"I'm currently dealing with how we're going to understand each other as part of our programs," he said.
As has already been heard, the elected Lukas Kaucky would apparently not recognize in the legend above, that it is the influential Janousek on the entrepreneurs and friends of ex-mayor Roman Bem.
"Voldemort" says nothing á'Kaucky and the only expression he guessed in the test is "the biggest golf hole in Prague".
"This is the Blanka Tunnel," Kaucky said and immediately added: "But I know from the newspapers."
"I'm going to have to pay for a language lesson," he jokes.
Not only the matador Karel Brezina but also Lukas Plachy, the chosen one for the environment, know that the golf hole is Blanko tunnel.
The two Social Democrats know who Voldemort and Lukas Plachy really knows where to find the character – he thinks of the meaning of “going to pastry.”
“It’s, I think, about going into a company,” Plachy is willing to guess that it’s Janousek’s corporate headquarters.
In the same tone Brezina reminds us that he draws his acquaintances rather from the newspapers and not from any vocabulary that he would use.
According to the candidates for the tests, there is no lexicon of argost in social democracy.
“I’ve taken advice with colleagues and it appears that we really don’t have something like that.”
“We’rely tell each other sometimes we use abbreviations and symbols,” says Peter Hlinsky, president of the Social Democratic Party, which Party members call Hulda.
"I'm called Briza at school but I don't know if my colleagues call me like that," says Karel Brezina of the nicknames.
Actuel.cz has tested the name and it works.
The elected Weinert is Grandpa for the members of the Party or the less friendly Petit vieux, the representative Miroslav Poche, who drew attention to him before the elections by the scams during the sponsorship of the party, is nicknamed "the little toy".
The nicknames are also the only ones that the social democrats admit to employ within the party.
It seems that Hulinsky doesn't really like to hear Hulda, but Akutel.cz recorded another nickname for this Prague president.
"Doctor in baton's law" it seems that Petr Hulinsky obtained his title of Doctor of Law JUDr. at the police academy of bratislava.
The government wants to reduce the torture of "sorcerers", has edited a brochure.
In Burkina Faso, women declared witches have common traits.
Rather, they are part of the old generation, cannot work for example for health reasons, are free and do not have children.
The one that becomes "wizard" is isolated so as not to threaten the rest of the village.
“Man rarely has that fate.”
“We also don’t judge healthy young women because they participate in the work and give birth to children,” says a sister who cares in the capital of this West African country, Burkina Faso, in a special establishment of more than 100 women who have been rejected by society.
The local government has decided to intervene against this unusual "social measure".
He edited a brochure that should help limit the witch hunt.
There's got a "hic".
“More than 70 percent of adults in Burkina Faso can’t read,” the Die Standard newspaper warned of the downside of written education.
The accusation of witchcraft is common in other African countries.
In Ghana, Burkina Faso’s neighbor, Ammy Hemma, a 72-year-old woman died after being tortured by three women and a man then burned with oil they sprayed.
It was the BBC's server that transmitted this information.
The criminals say it was the evil eye.
According to their version the oil that is used for ritual unctions has ignited in contact with the woman.
The oil flamed, it seems, when the team wanted to "chase out the bad spirit" of the woman.
Isolation of "sorcerers" is approved by people of all levels of education.
The newspaper Der Standard quoted a 42-year-old entrepreneur from Burkina Faso: “Human spirit eaters have always represented a danger,” he says.
As Burkina Faso strives to reduce the witchhung, the Gambia not later than last year even saw the President himself participate.
"Gambian President Dzammi, after the death of his aunt, launched a broad witch-hunt that appears to have participated in his death and struggled with the black magic to also kill the President."
"Shamans from nearby Guinea who have come to the rescue, brought to the presidential farm in Kanilai, under the protection of police officers and the personal guard of the President, men and women chosen randomly in villages of Gambia," said the organization "Amnesty International.
On the farm, imprisoned people had to drink a herbal potion with hallucinogenic effects that caused in addition to other effects on the kidneys and intestines.
Two prisoners did not survive.
About a thousand people were released on international pressure.
Bakal's tenant: The poles are heating up, he's signed a higher rent.
An imposing elderly man came out through the door of the RPG Byty of the "Coal Baron" Zdenek Bakal, the largest private owner of apartments for rent in the Czech Republic.
He carried under his arm a file with three new leasing contracts for three apartments: his, that of his daughter and his son who is serving a sentence.
Deregulation has increased the rent to these three people.
Instead of paying 3500 the son will have to pay from next year for an apartment 2 + 1 of second category 4100 crowns.
"And there's no hot water and the heating is working on solid fuel," the man who has the power of signature said on his son's new contract.
"But I signed anyway," he explains, motivated and half-resigned.
The Citizens' Association of BytyOKD.cz Apartments, which has described the new rental conditions as "immoral and indecent", advises tenants not to sign and wait until 7 December, when will be published a notice to explain the method to be followed to claim.
But the man reacts to this with a gesture of the hand.
"One says that, the other says something else" by frowning.
Ostrava region becomes the laboratory of deregulation.
After the New Year, this is what awaits most apartments in the Czech Republic whose rents do not correspond to the market price.
Aktualne.cz for this reason decided to personally visit the "laboratory".
The RPG waiting room in Havirovska Street is one of four in the Ostrava region and it is already full on Tuesdays at 09 a.m.
Right next to the door stands a guard from a security company with a device to call customers.
A special button, "rent 2011", is turned on on the touch screen.
A single pressure and a piece of paper with a number comes out of the device.
You don't have time to read your "157" number that your number is already starting to light up on the big screen under the top of the waiting room.
In a split second it's your turn.
After a short moment, an old couple arrives to inquire about the rent, but the new rents do not seem to concern the people in the waiting room.
The majority of people have come to solve common problems as everywhere - demand for repairs, insurance events and others.
In the region of Ostrava, the people of more than 44,000 apartments in RPG’s portfolio have received letters with the new leasing contracts in their mailbox since about mid-November.
RPG Byty offers rent increases from 15 to 38 percent.
The special window for new rents has been open to the public since November 18.
"it's been working for almost a week, so it's stilly," replies, after a moment of hesitation, the employee behind the counter when the client introduces herself as a journalist.
The deragulation and old apartments of Bakal miners are a very sensitive local subject.
The vice-president of the parliament and from the region of Ostrava Lubomir Zaoralek of the Social Democrats mentioned this in a rather sustained way before the elections and this now fills the local newspapers.
"20 years after them, I want a new floor."
"They didn't even change me a put... a board in the toilets, relieves himself in the introduction the man mentioned.
However, it is above all, according to the employees, retirees who come to make sure that they will receive a discount on the new rent price if they now sign as RGP promised in a special offer.
It is a rebate of half the amount of the rent that the other tenants who have signed their new contract will receive before December 15.
But the "deregulation" counter on Havirovaska Street is really not overwhelmed by questions.
"Among the 360 customers who came yesterday, only 40 to 50 came for rent," says the employee at the counter.
And yet RPG porposes both on its website and in the showcases of individual interviews on rents when it is necessary.
If both parties do not agree on rent it is an independent tribunal that will make the decision.
“It’s not about threats, it’s just about informing about the unique and legitimate way to act legally,” RPG said on its website.
"Here no one will go to court" thinks one of the employees of RPG Byty and the man on the street in front of the customer center agrees.
"I've got 13,000 retirement crowns and I still have 5000 wreaths to eat, I don't have enough to go to court," he said.
The employee suddenly feels uncomfortable.
"We'll see."
"Mr. Zaoralek in the media asks that no one signs and therefore the people are waiting," she said.
Rent regulation ends in the Czech Republic with the beginning of 2011 and increases, according to RPG Byty, are an economic necessity.
"And, in addition, the current rents still do not reach the level necessary for the simple maintenance of the fund for housing," recalls Pavel Klimes, director of the residential portfolio of RPG Byty, that it is precisely the negligence of the fund for housing that makes the tenant angry is the main reason for the increase in rent.
However, in 5 years between 2005 and 2010 RPG was able with rents to invest in apartments 4 billion, in four years from 2011 to 2014 it should be 5.2 billion.
The large screen hung to the ceiling in front of the upholstered armchairs of the waiting room on Havirosvka street, however, repeats some information indicated in red.
"The increase in rent in 2011 is 15 per cent and covers no less than 31200 apartments or more than 90 per cent of RPG apartments."
"And it will always be at most 7,50 czech crowns per m2 per month," says the screen.
The 38 percent increase concerns only 400 apartments in RPG’s portfolio.
"In these cases, it's an increase of a really very low sum 19.66 Czech crowns per m2," adds RPG.
Next year the rent will remain the same for 2600 apartments.
RPG Byty promises that deregulation will not cause a radical but gradual increase over 4 to 8 years.
It adds other commitments: all the money of the increase in rents (see the graph) is returned to the apartments in the form of heating savings - new windows and heating.
Those who sign the agreement on the rent increase have priority.
The nursery school is also part of the offices have included architects.
The Avriopoint administrative building in Brno is the new headquarters of the company Arch. Design.
No one could have expected it to also house a mini kindergarten with everything you need - color walls, imaginative furniture, toys and finally a miniature playground on the terrace.
Since October last year, the children of the employees of Arch. design can go there.
Architectonic project company Arch. Design. whose workshops have produced a large number of constructions in Brno, has imagined its mini corporate kindergarten since the first drafts of the Avriopoint building.
It employs about 90 people, mainly the age to have children, and it was important that women architects and project managers could quickly return to work.
At the same time as reduced-time employment contracts, kindergarten is a bonus offered to its employees by Arch. Design. as part of its social program.
Builders and architects thus have the opportunity to combine work taking care for children.
It was the employees of Arch.Design themselves who imagined this kindergarten and with the support of the management they were able to obtain an endowment from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
The mini kindergarten shall be financed by means of the European Social Fund through the Operational Programme of Human Resources and the State Budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to the financing allocations (up to 2.8 mil. of Czech krona). They were able to buy equipment and devices and after two years to ensure qualified staff as well as the start-up.
After using the financing endowment (two years) the company Arch. Design. has the obligation after 3 years to operate the mini kindergarten on its own funds.
The preparation of the project lasted one year and it was the company Economy rating that helped make the staffing request.
Child care is provided by qualified staff, throughout the day a nurse and kindergarten teacher take care of the children; children can go to kindergarten without age limitation but they are more than three years old for most.
As this is a "mini kindergarten" there can only be 5 children present at the same time, but during the day there are more who replace themselves.
The functioning of the school is flexible according to the needs of the parents and the children of all employees have the same right to use this school without regard to the hierarchical position.
Although this is individual care and education, the kindergarten program is comparable to that for preschool education.
The children do not only play but a teaching of quality is also assured - from the beginning to the preparation for the benches we teach English.
The class is equipped not only with imaginative toys and games but also with a computer that children can use.
The presence of small children in society creates a friendly and positive environment.
Parents can control their child at any time and do not have the stress of a delay to pick up the children from a school far from the parents' workplace.
Parents therefore use their working time in the most productive way.
It is not surprising that a natural relationship of loyalty between the employee and the employer is created.
Arch.Design is thus part of the companies that offer a bonus of choice for the recruitment of new employees and that are friendly towards the family and through this program strengthens HR marketing.
The bone house still fascinates after 100 years. Take a look into Gaudi's interiors.
The roof resembles the back of a prehistoric saurian, the staircase to a dinosaur backbone and the balconies to open fish mouths.
This is the Casa Battlo house in Barcelona by the architect Antoni Gaudi.
Due to its organic shapes, the house received from the locals the nickname of Maison en bone.
Organic shapes, wild colors, unusual use of stones, fragments of tiles and mosaic, facade reminiscenting of scales and everywhere present curves, this is all what is typical for the Art Nouveau of Antoni Gaudi.
The frontal facade of the house unfolds its dream lines, its pleasant colors and textures reminiscent nature and animals.
During a more detailed observation we see the geometry, the rhythm and the order thought accurately.
In the night the facade of the house becomes even more dramatic and is lit in such a way that the impression of color shine is reinforced.
The winding and torsion of the shapes also appears in the interiors with the same measurement.
The wood copies in waves the window frames, railings, the decorative elements of the doors, the eyelet and the slats.
The Battlo house that stands in the Eixample district in the shopping street Passeig de Gracia at number. 43, was born from the construction of an 1877 house of the architect Emilio Sala Cortes.
The reconstruction took place for two years and the house was completed in 1906.
The investor, Joseph Battlo, a wealthy entrepreneur, first insisted on the demolition of the old construction and the construction of another brand-new one but quickly accepted Gaudi's idea of an unusual and cheaper reconstruction.
But Casa Battlo’s idea as a multi-dwelling house wasn’t designed by Gaudi alone.
Architect Josep Maria Jujol worked with him.
It is precisely on his project that the decorations were born for an apartment of representation of the owner of the building and the use of the mosaic for the facade.
For the facade also worked with other artists and creators.
The joint work for the plans of the Casa Battlo house began with a successful cooperation between Jujol and Gaudi.
Jujol participated in the construction of Gaudí Casa Mila's house and Guell Park in which there are other works of Gaudi.
The locals dubbed the hot house just after its finishing because of its organic shapes "The Bone House".
Others, having regard to the specific mechanism of opening windows have called it "the house that yawns".
It is said that at the top of the construction there is a sword similar to that of Saint George the patron of Catalonia.
Other elements of sculpture of the construction contain hidden religious motifs.
Gaudi has designed for the house a unique system in its central heating time, with ventilation vents and typical chimneys.
Their 45-degree inclination seems odd.
During the creation Gaudáö did not respect the construction guidelines of the time and did not allow himself to be hindered by them.
At the beginning of the construction he obtained from the city the building permit, knowing, of course, that the data on the reconstruction plans was so vague that two years later during the finish the municipal council was horrified and asked for modifications.
For example, it did not appeal to the members of the municipal council that the pillars of the construction encroach on the public pavement.
There was never any modification, but, on the contrary, only 15 days after the call of the city Josep Battlo asked (and obtained satisfaction) permission to rent the two highest floors.
The owner moved to the lower floors.
In the gallery on the second floor there was a shared living room and in the third a bedroom and a private part of the apartment.
The ground floor was rented from a jewelry store.
The entire six-storey house is shared by a staircase through which you can reach an independent roof terrace.
All right angles and straight lines were canceled by the basic elements of the reconstruction and their replacement by curved walls.
Gaudi also worked on the modern concept of connecting spaces.
The common part can be completely connected by opening the doors of the living rooms and the high society can communicate comfortably or confine itself to the private space for more intimate interviews.
The house with the reconstruction of Gaudáni has received besides new facades a non-traditional roof and two additional floors.
Gaudi’s play with light is also impressive, blue and white glazed tiles with which the inner courtyard is decorated and with skylights.
The two-colored game gives maximum light in all floors of the house.
It was simply enough to bet on the principle that the white reflects more than the dark which on the contrary absorbs the rays of the sun.
The Casa Battlo house, which was inscribed in 2005 in the UNESCO World Heritage Fund, can be visited by everyone.
On the first floor of the house is the most spacious and splendid apartment accessible to the public.
The owners decided to take this step in 2002 on the occasion of the 150th anniversary of the birth of the famous creator.
They continued the visits because they found that it is a lucrative business and they expanded the scope of visits to the common areas and the attic including the roof terraces.
The admission ticket to the monument is really cheap and costs 16.50 euros; students, retirees, groups and tourist card holders have 20 percent discounts.
The sum is still above the price of entry into Gaudi's other constructions in the city.
If you want to feel the real atmosphere of the house with its fantastic turrets, you must quickly find a comrade among the inhabitants of the building and completely visit the house.
How to choose a mattress and box spring: women have criteria other than men.
No ostentatious frame, but rather the quality of the surface reserved for the bedtime, so the mattress and the box spring give a real basis for a good bed.
Of course, they must complement each other harmoniously to please a concrete user.
During the choice several things are determinant: weight, height, age, state of health as well as gender of the owner.
The type of interior in which you will place the mattress and bed base is also important.
Typical example?
For example in the country house where one does not continuously sleep the latex mattresses are not appropriate because they "warm up" slowly.
In addition, they have a weight greater than standard polyurethane mattresses.
It is necessary to return them frequently.
If they do not also have enough ventilation channels in the heart and if they remain, in addition, on a solid board, they retain moisture. Mold can also form there.
Habit is also decisive in choice.
One who spends time abroad where very thick mattresses are frequently used with a softening layer, will hardly feel well on a low and hard surface.
"That's how every Hokeyist returning from NHL complain that they can't sleep there."
“For their tired shoulders, feet and ribs, they needed mattresses with a clumping layer of lazy foam.”
"Such mattresses are also recommended for people who suffer from diabetes who have a disorder of the nervous system that therefore poorly assumes "the information" of the joints and skin," says Sylva Adamczikova who specializes in upholstered furniture.
That's why a few years ago the mattress with lazy foam conquered hokeyists as well as other customers.
"Today everything works well, Tempur, the Italian Magnifex but also the Czech Tropico have for example suitable mattresses.
You don't have to be an NHL guy to be able to enjoy a lazy foam.
This "miracle" simply works.
It optimizes its rigidity and shape according to the heat absorbed or adapts to the heat of your body.
Nothing puts pressure on you and you won’t turn around in the night like after a tough game.
You're just sleeping better.
Lazy foam also works as a protection against nozzles.
For the choice of mattress according to your size, add the length of the feet, i.e. about 21/34 cm.
A man and a woman with a height of 180cm therefore need a bed of 210/220 cm.
The minimum width for everyone for a two-seater bed should be 90 cm i.e
For single-seat beds, singles for example, it is recommended to take at least 120 /140 cm.
It's convenient in case of a visit.
"However it is a simple rule for the width of the bed: when you are lying on your back you should lay your hands in a comfortable way with slightly bent elbows. Helena Prokopova, President of the Association of Executioners and Decorators who has been dedicated for several years to the problem of a healthy sleep and the ergonomics of furniture and the sign in specialized schools.
For a two-seater bed each needs its own base and mattress, so be careful however during to choose the height of the mattress.
Otherwise you will only have to mount the bed base at different heights and this is not possible on all bed frames.
Foam mattresses should have a minimum height of 14 cm, spring mattresses at least 15 cm, of which count for the layer of shape and ventilation at least three centimeters.
"At touch or while sitting on the mattress you must not feel the springs," warns Héléna Prokopova.
Each mattress requires a well-determined type of bed base.
The sleeping bed, when the mattress and the bed base are united like Siamese sisters, has nothing to do in the bedrooms.
The mattresses are very bad; it should not be washed, which is commonly done today.
And since during the night we normally sweat from 0.2 to 0.75 l of sweat it is clear that after 5/7 years, the mattress begins to recall a large (and clogged) sieve.
For this the specialists refuse to advertise over a lifetime of 25 years for a mattress or a lifetime warranty.
But all to his but.
And for this an experienced advice on the part of sellers: for a 120/140cm mattress, give up, for the covering of the mattress, hygiene or washing but rather add a protective mattress that you can easily remove and wash.
And even if nowadays it is common to make coatings with zipper for all the whole, that is to say that after the opening we have two parts (for the washing and the size of the washing machine it is very important!) otherwise the manipulation for large models (and for a single person) becomes when we remove them a fight between a light weight and a heavy truck in a ring.
Do you have in your bed a rigid board instead of a box spring?
Never!
At most for a bed intended for visitors for a single night.
However, equip the board (if there are none) ventilation holes and use the spring mattress with a minimum height of 15cm with sachet springs (from Germany Taschenfederken) and an aeration layer.
A polyurethane mattress should not be part of a bed of this type.
For single-position slat beds with 14/18 slats, latex mattresses or so-called sandwiches (maybe the combination of latex and coconut), are ideal but it will always be better for you to have a mattress with more slats, 28 and above, where the slats are lined in the part of the bottom of the kidneys and that it is thus possible to regulate it.
For mattresses with several positions it is not necessary to take spring mattresses because it does not respond to changes of position.
In addition to adjusting the rigidity of the slats, it is also possible to install what is called a shoulder cradle.
It is a special binding of slats that makes it possible to pressure or even tilt the slat so that the shoulder of the sleeper "integrates" well.
For men who have "the shoulders as wide as your back", it's really an important thing.
This thing is already being considered by mattress manufacturers who for the "masculins" have started to use a softening area at the shoulders.
But they also think of women – they again offer them a softer surface in the hips.
For the most difficult customer there are wing or wafer box springs with what is called a multi-strength suspension.
The pressure spreads in more points and reduces tension.
But of course, be careful, as soon as you use a bad pillow (large and inflated with air) on good mattresses and box spring, the "plus" disappears.
The basic ruler is a very simple pillow: the pillow has to support the head and its tips have to reach only the shoulders, so that the muscles of the shoulders can rest.
The recommended size is 50X60 cm that the Scandinavians for example prefer.
When lying on the side, the pillow should level (with the shoulder cradle of the bed base or the area branched in the mattress) the height of the shoulders.
Modern mattresses still have something in common with the first ones based on straw and herbs.
Manufacturers still use natural materials or mixtures.
The trend is also with an organic mattress.
Petrole-based raw materials from which polyurethane is made are replaced by natural oils, such as soybean oil.
Almost all known manufacturers use soybeans.
“The organic qualifier for mattresses means that the heart is made from natural products,” explains Petr vasicek from Tropico Plus.
The natural materials are also used for mattress coatings; one of the first was aloe vera.
But you can add micro-capsules that develop fragrances from the forest or lavender.
There are differences between men and women.
As birch plant extract so that the mattress coating absorbs heat and cools the body, "cools" the man, coatings "heat up" women.
Red pepper extract accelerates blood circulation and is therefore warmer for the weak sex.
Today's favorites?
Mattresses made from cold foam (it has more pores with a disturbed cellular structure therefore more breathable than standard polyurethane and has more elasticity.
The combination of different rigidities of cold foams gives polyzone mattresses that know exactly where the head, lower back and feet are.
You’ll pay a quality mattress from 7000 kroons and more.
Those that are characterized by their parameters for health have less VAT and are taken as instruments for health.
In the Czech Republic, Gumotex reached first place.
Saving for the mattress and box spring is not easy.
We can from time to time have in the context of special actions by manufacturers or producers two mattresses for the price of only one.
As for example the company Magniflex which offers Duofreeze mattresses with memory foam sewn to the coating for 1990 crowns.
Of course we can take the second for free.
The era of lazy or viscoelastic foams with an open cellular structure and a great thickness has been started with Tempur material at a "cosmic" rate.
It started as a matter for NASA because the astronauts were subjected to the beginning of the enormous load factors.
The Tempur softens to the heat and hardens, on the contrary, to the cold.
It therefore adapts perfectly to the hot human body and distributes optimal pressure.
It is for this ideal against evil sleeping and for an increase in comfort during sleep.
Viscoelastic foams are used not only for mattresses but also for health products, such as special foot cushions that relieve legs and venous system.
For example, Czech manufacturers use EucathermC or Twinsaver foam or of the same type.
Devices to track people are increasingly requested by parents who wish to monitor their children.
Fear for their child causes more and more people to think about watching them through a GPS.
The system, put in place at the beginning to search for cars can just as well be used to trace the movement of people and help for some cases such as that of little Anicka Janatkova.
The instrument is currently as big as a box of matches but will soon be the size of a watch.
The case of little Anicka moved a large number of people and they began to look for ways to better protect their children.
Even if the price is to disturb their privacy.
“We’re trying to tell everyone that it’s not spying but only another way to reduce the likelihood that there will be another tragedy,” says Petr Pilin of MAcro Weil which is one of the suppliers of tracing devices in the Czech market.
"I admit that there may be cases of misuse of such devices, such as putting it in the bag of a person who is not aware, but I have not yet encountered such cases."
"The majority of people are customers who actually buy the product for safety reasons," Pilin adds.
According to him, children should always know for what purposes "the box" is used and how to use it in case of an emergency.
The children's psychologist, Dana Bockova, draws attention that if the child does not want to carry on him a thing of this type, he will get rid of it.
“And even the little ones can show great creativity when it comes to deliberately getting rid of something when they don’t want to be under control.”
"Mobile phones, which can cut off like these tracing devices, have already led parents to long-standing expectations," Bockova said.
According to her, the fact that this type of device can help in the event of aggression is very questionable.
"If it's a prepared abduction, the author will do everything so that the child doesn't have an electronic device that can locate it."
"Currently, it's the phone."
"As soon as tracing equipment develops, they (the criminals) will also look for them because the sale will be supported by an advertising campaign."
"In such cases criminals will always be ahead of the police," the psychologist said.
She still admits that such devices can help during an assault unprepared by a pedophile.
According to Robert Cech of RC Monitoring, which offers tracing services for both cars and people, says having this type of device can help, even if it is destroyed in the event of an assault.
"The device can be parameterized in such a way that it is in regular emission after a determined period."
"In the case where the device is destroyed, the last position of the person concerned remains."
"The more expensive devices also have an inclinometer that immediately signals the position of the person who slipped and finds himself in a lying position without moving, "said Cech.
According to him, so-called smart mobile phones that are equipped with a navigation system, can offer a comparable service after formatting it with a special software.
The problem with current tracing systems is that it is either a device similar to those that is worn as a pendant the size of a box of matches or the smallest telephone devices.
We can always lose them.
According to Pilina of the company Macro Weil, which is why tests are currently being carried out to make devices that look like common digital watches.
"This will have the advantage that the potential aggressor won't be able to recognize the tracing device."
"Or he'll realize it after a while, which will have given the police a little longer," he added.
Whether the tracing device is developed in the form of a bracelet or pendant, it will always use the same technology as to trace cars.
It is a combination of systems to acquire a position using GPS (which is commonly used in autonavigation) and a function of a mobile phone that alerts the current position of a vehicle.
The army has so many detonating cords that it could go around the Czech Republic with it, but it can only last for two years.
The army bought for 445 km of cords used for explosives.
Already last year, the Ministry of Defense had bought 224 km.
Therefore, the army has reserves for 225 years.
The problem is that the lifespan of cords is two to four years maximum.
The army bought detonating cords from the limited company STV Group for 40 million crowns.
The General General Staff gave as a reason for the purchase of an enormous amount of detonating cords from MF DNES that soldiers use 60 km per year.
According to the reserve calculations to be held made by the pyrotechnicians of the army, whom MF DNES has contacted, it emerges that even generals, officers with a command, secretaries or housekeeping personnel should have an explosive exercise each year.
Alexandre Vondra, Minister of Defense and Vlastimil Picek, commander of the army are no exception.
Only to argue the annual consumption of the army of 60 km of detonating cords.
At this distance corresponds to 25 000 explosions.
And even if that were true, the army reserves could support it for 10 years.
If on the contrary it would be true that for the basic training of each of the new professionals it is enough at more than 2 m of detonating cords, as the experts say, it means that the army with its new purchase has ensured - with also the consumption of special units and engineering - reserves for the 225 years mentioned.
Yet the soldiers of the Rapid Intervention Brigade recently complained to Minister Vondra that they have almost no ammunition for their handguns and that shooting sessions are limited due to the need for money.
About the lack of linen we don't talk about it.
"And I don't take the lead if they buy such a large amount of detonating cord from us."
“And that’s their decision and they really know what they’re doing.”
"For us the military is a good customer that we strive as shopkeepers to satisfy," says STV Group Commercial Director Jan Decky.
Jan Pejsek, spokesman for the Ministry of Defence, argues that hundreds of km of detonating cords are purchased by the fact that the army will have these "munitions" in reserve for 10 years.
Jan Decky of STV Group, however, reports only four years of the life of detonating cords and pyrotechnicians of the police and army of only two years.
"If they're perfectly stored in depots, it can go even up to four years," says one who doesn't want his name to be published with regard to criticism from the military.
The period of living past, each munition must be tested to see if it remains honest and does not threaten the life of the soldier.
However, these tests are not free and still cost money.
The department's other argument is a discount due to the quantity for 445 km of detonating strings.
"They cost us 60 percent less than last 245 km's supply last year," said spokesman Pejsek, referring to the defense ministry's buyers.
Only to compare: the annual consumption of detonating cords of police pyrotechnicians who neutralize the ammunition of the Second World War or those after the Soviet army, does not exceed 1500 meters.
And the police always only order the necessary amount of ammunition to be used before the end of their lifetime.
This has been established for the safety reasons of pyrotechnicians and to be able to be able to obtain more modern and more reliable types of detonating cords.
The Ministry of Defense now has major problems for the neutralization of dozens of tons of ammunition from the time of the Warsaw Pact when the strong Czechoslovak army of two hundred thousand men was preparing to move, heading to the Rhine.
What awaits in a few years this Czech army that MF DNES has contacted: neutralize hundreds of kilometers of detonating old ropes that it buys today "cheap".
Until today I benefit from contacts that I have obtained thanks to the competitions.
The franchise sale of his hotel helped Lukas Pytloun for the winner of the competition of the best Individual Entrepreneur.
The winner of the competition of the best Individual Entrepreneur for the past year was Lukas Pytloun, a 28-year-old entrepreneur.
His adventure towards success is like in a movie.
Lukas Pytloun loved traveling.
And abroad, he realized that in Libérec it was missing a quality hosting site at a reasonable price.
From his savings on what he had earned when he worked abroad and with the help of a bank loan he bought a small building of 5 apartments half demolished.
Knowing that he was still a student in the third year of graduate studies, it was a courageous action.
"The relationship between the quality and the price of accommodation in our area made me want to do something," says Lucas Pytloun.
He began to rent the first rebuilt apartment to foreign workers.
He reinvested the profit in the building he converted into a pension with 54 beds.
Since then, his entrepreneurial action has allowed him to open in Libérec 4 pensions, two style restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of nearly 300 beds of all categories of accommodation.
The most comfortable of them was until recently the four-star Pytloun design hotel.
This year there came another one of identical quality.
"We've just signed a contract with a hotel in Ostrova U Tise where we sold our franchise concept and other candidates for the Pytloun hotel franchise have come forward", last year's No. 1.
"And for the sale of the license for the start-up of a large-scale hotel the victory in the competition of the entrepreneur of the year served well, adds Pytloun with gratitude.
The victory brought him more popularity and to be known in Libérec and its surroundings.
"People recognized me in the streets, often congratulated me and, I have to say, it was very pleasant," he recalls.
It recommends participation in this contest to all those who strive to do something.
You don’t have to win, but any opportunity to be visible is good.
He says he uses his contacts and acquaintances until today that he could not get in another way.
"With those I met thanks to the competition, I began to maintain commercial relations regularly.
According to him, the reputation related to successes has also had an impact on employees.
And he's got about 70.
"I observe at home above all the loyalty and pride to have engaged in something that has generally been recognized."
"It's not, by far, the only result of my personal work, but the result of a good job of all of us together.
"Only my name is pushed forward," says Lucas Pytloun modestly.
I wish my successor a better year than we had in Farmet.
The euphoria of victory has been greatly replaced by the effects of the crisis.
Thanks to the meaning of the goal of the winner Farmet has the worst moments behind himself.
Who picked up the prize in Slovansky dum for the first place of the competition for the Firm of the year organized by Vodaphone last year is Karel Zdarsky the owner of the Farmet company.
The jury rewarded the efforts with which it transformed a tooling workshop in Ceska Skalice into a firm with a turnover of 100 million crowns.
"I can say that the award has encouraged not only my collaborators and increased our self-confidence to fight the competition," said the winner of last year's contest.
At the beginning of the history of the company Farmet there was the desire to do something useful.
And so when the state offered Karel Zdrasky's father to praise the small workshop as compensation after the confiscation of property by the communists, he did not hesitate for long, refused a career in a firm where he worked at the time, and created a company with a few comrades in his father's entourage for the manufacture of plastic bathtubs.
Not very long after they began to specialize in vegetable oil presses.
After eighteen years, it has become the largest manufacturer of automatic presses in Central Europe.
The company was known even in the non-specialized public after the success achieved in the competition through the media.
“Not only our customers started to really get to know the Farmet brand but also the foreign partners who began to realize that they were collaborating with a company that achieved the greatest success in its country of origin,” Zdarsky said.
But the past year did not pass at Farmet only in the euphoria of victory.
The company's evaluation was based on the economic results of 2008 and in part in 2009.
And precisely in 2009 it was the most difficult year for the sale of engineering machines.
At the height of the crisis, which did not really affect the citizens, companies were forced to stop their investment projects and begin to limit their purchases of new materials and technologies.
"Of course, we have not escaped this situation."
"The turnover has fallen by 40 percent, and we must limit our production and even lay off," Zdarsky said.
A viable society must know that from time to time unpopular action must be taken.
But they managed the situation properly and retained benefits even though they were inferior to those they had before the crisis.
At Farmet, however, they went uncompromisingly to invest in research and development.
"We built with the endowments a training and research center, strengthened the presentation of the company in international exhibitions and won the main awards in national exhibitions," says Zdarsky.
Everything leaves us to think that the crisis is behind them.
The sales figures go up and with them the number of employees too.
"To the new winner I wish this pleasant feeling of victory but in a calm atmosphere," says Karel Zdarsky.
An investigation that removes senseless regulations.
The investigation into the absurdity of the year is for the fourth time part of the business clan.
Some absurd regulations have been removed.
The unglorious winner of this year's inquiry into the Absurdity of the Year has been the obligation to bring to the offices documents that public servants can obtain online.
More than one-third of 4000 voters voted for this.
For example, the administrations of social affairs requested a paper excerpt from the commercial register while the official can get it in two mouse clicks.
It's changing thanks to the investigation.
"The Czech Social Insurance Administration will no longer request an excerpt from the commercial register for insurance payment controls," said Jana Buranova, spokesman for the Czech Social Insurance Administration (CSSZ).
To bring out all the regulations and decrees that companies and contractors must fulfil in the Czech Republic and to suppress those which are absurd is the meaning of this contribution of the Absurdity of the year.
This survey is again this year for the fourth time part of the business clan organized by Hospodarske noviny and still has greater popularity.
If last year 3000 voters came forward, it was at least 4000 this year that brought their voice.
And this is not surprising, there are in our country the most various legal obligations that only slow down the company, and this, more and more.
"We may be the number of documents to be completed for tax returns in the first places in the world," says Karel Havlicek of the Association of Small and Medium Enterprises and Entrepreneurs of the Czech Republic.
Simplification of entrepreneurial activity has become a proclaimed goal of all governments in recent years.
Compared with the whole world the situation in the Czech Republic does not improve at all.
According to the World Bank the Czech Republic ranks 74th in terms of the conditions for the company, 8 more places compared to last year.
The Absurdity of the Year competition was set up for the first time in 2006 and continues each year under the same scenario.
Anyone can report administrative and bureaucratic nonsense every year from April to the end of September, the last delay before the conclusion of the competition.
To report there is the website www.firmaroku.cz. Then, the contest organizer chooses seven candidates for the "anti-prix" and lets the public vote during the month of October on the site www.firmaroku.cz/absurdita and on www.ihned.cz.
Absurdity which has received the greatest number of votes is declared victorious.
Until the last survey last year, it is a hundred, or more exactly 105 regulations and decrees that have lost their sense of existence.
Remarks are sent by entrepreneurs but also professional unions and professors.
Among the "finalists" of the competition, therefore among the nominees is, for example, the obligation that highway and expressway maintenance vehicles pay the tolls, or that contractors with twenty employees, repaint two offices and two toilets every two years.
A list of seven proposals is published on the competition website.
The proclamation of the competition, which always begins at the beginning of November in Wenceslas Square in Prague, is part of a happening in which personalities of political and public life regularly participate.
This year, for example, Eva Svobodova, director of the Association for Small and Medium Enterprises of the Czech Republic or Petr Kuzel, President of the Chamber of Commerce of the Czech Republic, attended the highlight of the competition.
"Economic prosperity can't work when the state puts people in the legs of entrepreneurs and for that my goal is to remove, every year, at least three dozens of paragraphs that today only complicate the company," Martin Kocourek, the Minister of Industry and Trade.
The effort to remove absurd decrees is not a sleep in the water.
In 2007, what did not appeal to people was the obligation of contractors to report three days before to the office for the one-on-one company at the location of the company, the intention of going to an exhibition or a pilgrimage with a stand.
This obligation was abolished with the amendment of the Sole Proprietary Enterprise Act.
The second most acclaimed obligation in 2007, which was to report a 30-day service trip to the Labour Office for a foreign employee, has been removed with the text of the Green Card Act.
And it's ok until the third session of the contest.
Absurdity of the year 2009 was the debate for physiotherapists to have a large mirror in their office, a refrigerator and two automatic scales.
This was changed by ordinance from the Ministry of Health last year – the requirement to have a refrigerator was removed.
A new category rewards socially responsible firms.
Ecological, humanitarian, training or sports projects are evaluated.
The novelty of this year's Hospodarske Noviny competition, which has already existed for 5 years, is the category known as the Company Responsible for the Year 2010.
The objective of this contest is reasonable and fair business support.
"We want to support companies that monitor their company's impacts on the environment and carry out a socially responsible activity," says Patrick Bartholdy, the regional manager of the mobile phone operator, Vodaphone, who is the guarantor and promoter of this competition, as in the case of the competition for the rebel of the year.
For this first year of this sub-category of the competition of the firm of the year 2010, organized by the firm Vodaphone, have registered the companies that do not succeed with a single long-term goal for the company.
The adventure of each of them is rich with inspirations and an independent article should be edited.
Although the purpose of their company is different from each other, their support and forecasting activity can be seen as the denominator of their efforts.
In one case, this applies to people with disabilities and the personal or health problems of their employees.
Among the fourteen finalists for the whole of the Czech Republic is no example the Prague software company Et Netera which supports the creation of informal sports teams of their employees who then participate in many tournaments in different sports disciplines.
Another example of a firm responsible for the Pardubice region is Isolit-Bravo which has been the main contributor to the endowment fund that sponsors children with disabilities for 17 years.
To the fact of taking care of employees with health problems, we can also mention the old Podebrad paper factory which employs more than 70 per cent and which, for this purpose, has obtained the title of the firm Responsible for the Central Bohemian region and which placed third in the region in the competition of the Firm of the year organized by Vodaphone.
In Olomouc, it is MiRyz Soft, a financial company, which won for having introduced flexible working hours and even decreased up to 35 hours per week on average and, without, proportionately, reduction in wages.
The jury also deferates the title because it buys primarily from Czech manufacturers, supports a day-to-day activity in its close environment and is concerned about the environment.
“We strive to recycle as much as possible so that our business activity and administration have the least impact on the environment,” explains Miluse Ryznarova, the company’s executive director.
IT Enlogit of the Usti Region also thinks about the health of its employees and has set up an ergonomic chair and keyboard for each laptop, encourages their work at home and uses the catering services of a local protected factory for its actions.
"We also support the The Man in the Shadow foundation that helps the local Roma community," says Ondrej Suchy the company's representative.
At the very last place is, also for its activities, a washing and cleaning company in the Karlovy Vary region, which participates in the retirement insurance of its employees in the form of bonuses above the standards.
"In our society we contribute to food, transportation, increased qualifications and recreation," says Joseph Strapac of the Bruntal construction company Czasch, winner for the Moravia-Silesia region.
Were automatically enrolled in the Responsible Company category, all the regional finalists for Vodaphone's Firm of the Year competition, who had responded to the questionnaires as an attachment of the document for registration and had returned it to the organizer before August 15.
Answers poorly completed or incomplete in the questionnaire were removed from the contest by the organizer.
"If the representatives of the participating firms had not answered the questions, they could not participate in the competition of the firm Responsable but were not disqualified from the "main" competition the Vodaphone Firm of the Year."
"Independent entries aren't possible because it's a sub-category of the firm's main competition of the year," says Karel Goldman of Bison&Rose.
At the Lands Fund in Plzen, the police made arrests during a public competition
In the Lands Fund building in Plzen, anti-corruption police have made arrests in recent days.
The detectives handcuffed to the local entrepreneur and real estate trader Lubos. G.
He had endeavoured to manipulate precisely a public call for tenders for the settlement of a request for restitution in such a way that the proposed land was obtained by a particular candidate.
He had to pay to Lubos G. half a million crowns.
"For the moment I can only confirm that the police are prosecuting a person for insider trading in connection with a call for tenders and a public competition," prosecutor Antonie Zelena's head of the Public Prosecutor's Office said in connection with further explanation because the investigation is ongoing.
The policies released the 46-year-old contractor after 24 hours.
To find out how it could affect a public competition, one can only speculate with respect to such little official information.
To a question from Pravo, the spokeswoman of the Lubomira Cerna Field Fund indicated that the police had actually made an arrest in Plzen at a separate place of work during the public competition.
"It was a client and in no way an employee," she said.
The public competition according to Cerna has the real name "Call to propose rights for the transfer of land according to the Land Act" and is put in place when several candidate beneficiaries of law appear.
"These persons propose, in the context of the appeal, a higher value of their rights to territories that must be settled before the value of the land is announced."
"According to the proposed rights a ranking of acquirers is put in place and the Land Fund then transfers the land to the person who has proposed the highest rights," explained the spokesman of the Fund.
Lubos G. had, it is clear, the information concerning the people who had signed up for the appeal and since he himself is, also, a recipitous person because he had purchased a large number of restitution rights, he had proposed to candidates in the field not to participate in the competition.
And he was probably cashing in by withdrawing.
This would explain why he had so much money in his possession at the time of his arrest.
Lubos G. had contacts well above the FdT (Fonds des Terrains).
It was included in the case of the ex-head of the FdT Vaclav Sankot who was prosecuted in criminal proceedings for having edited land for which the decision to set up a construction had already been edited.
Precisely Lubos G. had obtained, for example, land for the construction of public communications, concretely in place where the artery of access to the Borske Pole industrial zone was to be built.
Later he exchanged it with the town hall for a building in the historical center of the city whose value is far superior.
The town hall was forced to accept because otherwise it risked jeopardizing the construction by blocking the land.
Households will pay an addition of a hundred crowns on a monthly basis for housing.
Households will have to pay a surcharge not only for all types of energy but also for water and sanitation, heating supplies and in some places rents will also increase.
From January onwards, families will have to draw from their purses up to a hundred crowns monthly more than now.
Electricity increases by 4.6 percent, gas is almost 2 percent more expensive, in some localities you’ll have to pay up to one-tenth more for water supply, and in many places central heating supplies will also be more expensive.
The increase in electricity is expected to be close torn for households in apartments for the 100 crowns annually more than at present.
However, all suppliers have lowered the prices of electricity given the significant development to encourage the manufacture of renewable energies, especially photovoltaic batteries.
The gas increases moderately but of course at a time when people need it the most to heat and therefore have the greatest consumption.
We can therefore also have a hundred more crowns.
Although electricity and gas are increasing throughout the Czech Republic, the change in the price of central heating differs from city to city.
It is mainly the largest municipalities that are planning the price increase.
For example households in Pardubice or Hradec Kraloce will pay from January for central heating almost 50 more crowns per month, Libérec’s families will pay more than 1000 kroons annually.
For the inhabitants of Prague is preparing an increase of 9 percent for water and sanitation in the event that the city increases infrastructure rents.
If rents don’t change pragoises will pay an extra 6.8 percent.
The price in Prague will still be among the lowest in comparison with the whole country.
In North Bohemia, where prices are already among the highest, water and sanitation increase by 7.4 percent.
Rent prices will increase, for example, significantly in the 450 thousand apartments for rent from January, i.e. at the end of regulation.
For example the company CPI Byty which owns in Usti nad Labem almost 2500 apartments, has decided an increase of up to 100 percent.
Those who do not agree with the new rent increase will be brought to court by the company.
Regulated rents that people pay in nearly 300 thousand apartments could increase next year by 10 percent.
The Czech children get drunk with rum and fernet. The beer is no longer fashionable and acts very slowly.
Fernet, rum, vodka and exceptionally also the best brand of whiskey are stolen from parents.
They are drinks that most often bring those who get drunk, who are not yet fifteen years old, in the hands of the doctor.
Children who are cared for in hospital for recurrence are no exception.
This is the result of the experience of physicians who encounter cases similar to the admissions of the children's departments of the hospitals.
"Beers or wines aren't fashion anymore because it takes time to be drunk."
"The trend is really strenuous to strong liquors," says Pravo Ludek Pelikan, head of the children's department of Horovice Hospital in Central Bohemia.
"When they manage to steal a daddy bottle in the cabinet, it's sometimes Wisky but it's fernet and vodka that prevail."
"Vodka has this advantage that they can mix it with fruit juice and so do not feel it," explains the head of the department.
Eva Jurinova, a spokesman for Motol Hospital, also proves him right.
"It's usually rum or fernet."
"It is today alcohol in fashion especially in elementary schools."
"In some cases, it was slivovice that they brought back from home," Jurinova added.
According to the Head of Department Pélikan, seeing drunken children between the ages of thirteen and fourteen in hospital admissions is a normal phenomenon.
Before working in Horovice since last year, he was in Motol where he practiced ten years in pediatrics where ambulances brought scholars from the wide areas completely drunk.
"A young girl was hospitalized that way four times
"She began her career at the age of twelve when she was brought with 3.5 for a thousand of alcohol in the blood with an additional mixture with ecstasy," the doctor described the particularly important case.
"She was at Holesovice's Exhibition Centre at a party."
"What's interesting is that it didn't look weird to the mother that his daughter was absent 48 hours," said the Department's director.
It is precisely the disinterestedness of the parents or their extraordinary tolerance in view of the fact that their offsprends indulge in the drink which are the main cause of the development of alcoholism of children.
According to the specialists, however, we do not speak of addiction, in its strict sense, for children.
"Under the alcoholic term we mean a chronic addiction to alcohol of several years with all the resulting phenomena but that we do not find in children."
"In those who use repeatured alcohol, it is rather a significantly unfavourable socio-familiar back-ground," explained the Head of Department Pélikan.
According to spokeswoman Jurinova, about forty drunken children were hospitalized this year until mid-November in the pediatrics clinic in Motol.
Are integrated into statistics only cases with severe intoxication requiring hospitalization.
Intoxic children who are after emergency care taken home by the parents or the police, are not counted.
In addition to the cases of children who sit and drink a bottle of liquor after school, according to Pélikan there are three seasonal waves with which we can count: the day of the witches, St. Sylvestre and the end of the school year.
"Either they drink out of spite after poor results or on the contrary they celebrate the beginning of the holidays."
"Of course I went through Witches Day an admission of 7 drunken children at once," Pélikan said.
The drunken child who spent the night at the hospital detoxing under medical supervision is paradoxically in a better position than the one who returned home and suffers the day after the "wharf of wood".
Morning headaches caused by dehydration and lack of glucose are eliminated by an infusion of glucose and nutrients that the body needs.
"They're for that better off since they're having a great evening and then a sweet awakening and nothing hurts them."
“They’re only astonished at where they’re,” Pélikan adds, knowing that from time to time it spoils the good mood of patients at least with the grade to pay for their care.
Ambulance transportation and intensive care bed hospitalization can range up to tens of thousands of crowns.
"From time to time, I applied this as a means of education by counting on these children how much their intoxication cost and that they could spend the whole summer in seasonal work to repay a single evening," added Pélikan.
However, according to him, the health insurance companies in their regressive managements, which are expected to recover care-related burdens from parents, do not deal with cases of drunken children.
Two L-159 planes are for the first time in foreign hands in Spain.
Two L-159 of all five aircraft, for which the Ministry of Defense exchanged Casa C-295M transport aircraft, took off Tuesday for Seville in Spain where the aircraft manufacturer Casa-EADS sailed.
This is the first time that devices of this type have become the property of someone other than the Czech army.
However, they have been abroad as part of exercises or demonstration flights.
These are the two-seater versions of the L-159T1 which were until now in service in the Czech army and which were in flight.
Three remaining devices are single-seater versions of the L-159.
For the time being, they are stationed at Aéro Vodochody and the commercial intermediary - Omnipol - took them into account last week.
According to Jan Pejsek of the press department of the Ministry of Defense, the physical departure of aircraft from Vodochody is currently unknown.
"The L-159 single-seater aircraft planned for the exchange have been taken on long-stored aircraft without causing additional charges or investments for the Ministry of Defence."
"The two-seaters L-159 T1 have been traded in the state," Pejsek said.
He added that the army would not remain without a two-seater since the ministry signed in May 2009 with Aero Vodochody an agreement worth 200 million crowns for the transformation of four unused L-159 single-seaters into two two-seaters.
The army took delivery of the first aircraft in July, the second scheduled for delivery at the end of this year.
According to Pejsek EADS has pledged to introduce airplanes into the equipment of certain Alliance countries.
"In the contract it is clearly stated that whoever takes L-159 aircraft into account is EADS and that they will be used to train pilots from NATO member countries."
"This is therefore the case of the one who takes into account determining how to achieve this," Pejsek said.
Hospodarske noviny announced, some time ago, that the Spanish army is not interested in the L-159.
The exchange of five L-159s for a Casa aircraft and the purchase of three other aircraft for 3.5 billion crowns was endorsed last year by the government of Mirek Topomanek.
The Ministry of Defence did not call for tenders and contacted EADS directly.
the Directorate of Defence argued that the exchange ensures a future export of the L-159 abroad.
The Czech army bought from Aéro Vodochody 72 aircraft and only uses 24, wanting to sell the rest.
Purchasing Casa planes will likely have legal implications.
However, the European Commission has lodged a complaint against the Czech Ministry of Defence with the European Court of Justice because the aircraft should have been chosen in the context of a call for tenders on a European scale and in no case by contacting a manufacturer directly.
The army has already taken delivery of three Casa planes, the last one is expected to arrive at Prague Kbely airport in two weeks.
A longer index than the ringer is a sign of low risk of cancer
Men who have a longer index than the ring finger are exposed to a lesser risk of having prostate cancer.
This is the surprising conclusion of a study published Wednesday by the British magazine Cancer.
It may depend on the level of the male hormone testosterone.
“Our findings show that relative finger length serves as a simple risk test for prostate cancer, especially for men before the age of 60,” said study co-author Ros Eeles, a professor at the Institute for Cancer Research at the University of Warwick.
The finger could thus help determine which men should undergo prostate cancer testing, including combining with genetic risk or other aggravating factors such as family health history, Eeles added.
During the period of 1994-2009 she followed with her colleagues in Great Britain more than 1500 patients with prostate cancer and at the same time nearly three thousand healthy men.
More than half of the participants in the study had the right-hand index finger shorter than the ringer.
For them as well as for the 19 percent of those who had fingers of the same length, we found the same probability of prostate cancer.
However, in the case of those who had a longer index than the annular the risk fell to 33 per cent, for men before the age of 60 the decrease in risk reached 87 per cent.
The length ratio of the tword-fingers cited - formed during the prenatal period - indicates in particular different levels of sex hormones to which the child in the uterus is exposed.
Less testosterone corresponds to a longer index.
Previous research had shown that it is precisely testosterone that helps in the development of prostate cancer.
Professor Vacla Paces, a renowned Czech geneticist and former president of the Academy of Sciences, told CTK that he is aware of this study but that it does not seem to him that the relationship between finger length and cancer is "completely demonstrated".
The Czech Republic is under the snow, highways D1 and D5 are closed.
The Czech Republic like most of Europe is this Thursday morning covered by snow.
On the roads and sidewalks, there is most of the time compact snow, clearers have worked all night in many places.
The D5 motorway is closed in the morning between the 89th and 93rd kilometre towards Rozvadov due to several accidents of trucks and cars and the road D1 in Brno in the direction of Prague suffers the same fate.
The dispatcher of the Central Automobile Club (UAMK) told the information
In the 91st kilometre a truck was crashed, at the 93rd two heavy trucks crashed, the accident left injuries.
A number of cars also ended up in the ditch.
The problems are also on the D1 motorway at 185th kilometre towards Prague.
Two trucks crashed into it
Drivers are expected to exit just after Brno in the 190th kilometre or go by Brno and then enter the 182nd kilometer to Kyvalka.
A line of cars of several kilometers formed at this place.
Also on the D11 motorway, there is an accident and just on arrival in Prague.
In Prague, there is a layer of snow several centimeters thick, the thermometers indicate - 7 degrees Celsius.
Lines of cars are created for example in Prosek, Sterboholska Street is also clogged.
The safest means of transportation is the subway.
Trams fall behind, for example, on the line between Place Charles (Karlove Namesti) and Place de la paix (Namesti Miru).
At eight o'clock in the morning the Prague Crisis General Staff has yet to meet.
The railways and the airport also face problems.
The Czech Railways have already had to eliminate because of the snow 7 fast connections.
Lines, for example from Prague to Kralupy or from Prague to Beroun have problems
Travellers should monitor the current situation.
Ruzyne airport has been closed since Wednesday 11 a.m. in the night until 5 a.m.
Most roads in Southern Moravia are already rollable but redoubling with vigilance.
On the road to Vyskov, three lateral roads are impassable, on the road to Brno there begins to be gusts of snow in places and on the road to Blansko the chains are mandatory to pass certain communication lanes in the places above.
The Vyskov region has been among the most affected places.
Until now the axes are closed: Rousinov-Slavkov, Bohdalice-Kucerov and also Saratice-Milesovice
To clean them it may be necessary to send snow removers and snow strawberries.
We can't estimate at the moment when they're rollable.
On the road to Hodonin prudence is de rigueur in the white Carpathian region.
On the roads that are, having regard to the protection of nature, maintained only by the sprinkling of inert products, there is still a layer of 5 centimeters of snow warns CTK.
In the Czekish, it fell between two to 10 centimeters of snow; it fell the most in North Bohemia, often with up to 18 centimeters of snow.
In the Moravia-Silesian region, it fell up to 15 centimeters of snow.
Sneedy disappears in the Czech Republic gradually.
The Americans have a new "smart weapon"
The U.S. military sent a new "smart" weapon to Afghanistan, known only as the XM-25 code.
Generals believe that this new automatic rifle will have an influence on the conflict there with the Taliban and "will change the game" for their own benefit.
The new weapon is like a super-precise cannon
Its projectiles are laser guided with an accuracy of a few meters.
The special 25-mm-caliber munitions developed for this rifle and containing a highly shattering explosive, actually works like small grenades.
Ammunition can be programmed in such a way that it will explode in contact with the target but also at any point in their ballistic trajectory.
We can therefore use it against an enemy hiding behind an obstacle, a wall or a ditch.
It is also not protected from above and when it falls on it a murderous rain of steel chips, it can put it out of combat too.
According to Richard Audette who took part in the development of the XM-25, the new rifle is the first infantry weapon that uses "smart" technology.
"The soldier uses it essentially in such a way that he finds the target, sends him a laser beam that determines the distance, thus obtains a precise point of sight, according to which he corrects his aim site and presses the trigger.
"Let's say you aimed at 543 meters."
"When you squeeeze the trigger, the weapon loads and shoots at the distance of 543 meters more or less 1, 2, 3 meters, said Audette.
400 jobs relocated
The Fujitsu Group relocates its subsidiary from Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
Next year in July, 400 employees will transfer to Astro Park their workplace that is currently located at the Mareinbader Square office building in the Bad Homburg area.
The Japanese group Fujitsu is relocating its branch from Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
As the giant specializing in the manufacture and maintenance of computers said on Wednesday, next year in July, the workplace of 400 men and women, located so far on the Marienbader Square of the city of Bad Homburg, will be transferred to Astro Park, a huge office complex located on Lyoner Straße Street in Frankfurt’s Niederrad district.
A spokeswoman for Fujitsu explained that in the medium term, the group is expected to consolidate another 100 jobs still scattered so far in the region.
The rental offer has been extremely attractive. In addition, Astro Park's open space offices are better suited to employees' mode of work.
Also in Bad Homburg, Fujitsu was just a tenant.
Managing Director Bernd Wagner said: "Frankfurt-Niederrad is for us an essential location with remarkable infrastructure".
He argues that the connection to the airport is precisely of great importance for a global company like Fujitsu.
The new relocation of a large company inside the region sparked yesterday the joy of the winners and caused the disappointment of the losers.
The head of economic affairs of the municipality of Frankfurt, Mr. Markus Frank (CDU), said that once again, a "planetary actor" had thus been conquered.
It is an "extraordinary profession of faith relating to the place of implantation".
Fujitsu is suitable for the city and its location is an important impetus for Niederrad.
Bad Homburg Mayor Michael Korwisi (the Greens) regrets Fujitsu’s decision.
Management briefed the municipality very early on this intention.
"It goes without saying that we offered our assistance to this company," Korwisi said.
However, the friendly relations could not have changed the difficulties of the computer manufacturer against the owner of the building of Place Marienbader.
In the end, economic reasons would have been decisive.
It is now a question of preventing such an exceptional place from remaining unoccupied for a long time.
"I have the hope that the owner and landlord of the building will be cooperative."
Ten years ago, the building of the Place de l'Hôtel de Ville, used so far by Fujitsu and the nearby "small vertical", as well as the residure of the buildings of Place Marienbad, were part of the controversial construction project of the spas.
The result of a joint project of the municipality and a developer of private projects, a new entrance of the city was created between the station and the Town Hall. It aroused many criticisms, not only because of the impressive layout of the surfaces that once served partly as parking, but also because of the height of the buildings.
In 2001, the rating and investment consulting company, Feri, the manufacturer of computers for travel distribution systems, Start Amadeus, as well as Fujitsu, a manufacturer of computers at the time still associated with Siemens, moved into buildings.
In 2006, the German group from Frankfurt specialized Immobilien-Chancen bought the buildings occupied by Fujitsu at the same time as the real estate fund Morgan Stanley. A year later, these buildings were sold from the Spanish company Falcon Real Estate Investments SA based in Madrid.
The Fujitsu Group is headquartered in Tokyo as the world’s third-largest provider of IT products.
For example, it makes computers and servers available to businesses and also provides essential service.
Its branch established in the Rhin-Main region is one of the largest in Germany: Fujitsu certainly has many more employees in Munich.
The branch, Fujitsu Technology Solutions, leaves Bad Homburg to move to Frankfurt-Niederrad. The other subsidiaries on this site are Fujitsu Services and a company named TDS.
For this, 7000 square meters have rented in the Astro Park building.
This twenty-year-old complex, whose name recalls its star-shaped plan, houses, among other things, the headquarters of the Confederation of the Electronic and Electrotechnical Industry.
Expulsion of the server by Amazon - Threats against Mr. Assange
Wikileaks is experiencing more and more difficulties.
The Internet service provider Amazon has deported from its servers the site specialized in revealing confidential documents.
Perhaps this attitude hides political pressure after the publications of the hundreds of thousands of secret diplomatic notes.
Wikileaks founder Mr. Assange apparently travels to England.
In the United States, the Wikileaks site is being attacked more and more forcefully.
The Wikileaks group of cyber-activists no longer has access to its server hosted so far in America and is now looking for a host in Europe.
Meanwhile, prominent U.S. Senator Joe Lieberman called on other countries and companies to boycott Wikileaks.
And Wikileaks founder Julian Assange, who is wanted by Interpol, is probably staying in Britain, according to information published by newspapers.
"Wikileaks excluded from the Amazon server."
"Freedom of expression in the land of freedom," said Wednesday (local time) the cyber-activists at the origin of the revelations via the social network Twitter.
Shortly before, the cyber-company, Amazon, had removed Wikileaks documents from its servers.
Following the release of the secret diplomatic notes, cyber activists used the Amazon Web Service (AWS) in order to reach the greatest number of access to documents.
Through Twitter, Wikileaks continues its revelations: “Great, our dollars are now being spent to take care of people in Europe.”
When freedom of expression poses a problem for Amazon, “they should stop selling books.”
CNN, quoting Liebermann’s office, reported that Amazon banned Wikileaks from using its servers after an investigation by Senator Joe Lieberman’s collaborators.
The chairman of the Senate Committee for the Protection of the Territory had threatened the Amazon site to orchestrate a boycott against it, reported the British newspaper, "The Guardian".
“I would have liked Amazon to take this step earlier in light of Wikileaks’ previous publications classified information,” he said.
At the same time, Liebermann called on other countries to deny the platform’s use of servers.
"Wikileaks acts in an illegal, monstrous and unscrupulous way. In doing so, it puts our national security at risk all over life all over the world," according to an independent senator's statement.
"No company aware of its responsibilities, whether U.S. or foreign, should help Wikileaks spread the content of the stolen documents."
Wikileaks founder Mr. Assange, who is wanted by the Swedish authorities because he is suspected of rape, is on Interpol’s wanted list.
According to information released by the London newspaper "The Independent", the 39-year-old Australian lives in Britain and Scotland Yard knows exactly where he is.
The cyber-activist has consistently called plots orchestrated by its critics the prosecution of him by Sweden.
Wikileaks spokesman Kristinn Hrafnsson said in London that threats have been made against the site.
“Calls have even been made for the murder of Julian Assange.”
That’s why Assange particularly fears for his safety, Hrafnsson said.
That's also why the Wikileaks founder continues to hide.
Hrafnsson clearly reacted to statements made in North America.
In America, former Arkansas Republican Governor Mike Huckabee demanded that those responsible for Wikileaks’ revelations be prosecuted and executed for treason.
In Canada, Prime Minister Stephen Harper’s adviser on television, obviously ironically, that Assange should “be killed” and that Obama could “use a Drone.”
Meanwhile, there was controversy between the White House and Mr. Assange.
Robert Gibbs, spokesman for U.S. President Barack Obama, described as "ridiculous and absurd" the fact that Mr. Assange demanded the resignation of Secretary of State Hilary Clinton if she was responsible for the order given to diplomats, and leaked recently, to engage in espionage activities.
"I don't know exactly why we have to deal with the opinion of an individual who owns a website," Gibbs said.
“Our foreign policy and the interests of this country are far more important than the affirmations of a website.”
The U.S. government is now completely moving to the test bench to protect its databases.
According to a statement from the White House, Mr. Russell Travers, deputy director of the Anti-Terrorist Center, has been appointed responsible to combat leaks.
Travers is tasked with putting in place "the necessary structural reforms" made indispensable after Wikileaks disclosures of diplomatic cables.
The White House is also committed to verifying the means by which the entire administration exchanges and protects information.
Moreover, the U.S. government has announced that it wants to take care of the safety of dissidents and activists if they are ever threatened by Wikileaks revelations.
“We’ve made every effort to get in touch with them,” State Department spokesman Philip Crowley said.
"We are ready to protect them if necessary."
Ice University
At "Gelato University" in Bologna, ice confectioners are prepared for the best seasons of the year.
The three-day introductory course organized for future glaciers had a very great success and attracted visitors from all over the world.
By the way, where does all the ice come from?
Glacier and ice machine.
The glacier, i.e
The ice turbine removes ice cream from the refrigerated display case using a spatula, arranges it in the waffle and then starts selling it.
To find out, you don't need to go to Anzola dell'Emilia in the province of Bologna.
But anyone who wants to know more about the ice, can not avoid "Gelato-University" of the Italian firm Carpigiani.
The sorbettery manufacturer founded the Ice University in 2003 so that ice creams out of its machines respond everywhere to high Italian ideals.
The three-day introductory course organized for future glaciers had a very great success and attracted visitors from all over the world.
Also offered are special courses on chocolate ice and extending over several days. Similarly, hands-on sessions are organized in the private icery.
In the midst of the midst of the quarantine crisis, a number of people dream of a new career, others simply venture to realize their ardent desire on smooth ice.
This time is 40 participants from 19 countries from all continents, from Brazil to Saudi Arabia via Hungary.
Jacky, a professor of arts education from California, is familiar with the Slow Food movement. He wants to offer in the future a quality European product in the land of Industrial Ice.
Anke de Berlin is a business management consultant and she wants, after a few years, to draw something singular from this nerve-stressing profession.
Two young Egyptians dream of opening an icery shop in Cairo.
A man from Malta and another from Brazil would like to improve the quality of ice cream in their bars, and the Canadian maid has come because her children love Italian ice cream.
The master glacier Luciano Ferrari first explains to the participants the ideal characteristics of the perfect ices.
Is decisive not only the good flavor, but also the silky suppleness and good consistency.
However, Luciano does not forget during the icing formation on the cake of the national superstructure and, in general, from the south: "Italian ice is simply the best: and then it still retains quite a dose of romanticism."
The first step in manufacturing seems quite simple.
Luciano pours the basic ingredients, such as milk, cream, milk powder, sugar and stabilizers into a large blender that heats everything to 85 degrees thanks to an electronic program, then immediately cools to four degrees.
It then opens a faucet installed on the machine: it comes out a white cream of pasteurized base, foundation of all kinds of milk ices.
The trainer is immediately assailed with the question: Is it possible to make ice using only natural substances without a stabilizer?
How can we give free rein to the personal pleasure of experimentation?
Mr. Luciano warns against letting himself be misled by the spirit of time.
One who speaks only of ices "manufactured from the natural ingredients", has perhaps nevertheless used stabilizers: most often a natural product, for example guar seed flour obtained from guar beans.
It is obviously possible to make ice without using a stabilizer, only you have to sell it within 24 hours, otherwise it collapses and the customer will crunch nothing other than ice granules.
Some changes to the base mass are permitted.
It becomes more exciting with aromas.
Master Luciano establishes the difference between the ingredients that one acquires in the fruit or confectionery department of the supermarket.
In addition, there is powder and pasta.
“Suppliers will all try to give you dozens of flavored ingredients.”
"However, you just have to try."
Either in recipes of fruit sorbets with sugar and fresh fruit.
Either in ice creams made from the white base mass which, by adding eggs becomes a yellow cream and constitutes, when added cocoa powder, the basis of all possible chocolate desserts.
Now, all participants in the course must move into the use of the ice machines.
Luciano invites them from time to time to make four kinds of ice cream: vanilla, chocolate, mocha and hazelnut with or without eggs in the base mass, with more or less vanilla aroma or other natural ingredients.
The different kinds of fruit ices are also subjected to the same experience.
More aromatic substances, more fruit purees: from the point of view of taste, this is not absolutely better.
Hazelnut ice cream does not necessarily have the taste of nuts, when it consists largely of hazelnuts.
Kiwi ice cream, very popular, is made from the group using more fruit, less water and a little less stabilizer.
“Only a balanced blend correctly enhances the taste of an ice cream,” says Luciano.
The manufacture of icing is a calculation exercise: if we use peach puree with a lot of sugar, it is necessary that the amount of sugar is reduced in the basic recipe.
Sugar supplement is required for lemon icing.
Luciano presents a device with which he determines the sugar content of the different types of fruits (the winemakers use it to measure the degree of Oechsle in grape must).
Luckily, the tables make the calculations easier.
The manufacture of ice no longer requires, as in the past, so much physical intervention.
It’s not only the basic mass automatic manufacturing machine that helps.
There is also an apparatus in which the ice mixture is simultaneously beated available to make it unctuous and brought back to negative temperatures.
In spite of all the automations, slight breakdowns are not excluded.
Brazilian Gustavo smears with fruit puree from head to toe and, thus, splashes his comrades of studies when the control of the colossal mixing bowl escapes him.
Master Luciano is of the opinion that an "incident like this is likely to occur during each course: it's only a matter of time."
Comptrollers in an ambiguous position
According to some sources, the industry experimenting with the cultivation of genetically modified plants has joined the European Food Safety Authority.
This accusation is now formulated by the Testbiotech expert group, which is considered an independent institute for the assessment of the consequences of biotechnology.
“It sounds like a conspiracy theory, but we’re well informed,” Christoph Then, Director of Testbiotech in Munich on Wednesday.
There are reports that the Chairman of the Efsa Expert Group for Genetically Modified Plants, Harry Kuiper, as well as other Efsa experts are under contract with the Ilsi lobby.
It is funded by groups such as Monsanto, Nestlé or Bayer.
Mr Kuiper worked decisively towards the development of EFSA's testing requirements for genetically modified plants and is therefore clearly in a conflict of interest situation, according to the criticisms made by Mr Then.
The elaborated requirements correspond textually, by subparagraph, to the positions taken by the Ilsi pressure group and thus bear the mark of the industry which, at bottom, would be controlled by the authorities installed in Parma, Italy.
This is already the second time Testbiotech has denounced a conflict of interest.
A high-ranking Efsa official has recently joined the Swiss agri-food group Syngenta unconditionally or without a transitional period. In doing so, she has switched sides.
Shortly ago, even Central German Radio (Mitteldeutsche Rundfunk, MDR) had revealed the existence of personal relations between the European Union's supervisory authority and the industrial lobby, raising suspicions from both the German Federal Environment Agency and MEPs.
However, Efsa does not accept the existence of any conflict of interest.
There is one opinion that all reliable data, including those of the industry, is used as long as they are of good professional quality.
Experts working on behalf of Efsa are not prohibited from making contacts with this industry.
But they should make them public.
Kuiper admits he works for the U.S.-based International Life Sciences Institute.
This organization sees itself as a neutral forum bringing together experts who belong, among others, to the field of science, industry and policy in order to assess the risks of biotechnology without being the balance of a lobby.
In this case, independent experts such as Mr Then disapprove of the fact that genetically modified plants are accepted without any animal feeding tests and that the impact of these plants on the environment has not been tested.
"We would like to know, for example, the implications of genetically modified maize cause in the field," says Mr. Then.
The current practice is to test only isolated proteins and not the entire genetically modified plant, which does not provide reliable results.
"If we don't look for the risks, then how can we discover them?" wonders Mr. Then in an accusing tone.
That's also Mrs Angelika Hilbeck's point of view.
This scientist has been researching in the field of genetics since 1994 and is currently working at the Zurich Polytechnic.
In this region as in the United States, and now also in Europe, the industrial sector is increasingly trying to influence or discredit critical scientists.
That is why there are barely yet researchers who are unrelated to societies.
In the opinion of the companies, the critical studies would not be published.
Hilbeck says "industry controls security research."
The trace of stone
Uli Gsell obtained it, in total about 400 kilos.
On Monday afternoon, she was in front of Stuttgart station with her tube on the back.
There was a demonstration.
It was snowing.
By September, excavators had come and began demolishing much of the north wing.
The world of Stuttgart was no longer the same: Several thousand people demonstrated against the S21 project, the transformation of Stuttgart Central Station into an underground station that will cost several billions.
Much of the wing was in ruins when demonstrations led to the shutdown of construction work.
Broken in 6000, 7000 pieces.
The Muschelkalk (the shell limestone) of Crailsheim, a tender and coarse pavement badly built between 1914 and 1920.
Mr. Gsell is a 43-year-old sculptor living in Ostfildern.
He bought a few fragments.
"These stones are relics."
"They must not disappear."
He enthusiastically declares: "a modest and cheap rock, simple and terrestrial."
He asserts that by cutting it with the saw, he smelled the smell of cigarette smoke.
He doesn't yet know what he'll do with this fragment.
Rocks have time.
Uli Gsell, too.
The traces of the stones of Stuttgart lead to a former ammunition depot of the French army in a forest near Tubingue, where Manuel Rongen built his stone empire.
The 44-year-old man trades in old stones.
He bought all the stones from the demolition in Stuttgart.
“It would be a shame if they were crushed or if they disappeared into the discharge,” he said in his office in a hut, which was once a guard post.
He recounts that he was the subject of insults and threats when in September the newspapers reported that he had bought the rest of the north wing from the demolition company.
But he traded for entire days with the opponents of Project S21.
And he explained to them that he couldn't do anything at all to oppose demolition.
And if it takes place, then the most reasonable thing would be to keep using the stones.
Approximately 200 opponents of the S21 project subsequently won one of the parallelepiped of stone weighing on average 80 kilograms whose value is between 6.50 and 14 Euros.
Some keep it as a souvenir while also hoping to be able to give it back during a reconstruction.
Rongen had acquired 500 tonnes, 25 full loads of a truck.
Approximately half are still crammed into the forest, the rest of which has long been sold.
Garden Owners, Landscape Gardeners, Individuals.
They came from all over and carried away everything that could get into the trunk and trailers.
These stones of scandal in Stuttgart have long transformed into "Mäuerle" or limitations of herb beds.
“By the way, this stone is nothing special,” Rongen said.
"hasn't been that political component."
He enters his all-terrain car, goes around his vast empire of 20 hectares.
He went to law studies that he abandoned. Later, he undertook to study biology before subsequently taking an interest in computer science and eventually becoming a quarrier.
And a lover of stones.
It stores 40000 tonnes.
He amasses his treasures all over Germany.
He claims that he is the greatest merchant of ancient stones.
He's restraining himself.
Sandy stones carved with art are placed on snow.
"Formerly, they were part of Marienbrücke Bridge in Dresden."
Elbsandstein, yellow, warm, he says with enthusiasm.
"My favorite stone."
He'll take the path that goes through the forest.
He shows a heap of his finger: Red sand stone, previously, part of Karlsruhe station.
And so on.
"Pretty travertine."
The parallelepiped was once part of the gallery of the Neckarstadion in Stuttgart.
Everywhere in the snow of stones and histories, Cannstatt's travertine from the police prefecture, the reddish granite from St. Blasian Cathedral, the tuf from Heilbronn Hospital.
What a job, what an effort, what a sweat!
When Mr. Rongen thinks that once the stones were carved by hand and moved using simple hoists and carrettes pulled by oxen, he becomes quite respectful.
"It would be a scandal if such a heritage were torn apart."
He didn’t get rich, even less wealthy.
But this quarrier now employs 14 people. In addition, stone tailors, a sculptor, a blacksmith, a glass blower and a gardener settled in the forest.
“We’re working hard, but it’s rewarding,” the waste recycler said.
Soon, he has to go to Cologne.
There too, there are stones to save.
Struggle of talents to conquer high potentials
The counter has a strong influency.
16 students jostle to buy salmon rolls, tomato sauce and eating whites.
The Technical University of Braunschweig and the car manufacturer Volkswagen invited students in design and mechanical engineering to a workshop.
As part of a kind of idea competition, they have to talk about the car of the future with the designers of VW, and, by the way, know the firm Volkswagen.
Nowadays, many companies do not hesitate to go hand-to-hand in the competition to find better brains and to engage, like Volkswagen, in a multifaceted cooperation with universities.
Their mission: To recruit the most gifted graduates as much as possible in amphitheaters before they arrive on the job market and they are courted by others.
And since universities do not publish statements about the performance of their students, we try to identify exceptional talents in practical projects and retain them.
On the second day of the workshop organized at the Technical University of Brauschweig the automotive group amadows talents.
A young lady from the staff department shares the pens, candy and cards featuring the Volkswagen logo.
She talks about the calendar of professional internships and student jobs to young talents; she shows them the paths to access the group.
Nico Selle of the Technical University of Braunschweig is responsible for the project initiated with Volkswagen.
For him, there are reciprocal advantages for both parties: "Students come into contact with this industrial sector and gain experience."
“As for the Volkswagen Group, he can get to know the students, take a closer look at the serious candidates, and appreciate those who come into play as an employee.”
According to Mr. Saelle, such cooperation is unusual.
What's new is the offensive we're trying to attract students with.
"Formerly, the focus was rather on the professional project," says Mr. Selle.
The struggle for the conquest of the new graduates will become much tougher.
In goods of the sectors, companies can no longer recruit their employees by sorting a kyrielle of candidates.
But they have to praise their reliability and above all woo the highly skilled workforce.
It is above all Graduates from the engineering fields who find themselves in an excellent position to negotiate.
According to the Association of German Engineers VDI, more than 40 000 engineering positions remained unoccupied in October.
And according to VDI's forecasts, the vacuum relating to engineering stations will further expand.
Roman Dykta hunts talent on behalf of KPMG, an auditory, accounting and advisory firm.
It organizes expensive events aimed at recruiting talent, such as the KPMG-Race cruise race, a kind of rally-paper where competitors have to find their way using GPS coordinates.
The company will send eight promising students next year to travel the United States in this way.
Attendees can conveniently visit KPMG's subsidiaries and get to know the company.
The person who is to participate decides in advance after two selection procedures.
Only the cream cream of the many candidates will fly to the United States.
According to Dykta, the means used for the question of high potentials in universities are increasingly important.
"We want to stand out thanks to the cruising race."
"For us, a simple cruise was too little"
For Mr. Dykta's talent hunter, the account seems to be there.
“Until today, we have kept in touch with all the people who took part last year in a similar action.”
"Some of them are about to complete their studies and wish to address their requests to our company soon," he says.
Apart from the direct confession carried out on campus, small and medium-sized businesses proceed in a completely classical way by participating in the professional fairs where they engage in brain hunting.
"We're trying to reach graduates by participating in trade shows."
“For example, at the solar technology show,” says Sitha Stübe, director of human resources at Solarworld, a manufacturer of photovoltaic panels.
This company located in Bonn employs about 1500 people in Germany.
Ms. Stübe is aware of the problems related to succession.
Currently, 35 positions are put into competition on the company's website.
"Right now it's very difficult to fill vacancies."
"It's expensive and takes a lot of time," he said.
Another way to establish contact is through university professors.
Solarworld has established a cooperation with various institutions of higher education, for example, the University of Fribourg.
"Professions indicate that the student has the opportunity to carry out professional internships or to write their graduation project at home."
Expensive advertising campaigns are advertised among solar specialists.
That's why the company is trying to advertise by highlighting other qualities: personalized vibe, flat hierarchy and easy communication.
“Often, a large group can’t do that,” says Stübe, head of staff.
Money no longer plays the main role in the job search.
Flexible working hours, childcare or good promotional opportunities are the other determinants.
According to a study conducted by the Great Place to Work Institute, Solarworld ranks 55th among Germany’s Top 100 Employers.
The institute is an annual honor roll for German employers.
To this end, he asks employees about their level of satisfaction in relation to their work and its context while looking at the managerial conditions of employees.
The head of staff Ms Stübe hopes that this placement will enhance the company's image among job seekers.
"I think the candidates rely mainly on external evaluations when it comes to a potential employer," she said.
Wolf Reiner Kriegler of the German DEBA (Deutscher Employer Branding Akademie, DEBA) advises companies that wish to build a good reputation as an employer by applying a long-term strategy.
He advises his clients to show more authenticity.
And, according to Kriegler, this means, above all, taking internal measures.
“The best way for an employer to make itself attractive is to go through its own employees,” he says.
This is called, according to the jargon used in the sector, the internal image strategy, i.e. the creation of the employer's brand image within its own workforce.
"satisfied collaborators are the best messengers."
When dealing well with our employees and identifying themselves with the company, they alone advertise their employer to their friends and acquaintances.
"It's like playing in an orchestra," Kriegler said.
Obama puts brake on new oil drilling
There will be no new oil drilling in the Gulf of Mexico off the coast of Florida, the southeastern state of the United States, in the coming years.
The government of Washington has issued a ban An equivalent ban and, thus, definitively cancelled its initial plans.
Even off the Atlantic coast, new boreholes are currently banned.
Interior Secretary Ken Salazar made the announcement on Wednesday.
It is the explosion, in April, of the drilling platform "Deepwater Horizon" which is at the origin of this strict ban, which must remain in force at least for the next five years.
Faced with this accident, which claimed the lives of eleven employees and leads to the greatest environmental disaster of the United States, President Barack Obama, under pressure from the opposition and this sector of activity, had indicated that he was ready to authorize the realization of new drilling in the eastern part of the Gulf of Mexico.
Proponents of this solution want to reduce U.S. dependence on foreign countries when it comes to oil supply.
The question of who bears responsibility for the devastating oil spill in golf is not, to this day, definitively resolved.
Britain’s oil group, which is facing multi-billion-dollar claims, is at the centre of the charges.
A commission of inquiry created by Obama is due to present its findings in December.
In October, Interior Secretary Ken Salazar lifted the freeze on the borehole decreed after the explosion of the platform.
As a result of this decision, various petroleum groups had immediately submitted applications relating to new drilling projects.
With tighter controls and tougher security guidelines, Obama and Salazar want to prevent another disaster.
The competent supervisory authority, which has discredited itself after numerous charges of corruption, is currently undergoing in-depth reforms.
At the same time, Mr. Obama announced that it would remove the lucrative tax niches of gas and oil groups.
Actors in this sector of activity are now publicly warning that the flow of food in the Gulf would be significantly lower than the case so far.
"The government's goal is to curb the rapid growth of the gas and oil sector" according to the result of a current survey conducted in the sector by audit firm BDO, ahead of Wednesday's drilling ban.
Moreover, the American Enery Alliance deplores the uncertainty that characterizes this sector: "Nobody knows what will change exactly with the regulations."
Investment in new support projects has been deferred until further notice, to the detriment of growth.
Ogoni country is dry
The water flowing into the rivers is oiled brown; it exudes an odor similar to that which exists in the gas station: Due to a leak, billions of litres of crude oil destroyed the Ogoni country in Nigeria.
Men live on theft, corruption and refining of oil.
Celestine Akpobari has a good intention.
"Don't exhaust yourself," the environmental activist told reporters to calm them down: "You's still enough oil mud today in front of the water lens."
The 37-year-old Nigerian has tried to photograph a young woman sunk into muddy water to the chest, as well as the greasy trail that surrounds him: This scene seems almost beautiful in the sparkle of water to the color of the rainbow.
We can't see that the subsoil is full of brown layers of crude oil.
On the bank of the Bodo-Jetty River, Esther Badom recounts that she once collected shrimp by whole buckets here.
But meanwhile the calm waters have completely dried up.
Five years ago, a huge amount of oil sprang from a pipeline that exploded in the huge swamp.
The fishermen living in the nearby small town of Bodo now have to row for six hours to reach fishy waters: The Ogoni country located in the heart of the Niger Delta is considered one of the most polluted regions in the world.
All the extent of the ravages is obvious when the mangrove swamps crossed by streams.
Instead of clear water, the motorboat breaks down crading brown water. Anyone who plunges his hand removes it all covered with oil.
The roots of young of the mangroves emerge from the soil like licorice sticks. Isolated palm trees sadly lean their palms.
We look in vain for the clouds of birds, as we usually meet in wetlands: As soon as the out-of-head engine stops, a ghostly silence descends on this paradise of old that resembles a gas station by the smell it emits.
"What's happening here is a war on the environment," says Celestine Akpobari with a sad air: "Our life is characterized by a permanent danger when it comes to health."
The comparison of the wars has been made.
The area where the Ogoni live is a besieged battlefield for decades: More than 60 years ago, the first European explorers penetrated here; the first resistance was organized in the 1980s by writer Ken Saro-Wiwa. it was executed later. In 1993, the Shell group had to interrupt its production activities in this region because they had become very dangerous for its employees.
Even today, the return to the Ogoni country is forbidden to this company: While more than 2 million barrels of black gold are daily pumped thanks to more than 5000 boreholes and 7000 kilometers of pipelines throughout the Delta region, three times large like Switzerland, the oil fields of the Ogoni country are untapped, because the situation is too unstable for this oil group.
Every a few hundred meters rise in the water of steel trestles as strange sculptures: We very affectionately designate "Christmas trees" the heads of boreholes.
They are up to 50 years old, most of them are consumed by corrosion up to the marker.
Akpobari believes that a drilling head of this nature breaks from time to time in pieces, because a constant flow of crude oil flows into the water.
According to the experts' estimates, more than two billion liters have poured into the ecosystem in 50 years: Each year, the amount of oil equivalent to that which flowed into the sea during the accident of the supertanker "Exxon Valdez" 21 years ago off Alaska, one of the biggest environmental disasters in the history of humanity.
Who must bear the responsibility for persistent environmental pollution? the answer to this question divides the actors.
For the Shell group, the sabotage and siphoning of pipelines are responsible for more than 90 percent of pollution. Environmentalists, for their part, consider the explosions of drilling heads and perforated pipelines as the main causes.
In 2008, U.S. researcher Richard Steiner accused the Shell Group of applying essentially less stringent safety standards in Nigeria than anywhere else in the world: Only the rate of pipeline failures is "still much higher than ale".
Some forms emerge from the mangroves in a place that, seen from a distance, resembles an abandoned workshop, full of cambouis and installed in the open air: They are completely bare and covered with cambouis up to the neck.
A fire sparkles under one of the many barrels that sometimes connected to the pipes, sometimes scattered without pattern on the site must be detected: a small clandestine refinery.
We're heating the crude oil in the barrel for several hours, says Michael, the team leader.
Initially, gasoline enters into a container passing through pipes, then, at the end of the piping of kerosene drops fall and diesel is finally obtained.
That's how Michael and his four collaborators daily produce four 300-litre diesel drums: "It brings us 34 000 Naira", about 22 dollars.
Residents of the Delta are taking advantage of the chronic fuel shortage in the largest oil-producing country in Black Africa.
Nigeria has only one refinery.
Illegal refiners can sell at a cheaper price than the ridiculously low price of just 40 cents per liter of Diesel: Because they acquire their raw material free of charge.
it is offered by the "protectors" who put the network of piercing pipelines all over across the Ogoni country. According to experts, the country loses at least 16 million liters of crude oil in the entire Niger Delta.
Only a very insignifing small part of the provisionally stored raw material is illegally purified: The organized crime networks that also include high-ranking politicians and military members, charge the lion’s share in tankers and shake it in the world market.
Activist Akpobari considers that the debate on the question of whether to attribute the environmental disaster or rather subversive activities to the population or the laxist mode of production is purely academic.
"If oil companies weren't perceived as intruders and if the foundations of fishermen's lives weren't so deeply destroyed, there would be no sabotage or illegal theft and refineries."
But meanwhile, Ogoni’s relations especially towards Shell have deteriorated so much that there was only talk of withdrawing the group. “Maybe operators with other nationalities, such as the Chinese, will prove better.”
Then Michael gets nervous.
"It's high time you'd run away" announces the sturdy oil worker.
"Soldiers can appear at any time."
If illegal refiners are surprised by the special units of the armed forces, they will certainly not be the subject of an arrest, but at the most they will offer them bribes.
Even soldiers mainly from the north of the country intend to take advantage like all Nigerians of the raw material.
"From every point of view, this bloody thing brought down our very low country," Akpori regrets.
Investigators discover building defects in A380 reactors
The A380 clearly suffers from a major security problem.
By examining an aircraft that carried out an emergency landing, investigators discovered a possible construction defect that can lead to "catastropic reactor failure."
Qantas airline now wants to file a lawsuit against the reactor manufacturer.
At the beginning of November, Airbus passengers belonging to Quantas had visibly been more fortunate so far: During its investigation, the Australian Aviation Safety Authority found that the engines of some A380 aircraft presented a serious safety problem, and it warns against a possible construction defect.
This potential failure could lead to serious consequences: it can cause fatigue cracks and leaks in the connection of the oil pipe, according to a statement published Thursday by the administration of the ATSB.
It could have led to a "catastrophic failure of the reactors".
Concerned are Rolls-Royce manufactured reactors and equipped with 20 A380 series aircraft.
The Airbus of the company Quantas, which had to land urgently on November 4 in Singapore, is among these.
The Australian airline has now brought legal proceedings against the English reactor manufacturer.
She has lodged a legal complaint with an Australian federal court headquartered in Sydney, giving her the opportunity to take further legal action against the company.
Quantas Director Alan Joyce said his company would claim damages in connection with the incident in early November.
Parts detached and the bearing surface was damaged during engine fire.
According to Rolls-Royce, it's oil ignition that causes the damage.
After this incident, at least 40 reactors must now be replaced worldwide.
Australian investigators have announced that further safety-related tests will be carried out on superjumbos which are also equipped with the reactors in question.
The safety recommendations are the result of a collaboration with Rolls-Royce, ASTB spokesman Martin Dolan said.
The security issue is considered sufficiently important to inform all parties involved.
Following the emergency landing of its aircraft, Quantas had grounded its entire A380 fleet for a week.
Singaing Airlines has temporarily stopped three of its eleven A380s.
As a precautionary measure, Lufthansa has replaced an A380 reactor.
The two-storey aircraft is the world’s largest airliner.
Snowfall throughout the territory causes chaos in transportation
Germany all covered in white: In one night, the snow fell on almost the entire country.
Chaos reigns on all roads, trains and planes are immobilized.
At least three people died in traffic accidents.
Winter is shivering Germany: By freezing temperatures, almost the entire republic is covered with snow in the night of Thursday, which leads to chaos in many localities.
To the north and east, snow cover reaches 15 centimeters in places.
Traffic is severely restricted on several motorways and on many roads.
On National A7 in Flensburg, winter service staff worked permanently, but they could not overcome the ever-increasing snow.
Moreover, violent gusts have swept the coast of the Baltic Sea.
The Federal Office for Maritime Navigation and Hydrography (DSH) expects tidal waves taking place on the Schleswig-Holstein Baltic coast with water levels of up to 1.2 m above normal.
Snore piles on highways and federal roads have led to serious problems in Saxony.
This is why the Meteomedia bad weather warning center has announced the level of violet alert has been announced in Saxon Switzerland: in fact, this is the highest level.
In places, 30 centimeters of fresh snow have dropped that strong and fresh winds have dispersed.
Several trucks and cars got stuck there at night.
Even in Saxony-Anhalt, police authorities have announced serious disruptions on the roads.
On the A2 motorway, snow and wind resulted in an interruption of traffic and caused traffic jams.
In Thuringia, snowfall affected the A4 and A9 motorways.
According to the Deutscher Bahn, serious disruptions of rail traffic took place in Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia and northern Bavaria.
The main problems are: snow on railways, fallen trees on railway tracks, catenaries or destroyed needles.
In Saxony we had to interrupt the Leipzig-Nuremberg interregional transport.
In Sallfeld, Thuringia, an ICE train to Munich had to interrupt its journey.
Firefighters and paramedics had to supply the 165 passengers of the train with blankets, food and drinks.
Snow winds have swept down the roads.
Snowfalls have resulted in numerous flight cancellations at Germany’s main airports.
According to a spokesperson for the standard and flight information, some cancellations have already been announced in Munich.
A spokesman for Fraport says 40 flights have already been cancelled in Frankfurt on the Main.
The winter service provides a permanent service.
It is true that passengers stranded at the airport are no longer numerous, despite this barely 400 beds are at their disposal.
Accidents took place Wednesday afternoon on many roads.
In the evening in Ostholstein, a truck driver lost his life in a collision with a train at a crossing.
The train driver was slightly injured.
Due to the layers of snow on the roads, a similar accident occurred not far from Nuremberg: even here, a truck was caught at a crossing because the vehicle was stopped there.
The driver lost his life.
Four passengers were injured on the train, which is why the road from Bayreuth to Nuremberg was closed until the morning hours.
In addition, a 30-year-old woman died when her car barreled on a slippery pavement in Aschaffenburg.
However, meteorologists announce good news: The snow will certainly always be soft and powdery, but it will swirl in case of slight gusts.
However, the wind will weaken.
Only the Baltic Sea will be afflicted by strong and rapid winds.
However, it is very cold, up to less than 15 degrees in places.
Deutsche Bank is looking for a purchaser for its twin towers.
Deutsche Bank seeks to separate itself from its emblem: According to information reported by newspapers, this financial institution is looking for buyers for its two office buildings located at its headquarters in Frankfurt.
This building could yield up to 500 million euros.
Deutsche Bank has just embellished the group's headquarters: The twin towers located in the Business District have been modernized; in the meantime, the bankers had to return to their former place of service.
From now on, the financial group is obviously looking to recover the costs of renovations and it is looking for a buyer for its two buildings, as the newspaper "Financial Times Deutschland", quoting a spokesman.
This spokesperson did not indicate the reasons for the planned sale.
He said "we're checking if it's more interesting to occupy the buildings ourselves or sell them, and put them back for rent later."
This is how Germany's largest financial institution has already ceded its headquarters shortly thereafter.
It was only in the middle of 2007 that the bank had acquired the twin towers for an amount of 272 million Euros from the holders of own funds. It had then rehabilitated them.
Transformations aimed at energy optimisation have swallowed, according to some indications, about 200 million euros.
A sale would obviously make a lot of money to the bank.
According to information provided by the newspaper, experts in this sector of activity estimate the value of the towers, renamed in the meantime "Green Towers", at about 500 million euros.
According to the spokesman, it is not yet known whether the buildings will again be sold to a fund owned by the bank or to an independent investor.
Thousands of people are mourning the minors who perished.
In New Zealand, more than 10,000 people had a thought for buried miners who were declared dead as a result of several explosions in a mine.
We had not yet been able to raise the bodies because the safety situation of the damaged well continues to be precarious.
Just two weeks ago, a violent explosion occurred in Pike River Mine near the town of Greymouth.
29 minors between the ages of 17 and 62 had been buried underground.
A probe had been inserted into the damaged well, but no traces of survivors had been found.
Due to the high concentration of toxic gases and other explosions that followed, no rescue teams were able to descend into the mine for safety reasons.
After the second violent detonation, the authorities and the administration considered that the minors had no chance to survive.
So far, the bodies could not be raised.
Today, relatives and friends organized a party in memory of the dead.
Thousands of people had gathered under a bright sun in Greymouth south of New Zealand Island.
They observed two minutes of silence.
In memory of the 29 buried minors, we installed 29 tables on which we could see the names, helmets and lamps.
The photos and personal effects, including clothing and surf boards, also had their place there.
The flags were flown at half-mast in front of all public buildings in the country.
New Zealand Prime Minister John Key told commemorative holiday participants that New Zealand has fueled the hope that miners will come to the surface.
"But they've never come home."
Mr. Lawrie Drew, whose son Zen was buried in the pits, said he cannot resign himself to accept the death of his son until he sees his remains.
In addition to the Prime Minister and the relatives of the victims, several foreign diplomats took part in the commemorative festival.
Miners of South African and British descent have also buried during the first explosion on 19 November.
Rescuers began extinguishing a fire inside New Zealand's South Island coal mine.
Prefect Gary Knowles explained to reporters that when the masses of explosive gases are moved, the burning temperatures inside the mine begin to drop.
Go to hell, warrior!
Is Splatter an artificial film?
Director Nicolas Winding Refn explores the limits of the ancient genre thanks to his film, "Walhalla Rising", the fighter's epic.
What audience could Danish director Nicolas Winding Refn think of when he turned "Walhalla Rising"?
A Viking warrior (Mads Mikkelsen) possessing only one eye is taken prisoner; he frees himself from his executioner and, with an almost supernatural force, eliminates his opponents one after the other. This seems ideal for young male spectators in search of a bloody action, precisely because our hero uses methods of brutal assassination.
On the other hand, until the end of the film, the blind does not say any word about its real name. Even the other characters let out, at most, at the bend of one or two sentences a few screams of appalling agony.
Otherwise, we don't say anything and festoy in the incredibly beautiful mountainous landscape of Scotland which is the setting of the Viking universe, about a thousand years ago.
It would be ideal plus matural filmmakers with an interest for existentialism and excellent traveling.
When a film targets several target groups, it is a pity that they are mutually exclusive.
Director Winding Refn is considered, since "Pusher", his trilogy relating to drug trafficker fierce and full of style, as a kind of Danish Quentin Tarantinon, but he refuses to bring a little more mood to the mainstream or introduce a little less brutality to the cinema of art and essay.
Most art cinema sympathizers have to experience horror in the face of the brain that spreads all around and the intestines.
Lovers of action films die of boredom, when the blind embarks, for reasons that can be freely interpreted, with a group of violent Christian missionaries in a ship bound for Jerusalem; they are drowned for an eternity by calm and mist before the crew arrives in America and continues to remain silent.
It’s no wonder that “Walhalla Rising” has not found any film distributors in Germany and it only exists in DVD format.
It's mainly because of the magnificent images of misery that deserved a certain attention, but also because the image of a thrilling film, from time to time, hides behind a pretentious and bloody appearance.
It's not about dialogues in this film, nor about a coherent action, everything is an atmosphere, a feverish dream transformed into a film.
The viewer can't escape it.
Refn barely gives him a moment of relaxation and slowly cuts his throat with icy images and a gloomy soundtrack, but above all with an elusive hatred that springs from the hearts of the characters.
Only a blond young boy, who stands faithfully with the blind, preserves a certain innocence, all the others are animated by anger.
More than anything, the blind hero whose mobiles are never known, but who returns from hell at any time to fight enemies and temporarily allies by turning into a warrior, may be an envoy of the devil himself.
It represents decay, but perhaps too, just for those who are, anyway, rotten.
"Walhalla Rising" is an enigmatic film that can be hated or admired, the purpose of which is annoyance, enthusiasm and astonishment.
Trausing experience.
Mysterious earthworks
The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) has new indications regarding a nuclear program developed by Syria.
But this country refuses to take a position on this subject, much less to submit to the controls of the agency.
On the night of September 5-6, 2007, around midnight, at least four Israeli F-15 I fighter jets attacked a mysterious cubic-shaped building on Syrian territory.
It is located on the bank of the euphrates; the largest nearby city is 30 kilometers away: it is Dair as-Sur.
Its name appears in the IAEA documents, as it is a question of the reactor that Syria apparently built with the help of North Korea in parallelepiped of stone, concealed in a valley marked by the absence of electric current.
According to a U.S
On the other hand, this technique has been valid since the 1950’s.
The CIA showed a presentation video featuring blurred satellite images.
Through computer-aided animation, it indicated the probable location of each component of the reactor prior to the destruction of the plant which represents a miniature copy of the North Korean model of the Yongbyon nuclear power plant.
In addition, she presented the photos showing the building located nearby, or even the interior of it.
It appears that the reactor was about to be put into operation, but it was not yet equipped with fuel bars.
Today, more than three years after the Israeli bombardment, there is barely more information available than at the time, and even, over time the mystery thickens more and more.
Former U.S. President George W. Bush recently revealed in his memoirs that prior to this attack, the CIA was certainly confident that Syria had built a reactor in el-Kibar, but had little evidence showing this country was developing “an active nuclear weapons program.”
Some intelligence agents in Europe prefer to be more cautious about it and only speak of an "alleged reactor", even if they consider the presentation of the CIA as "plausible".
They lack hard evidence, as well as at the IAEA.
Asked how he assures that he is dealing with a nuclear reactor, a high-ranking U.S. diplomat gave only a laconic response in summer saying that the IAEA had "sufficient documents to ask questions."
The Syrians have certainly hastily removed the rubble from the "unused military building" and proceeded with a methodical flattening of the terrain.
However, in June 2008, a team of IAEA experts led by former Chief Inspector Oli Heinonen, discovered traces of uranium produced by men in el-Kibar.
In addition, the IAEA has received through a Member State satellite images of commercial service providers showing the moment that directly followed the attack; it appears that they are more eloquent than those presented by the CIA.
However, these photos aren’t available on the market because the anonymous sponsor, maybe Israel, acquired them exclusively.
As a result, it is impossible for the IAEA to establish their authenticity objectively.
Syria has always assured that it has never carried out a secret atomic program and that it has not built a reactor.
Damascus maintains that the uranium found at the site comes from the weapons used by Israel.
But the IAEA rejected this argument after the analysis of the particles by calling it unbelievable.
Since that time, damas has been refusing any discussion about it, as well as about satellite images.
In addition, Syria does not allow controllers to carry out a second inspection at the el-Kibar site and denies access to the rubble of the building destroyed by the bomb.
However, even Israel remains stubborn silence.
Confirming the official position, Ehud Olmert, then Prime Minister in office at the time of the attack, recently said: "We will never comment on it."
For example, the investigation of this case has dragged long since June 2008 without reaching an outcome and "increasingly turning into a serious problem of the Agency's credibility", as acknowledged by a senior Western diplomat in service at the IAEA headquarters in Vienna, finally it is a serious violation of the treaty prohibiting nuclear weapons.
The IAEA considers that in order to make progress, it is necessary to clarify the existence of the three other facilities that must have "a functional relationship" with the alleged reactor.
So far, the inspectors have not indicated what facilities it is here or what their function is or what they are suspected to contain.
According to information published by the newspaper "Süddeutsche Zeitung", they are known within the IAEA respectively under the names of Marj as-Sultan, Masyaf and Iskandariayah also by the names of the neighboring cities.
The Independent Institute for Science and International Security in Washington has identified these localities and discovered on satellite images a complex near Masyaf that would most likely be one of the suspicious objects.
It is possible that it is located in or directly next to a military base; this is exactly why Syria denies access to IAEA inspectors for all three sites.
In Vienna, it is claimed that the Syrians fear that the inspection group will in fact be spies who would be sent on behalf of the United States by the IAEA in order to spy on military installations.
In fact, it's intelligence provided by U.S. secret agents that attracts the attention of nuclear inspectors.
The IAEA has information, including satellite photos, indicating that vehicles have made shuttles between the three sites and the stone parallelepiped in order to carry substances.
A high-ranking Western diplomat said in Vienna that at least one of the facilities could “serve fuel production.”
It would have "no indication of sensitive activities", such as the enrichment and reprocessing of uranium; processes that are essential for the production of atomic weapons.
This type of reactor certainly works thanks to unenriched uranium.
Nevertheless, there are plausible hypotheses concerning the following fundamental questions: from which uranium should have come from and where the plutonium necessary for the manufacture of the bomb should have been separated later.
It is unclear whether the rubble, which could hide traces of great value, have been stored in all three sites.
When the hundreds of trucks loaded with land have disappeared, no one will have information about the place of storage of rubble.
According to the IAEA, it is unlikely that Syria had al-Tibni's salt mine, located only a few kilometers from el-Kibar, across the Euphrates, examined in 1998 to see if it may be suitable for the storage of radioactive waste.
By recently analyzing satellite photos on behalf of renowned Jane's Intelligence Review magazine, former IAEA inspector Robert Kelley has established a link between them and the reactor in question.
Satellite photos show, however, that Syria has undertaken earthworks at the three suspicious sites and moved huge containers when the IAEA begins to demand access to these locations.
It may be a coincidence, but it did not help to dismiss suspicions.
In its view in diplomatic circles that the IAEA has spent several months preparing a plan for the inspections to be carried out. This plan should allow Syria to preserve possible military secrets. Only the time invested shows what great interest the inspectors bring to this matter.
Meanwhile, Damascus has also ignored this proposal.
A year ago, if Western diplomats in Vienna had not reported the destruction of the reactor, the emergency would not be reported for this case, says, for example, Glyn Davies, the U.S. ambassador who has evoked in recent months what is known as special inspections, one of the most powerful means that the Atomic Agency has to implement its controls.
IAEA Director Yukiya Amano, who had to offer these inspections, already spoke of "an option to which I am open in the future", but he is still hesitating.
On the one hand, it requires a majority in the IAEA Board of Governors and many of the 35 countries represented there have no desires to see an escalation.
on the other hand, like this diplomat who gives to reflect: "Would for this, if in the end they find nothing"
Love in time of war
It's been wrong since Angelina Jolie made her debut in the summer as a director.
She wrote the script herself: it’s a love story between a woman from Bosnia and a man of Serbian origin.
We don’t know anything more about this story, but that’s enough to cause unease.
A persistent rumor indicates that the woman, a Muslim woman, fell in love with her Serbian executioner during the Yugoslav war.
The federations of Bosnian women immediately protested. In response, some planned shooting sequences in Sarajevo were moved at the last minute to Hungary.
In the meantime, the film is shot in Hungary, but in the post-production phase, protests continue in Bosnia.
According to information published in a Bosnian newspaper, Bakira Hasecic of the organization of women victims of war sent correspondence to Angelina Jolie through the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) and she demanded to meet the actress.
She is not a diplomat. A few weeks ago, she proposed to these women to hold a meeting with them in Hungary, but they declined this offer.
"The meeting must take place in Bosnia," Hasecic said.
There have been no love stories in concentration camps, and Jolie’s script says a lot about her attitude towards the victims.
The Guardian reports that Hasecic's organization subsequently recommended to the United Nations High Commission to remove the status of Goodwill Ambassador to Angelina Jolie.
But, by the way, what kind of Jolie scenario did she write?
The Bosnian Ministry of Culture, which received copies of its screenplay, granted some hesitation a filming permit in Sarajevo, but it is not known why did not subsequently come despite this authorization.
The young author assures herself that she does not pursue political objectives with her film, that it is by making a lot of effort that she has become aware of the Yugoslav conflict. She maintains that she wants to highlight the terrible consequences of the war and give the opportunity to all stakeholders to express themselves.
However, it remains vague about the content.
This is how a second synopsis of the film circulates on the Net. It is quite different: The lovers knew each other well before the beginning of the war. They meet again when the woman, a Bosnian Muslim, is deported to the Serb concentration camp where the man works as a guardian.
This is still a big difference with the version "a victim of rape falls in love with her executioner".
That Jolie's film began long before the war, of course.
The pressure exerted on her, thus expresses Jolie, is "unfair and based on erroneous information".
Now, only Hollywood fanatics can give him peace: By illegally putting this scenario in the Net, like many others before.
Let's shoot us!
Tonight, everything's fine with the Irish again.
There is no trace of consternation.
This is why the local team excessively dominates visitors from Sweden, Finland and the United States.
Fans scream with joy against the backdrop of loud music, stumbling their feet.
Baileyu-Pub de Cork in southern irelande vibrates because the players throw and manage to drop table tennis balls at a distance of three meters in beer cups of the opposing team.
This bizarre show is called "beer-pong". The winner pockets 500 Euros plus a plane ticket allowing him to attend the World Beer-Pong Championship in January in Las Vegas.
Cian Tanner is a calm individual; this subtle drinking game and the reserved computer scientist, these two behaviors really don’t go well together.
And yet, despite this, the 26-year-old seeks to have fun, to turn away from the terebrating issue, which will lead to the future.
The pleasure of playing in Las Vegas does not appear in his projects. In January, he will lead job interviews in New Zealand.
"However, I have a strange feeling," he said.
"The homeland is so far away and I don't have a job there."
Finally: Her girlfriend is already waiting for her in Auckland, "in less than two, she landed her desired job as an event project manager, maybe a good sign," says the young man with a smile.
Due to the country’s bleak prospects, two of Tanner’s three sisters tried their luck abroad.
His sister Yvonne, a social worker, resides in Sydney, his brother Colin now holds a position as an IT consultant in London.
Young people leave: The situation of most families living on the island resembles that of tanners.
Experts from the Institute for Economic and Social Research indicate that 100,000 Irish will soon leave their country.
They are even predicting 200,000 emigrants by 2015.
This represents a huge loss for a small country of 4.5 million inhabitants.
If these predictions arise, it will be a current of emigration comparable to that from which Ireland suffered in the 1980's.
After a decade of economic boom during which the Irish enjoyed great ease, grief now takes the whole place.
The economic crisis, however, especially affects the well-trained young Irish people, who have so far only experienced moments of happiness.
Loss of employment and social decay seemed absurd in years of economic booms.
Everyone had a job, there's excess money.
However, in the meantime, three out of ten people under the age of thirty are unemployed.
The Irish Times newspaper publishes extensively the letters of readers written by young emigrants, who complain: "In Ireland, everything is drifting."
"There should be an uprising against politicians and bankers, but the one who has the possibilities, goes away and our country thus a whole generation."
"The last to leave the light off."
Law student Leanne Caulfield, president of the Irish Student Union, sees a darker future because the government is only throwing powder in the eyes and offering no prospect of a future for young people.
“They’re reducing minimum wages, but they have no strategy to create new jobs,” says the 20-year-old.
She sees how her comrades cling from one unpaid practical internship to another.
She states that are her greatest concern is to emigrate herself in four years.
"It's a sad and depressing thought."
However men like Stephen McLarnon are trying to positively address this theme.
McLarnon organizes the "WorkingAbroadExpo" show which offers full support on the theme "working abroad".
"But work abroad is an extraordinary opportunity for the new in the profession to take the first steps in the echelons of the professional hierarchy," says the official.
Its website shows photos of surfers in Australia and snow surfers in Canada.
The message is clear: Working abroad is not a hassle, but it also guarantees freedom and pleasure.
For the past six months, Seán O'Donovan knows what it means to be based elsewhere.
Since there was nothing left to do in Ireland for this 29-year-old engineer, he now works with 15 Irish colleagues in Germany’s construction sector.
A fashion house comes out of the ground in Gelsenkirchen.
In January, he will continue to work on other Hannover or Berlin projects.
In the meantime, O'Donovan rarely visits his homeland, as he participates in an extra shift, in order to be able to finish repaying the credit contracted to build his home in the home country.
"The situation is already a bit crazy," he says, crossing the threshold of his house for the first time after a long time.
"I make money in Germany to build a house in Ireland where I practically don't live anymore."
He laughs embarrassed as he walks through the cold rooms.
The small house sits in a residential area with well-groomed front gardens, expensive cars from major brands are parked on access roads.
Four years ago, O'Donovan bought the house in common with his sister for an amount of 380,000 Euros, financed at 100 percent by credit.
“They were moments of madness, without a doubt, the money was easy,” he says.
Is he afraid to see it all collapse because of credit?
O'Donovan thought for a while, served hot tea and said, "I don't have any children to maintain and I'm young; even everything goes wrong, I can start from scratch."
The building engineer doesn't like the lamentations he's often heard lately.
"No one must suffer from hunger, our situation is still not as dramatic as what we once experienced here on the island," he said.
“Working abroad for a while isn’t the worst thing, maybe that’s what makes us feel Irish first.
He laughs and then talks about his father who went to England during the last major economic crisis.
“He was back after spending seven years there and, in Ireland, life simply continued.”
This idea pleases Cian Tanner.
Leave the future and find out how life goes elsewhere.
In his record, he already has a few years of work in London. That's why life doesn't have to be as easy in New Zealand for him and his girlfriend.
But there’s still one thing he’s worried about in recent days: the party to be held on the occasion of his 27th birthday in Dublin.
He's worried she won't be able to turn into a farewell evening.
“Only, not a lot of emotions,” he said, drinking a long sip of beer.
"A farewell still seems worrying."
"Let's call it rather an evening of goodbye, all relaxed, bringing together some friends, with a few drinks and a few games."
Democratic Routine
Support for democracy in Latin America continues to increase, as is support for private enterprises.
Crime has become a greater concern than unemployment.
And Brazil is seen as more influential than the United States in much of the region.
These are some of the findings of the latest Latinobarómetro survey in 18 countries and published exclusively by The Economist.
Given that the survey has been conducted on a regular basis since 1995, it is tracking attitude changes in the region.
There are two related elements from this year's survey results in September and early October.
The first one is Latin America's fairly sunny mood.
The second is the increasing stability of attitudes towards democracy and its main institutions.
Support for democracy has increased significantly in several countries around South America's Pacific (see table 1).
For example in Peru, where economic growth reached an average of 6% per year since 2002, support for democracy rose from a minimum of 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
It has also increased in Mexico, where the economy has recovered after experiencing a sharp decline in output last year.
But only about half of the respondents in Mexico and Brazil, the two most populous countries in the region, are convinced democrats, which lowers the regional average (see Table 2).
Some 44% of respondents in the region were satisfied with how their country's democracy worked in practice; this is the same figure as last year, but with a sharp increase of 25% in 2001 (Chart 3)
Some 34% said they had confidence in their country's parliament and 45% in their government, the same as last year in both cases; but up sharply compared to 2003 (respectively 17% and 24%).
Ten years ago, economic problems in Latin America undermined support for democracy.
If this stood firm during the global financial crisis, it is certainly due to the fact that many American countries have suffered only a brief recession and enjoyed a strong recovery.
In addition, a social security network has helped to protect the poor.
(The United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America reported this week that the proportion of Latin Americans living in poverty increased only slightly in 2009 and fell again this year to 32%.)
But the mood varies considerably from one country to another (see Figure 4).
Brazil’s good economic performance and the popularity of its outgoing president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, translates into a widely shared sense of progress
This is rarer in Mexico and Central America where the recession has been more severe and where violent crimes have worsened.
It is only for the second time since the survey began that more respondents across Latin America as a whole have cited crime rather than unemployment as the most important problem for their country (see Table 5).
Approximately 31% say that they or one of their relatives have been the victim of a crime in the past year; nevertheless, this figure is down from 38% last year and is the lowest since 1995.
More robust economic performance is reflected in the increase in support for private enterprises and the market economy: about 71% of respondents in the region say that private enterprise is essential for development, compared to 56% in 2007 (see Figure 6).
Some 58% believe that the market economy is the only system that will make their country developed, compared to 47% last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela agree (and 80% support the private company); even if their left-wing president, Hugo Chavez, expropriates from many private companies.
Brazil is the largest leader in the region, mentioned by 19% of respondents (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, unchanged from last year) and Venezuela (9%, down 11% since last year)
But the United States is regarded as the most influential country by respondents in Mexico and most of Central America, while Venezuela enjoys this distinction in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
A defiled vote
No one expected to have a perfect general election in Haiti on November 28.
But it was much worse than the outsiders who wanted to help the disaster-stricken country and plunged into fear.
Voting boxes and a new ballot were presented, ballots dispersed in gutters, ransacked polling stations and bullying were presented
Polling stations opened late at night.
Many Haitians, including many who were displaced as a result of the January earthquake, were wandering in the neighborhood to find where to vote.
Some of them ended up giving up.
A dozen presidential candidates, including several presumed favorites, denounced the fraud and called for the cancellation of the election.
Protests, sometimes violent, have exploded across the country.
Nevertheless, Edmond Mulet, the senior official in Haiti of the United Nations, considers the event "a fairly good election in many respects".
Observers from the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community cautiously stated that they “did not believe that these irregularities, however serious, had necessarily invalidated the process.”
In other words, the show continues.
The electoral authority, whose opponents are indebted to René Préval, unpopular outgoing president, and his candidate, Jude Célestin, must announce the preliminary results on December 7.
The expected second round is scheduled for January.
Any hope in the election is not lost.
The two opposition candidates Michel Martelly, a rap musician, and Mirlande Manigat, a former first lady, withdrew from their initial call for the vote to be declared void.
But there may be problems if Mr. Célestin, who has little or no discernible support among the voters, is declared by the electoral authority as having reached the second turn reached the second round.
With a raging cholera epidemic, and more than 1 million earthquake survivors still living in tents, there were fears that participation would be.
In this case, many Haitians wanted to vote but were barred from doing so because of disorganization.
Nearly 250 000 identity cards, which are necessary to vote, had not yet been distributed shortly before the election.
The issue is important.
Donors see the election of a people's and legitimate government as an important milestone in the reconstruction of Haiti.
And so, it's clear, Haitians do it themselves.
The tip of the feet towards the center
The weeks after suddenly becoming a widow, Cristina Fernández, president of Argentina, benefited from a wave of public sympathy that improved her approval rate by more than 50%; this is her highest level since she was elected in 2007.
However, turning this into a successful campaign for a second term in an election next year could depend on Ms. Fernández’s choice, visibly in mourning, according to which she wouldn’t give up the intransigence of her late husband, predecessor and former political partner, Néstor Kirchner.
In this regard, the signs are still confusing.
Kirchner, who continued to manage economic policy, even after his wife succeeded him as president, used to develop budgets that underestimated tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the first couple to spend the extra income at will.
Fernández's government lost its majority in Congress last year on paper.
But the opposition is divided, and the situation fell into disarray following Kirchner’s death from a heart attack on October 27.
Some of the dissident members of the ruling Peronist movement rallied to Mrs. Fernández.
But after chaotic sittings (where a parliamentarian punched one of his peers in the face and where others accused the government of corruption), Congress does not have to approve the 2011 budget.
"I'm not going to argue," the president said.
Currently, the 2010 budget will be implemented next year, giving the president even greater flexibility to expend at will.
The economy overheats widely: it is likely to grow by almost 9% this year.
The official rate of inflation is now at 11.1% but many private estimates and the wage demands of the unions, see this figure close to 30%.
Kirchner, who took office in 2003 when Argentina feared economic collapse, made a priority for growth and poverty reduction, rather than controlling inflation.
He refused to deal with the IMF, he harassed the private sector and purged INDEC, the statistical office, which carried out falsifications of statistics on official inflation.
Can Ms Fernández now moderate these policies?
She called for talks between private companies and union leaders.
And its officials say they want to limit salary increases next year to 18%.
The economy has been boosted by high global prices of Argentina’s agricultural exports.
But inflation eroded the boost to growth following a strong devaluation in 2002.
Perhaps that is why Ms. Fernández is showing signs of wanting to restore Argentina's financial relations to the rest of the world.
This year, the government entered into an agreement with the bondholders who had refused an earlier forced restructuring of the debt for which Argentina had not settled its maturities in 2001.
Now Ms Fernández seem to be edging towards an agreement with creditor governments grouped in the Paris Club, to which it owes some $6.7 billion.
The Kirchners have always insisted that this could not implicate IMF surveillance, as is usual.
But last month, Amado Boudou, the Minister of the Economy, announced that the Fund would provide "technical assistance" to design a new national consumer price index; which resembles a prelude to the consolidation of INDEC.
It is not clear whether Mrs Fernández wants to remain in politics beyond the end of her tenure, now that her husband is no longer by his side.
If she does, she needs to gain support from the middle class, among whom kirchnerism is widely advocated.
When the Argentinians have returned from their summer holidays in Mars, the wave of sympathy for it will have flowed back.
If she wants to win another election, she will have to decide whether or not to repudiate, without noise, many of her husband’s policies.
Half a Hurra for China
Taiwanese voters have moved towards a kind of closer union with the continent.
What does Taiwan think of its older brother located on the other side of the strait?
More than 7.5 million voters ran on Nov. 27, or 70% of eligible people, to elect mayors and municipal councillors in five urban districts, as part of what is considered a general evaluation of the president, Ma Ying-jeou.
Mr. Ma himself has become a substitute for feelings in relation to the continent, having forged innovative business ties, including a pact of partial free trade, in the largest reorganization of relations with China proper since 1949.
Mr. Ma passed the test, but just.
Although his Kuomintang (KMT) party won three of the five town halls, he failed to win a majority of votes.
Instead, the Democratic-Progressive Party (DPP), which leaned towards Taiwan’s full independence, won nearly 50 percent of all KMT votes of 45 percent.
If this had been a presidential election, Mr Ma would be in trouble.
Since he was elected two years ago, his party's voting share has fallen by about 8%.
For the first time, the DPP attracted voters, even with the KMT in municipal councils.
The event unveiled most of the main topics of the Taiwanese elections, including roasting campaign trucks and mass gathering flags with girls dancing in minimal attire.
There was also an attempt at mandatory mysterious assassination.
Sean Lien, son of Lien Chan, honorary president of the KMT, made an electoral tour for a KMT candidate on the eve of the election.
A man from the mob shot the young Mr. Lien, purely through his face, wounding him and killing a passer-by.
Bizarrely, it has not been determined if the shooter wanted to harm Mr. Lien or settle a dispute with the candidate.
In any case, says Tsai Ing-wen, the leader of the PDP, the murder had consequences on the election by directing suspicion on the DPP and turning voters towards the KMT.
Maybe the biggest winner of the elections was China.
It was striking to see how the question of the mainland itself was absent.
Instead, candidates played with plans for public housing and job creation.
Taiwan usually enjoys de facto independence and hates China’s military threat.
Thus, the KMT tends to avoid parading its ties with the Beijing government.
But this time, the DPP also refrained from stoking anti-Chinese sentiment.
This is the response to a new realization that Chinese companies are indispensable to Taiwan’s economic survival.
Mr. Ma, for his part, claimed confirmation as to whether his policy on China was acceptable to the Taiwanese majority.
It can be expected that the Beijing government will continue to provide trade facilities, in line with its strategy that aims to lure Taiwan, back home.
China did not waste time sending a negotiator to Taipei to host a summit on investment protection and medical cooperation.
Admittedly, she would also like to see political integration; but let’s start at the beginning.
It’s best to wait for that after the 2012 presidential election.
The DPP has reinvented anyway.
Two years ago, it was going wrong: its former leader, Chen Shui-bian, had been sent to prison following corruption charges.
President from 2000 to 2008, he was a provocateur in favour of explicit independence.
However, a new generation of moderates has emerged under the direction of Ms. Tsai and the PDP is doing everything possible to attract professionals and young people.
Ms. Tsai was hailed as a DPP presidential candidate, such as Su Tseng-chang, who served under Mr. Chen.
Their party is doing its best to show that they too can engage with Beijing.
Ms. Tsai announced plans for a panel of experts to act as a point of contact between the DPP and China, provided that their talks could take place without preconditions.
Ms. Tsai tells the Taiwanese public that her party presents a "trustworthy alternative" compared to Ma's hasty approach to China.
Mr. Chen appears to be exasperated by this change.
In a statement released from his cell, he stated that the PDP candidates would have achieved better results if they had insisted that Taiwan is a separate country.
He even ordered Mr. Su, a pragmatist, not to run for president.
Even without Mr. Chen's intervention, the DPP will find it difficult to choose between its potential candidates.
The KMT has to do an introspection of itself.
Mr. Ma's assessments have been weak; despite dizzying economic growth, KMT candidates did not want to campaign with him.
His unpopularity seems to be largely due to unemployment and the widening gap between rich and poor.
A KMT spokesman says its cabinet and local branches will be overhauled.
The good results of the PDP suggest that Taiwan’s support for rapprochement is limited.
At the same time, most Taiwanese see economic integration with China as inevitable.
According to Antonio Chiang, a prominent columnist, "the point of non-return has already passed."
Grammy nominations: Jazz singer Esperanza Spalding is a surprise selected in the "best new artist" category
Carrying out at the request of a president, and people are required to review it.
Esperanza Spalding, the 26-year-old jazz bassist and singer hand-picked by President Obama to perform at his 2009 Nobel Peace Prize ceremony, marked one of the surprises of this year’s Grammy Awards with a wide-ranging nomination in the “Best New Artist” category.
Considering that Spalding is a well-knowned figure on the jazz scene since her 2008 album, "Esperanza", the nomination remains a little mysterious but it is compatible with the rules of the Grammy Awards according to which an artist must not release more than three albums before the recording; which "establishes the public identity of this artist."
His ambitious 2010 album "Chamber Music Society", has certainly marked a step forward and, given his slightly engaging vocal style, skilful hand of pop and funk and Latin influences, it is possible to draw at least one line dotted at Spalding compared to Norah Jones' dazzling success in 2003, the closest equivalent for Grammy Award voters taking into account jazz productions in this category in recent years.
Elsewhere, Grammy Award voters have rightly honored Vijay Iyer Trio's "Historicity" in the "instrumental album" category, a very on an invigorating thrill of a recording that was one of last year's primordial favorites.
Among these Iyers, "Moody 4B" by veteran saxophonist James Moody and "Positootly" by keyboardist John Beasley will notably compete.
Trombone Shorty, a favorite with New Orleans hoarse vocals and frequent guest at HBO's "Treme", has been honored in the category of contemporary jazce, which also tilts sharply towards a certain fusion with Stanley Clarke, John McLaughlin and Jeff Lorber's albums.
Secret Society of the Savage Darcy James Argue, familiar with steampunk, gained some well-deserved recognitions in the big whole category; in competition with Billy Childs Ensemble's rich chamber jazz and eternal adventurer Dave Holland whose album "Pathways" of his octet's live was a fine example of the democratic hand of this bassist as group leader.
Nomination of the Grammy Awards: Michael Daugherty and Steve Mackey lead in classic categories
Classical nominations for the annual 53rd Grammy Awards feature a mix of landscapes of musicians and composers from around the world.
At the top of the nominations are "Deus Ex Machina" by Michael Daugherty, for the piano and orchestra, with five "yes"; and "Dreamhouse" by Steve Mackey, an electric orchestral work, with three "yes".
Among the nominees with links to Southern California are Estonian composer Arvo Pärt for his Symphony No. 4, "Los Angeles", a work that was commissioned by the Los Angeles Philharmonic Orchestra and which opened the Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
Los Angeles-based Madolin Concerto Avner Dorman is also nominated for an award.
Classical nominations are spread over 13 categories covering orchestral, opera, choir and solo recordings.
One category for the Classical Producer of the Year features five candidates: Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
The nominations for the classical album are Bruckner's Symphonies No. 3 and No. 4 from the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" by Daugherty of the Nashville Symphony Orchestra; "Dreamhouse" by Steven Mackey of the Boston Modern Orchestra and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castrati" featuring various artists
In the category of the opera, the nominees are "Lulu" by Berg of the Orchestra of the Royal Opera House, "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Adolf Hasse of the Ars Lyrica Houston; "L'Amour de Fail" by Kaija Saariaho of the Deutsches Symphonie-Orchester Berlin; "The Enchanted Wanderer" by Rodion Shchedrin of the Orchestra and the Chœur
Composers who are in place for the Grammy Awards for Contemporary Composing count Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina", Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionatamente Plus", Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Wanderer Enchanted".
Pärt’s Symphony No. 4 premiered at the Disney Hall in January 2009 in a performance recorded by the Los Angeles Philharmonic, conducted by Esa-Pekka Salonen.
The approximately 30-minute work is dubbed "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to the ex-oil tycoon Mikhail Khodorkovsky, imprisoned in Russia.
Dorman's "Mandolin Concerto" was nominated for his solo instrumental performance with an orchestra.
The Los Angeles composer said that he had written the 15-minute work especially for mandolin performer Avi Avital, who played the concerto with Metropolis Ensemble, headquartered in New York.
“I’ve written it using sounds from the Middle East and a large number of ranges and rhythms from North Africa,” Dorman said in a telephone interview.
Conductor Pierre Boulez, 85, received a nomination in the orchestral performance category for conducting the Chicago Symphony Orchestra in a recording of Stravinsky's works.
Instrumental soloists who received an appointment included pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin; organist Paul Jacobs, violinist Julia Fischer and harpist Sarah Schuster Ericsson.
This year there are only vocalist-solist nominees; here they are: Anne Sofie Von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucie Duchonová, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the category of classical blends, soprano Jessie Norman received a nomination for her recording "Roots: my life, my song"
The Temecula Mosque project wins the approval of the planning commission.
A mosque project in Temecula received the unanimous support of the local planning commission after more than five hours of public testimony, often passionate.
Critics attacked the project by claiming that it was promoting both terrorism and traffic congestion.
But commissioners ruled Wednesday night that the new religious center respected local laws and would be aesthetically impressive.
“Many good people have come to support him: Christians, Jews, Bahá’ís, you mention it,” said Hadi Nael, president of the Temecula Valley Islamic Center, speaking Thursday morning.
“I’m proud of it as an American citizen.”
"It shows that justice prevails."
“The opposition is really minimal,” he added.
"They just have very strong voices."
"We've got good people in this valley."
The session began at 6 p.m. and lasted until almost midnight with, most of the time, people pronouncing themselves for or against the center, at the talking time roughly equally distributed, according to the Press-Enterprise.
The debate continued on the newspaper's website.
"There's no more America," one speaker wrote.
"It's another planet of monkeys with Miss Liberty dead in the sand."
"America has fallen."
Planned Islamic centers have also sparked protests in other parts of the country, including at the Ground Zero, the site of the September 11, 2001 attacks in New York City.
Imam Mahmoud Harmoush denounced violence in Temecula Valley.
Her congregation on the order of 135 families co-existes without incident in Riverside County for years, but she wanted a larger and better space than the warehouse she rented on a shared property with a water filtration systems manufacturer.
The 24,943-square-footed mosque with Mediterranean-style architecture would consist of two minarets and would rise 43 feet high.
It would occupy a site of 4.32 hectares on the Nicolas road about two miles east of Chaparral High School.
Opponents can still appeal against this project to the municipal council.
An arsenic-tolerant microbe raises the possibility of new types of lives on Earth, or elsewhere.
The discovery of a strange bacterium, which can use arsenic as one of its nutrients, expands the field to find new forms of life on Earth and perhaps beyond.
Although the researchers discovered the unusual bacteria here on Earth, they say it shows that life has possibilities beyond the major elements that have been considered essential.
"This organism has double capacity."
"It can develop either with phosphorus or with arsenic."
“That’s why it’s very special, even though it’s far from being a really ‘foreign’ form of life,” said Paul Davies C. W. of the Arizona State University, co-author of the report featured in Thursday’s online edition of the journal Science.
Six major elements have long been considered essential for life: carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur.
But the researchers have discovered that the bacterium, discovered in Mono Lake in California, is able to continue to grow after replacing phosphorus with arsenic.
"We're wondering what's still possible," said Ariel Anbar D. of Arizona State University, co-author of the report.
Discovery is important when it comes to looking for life beyond Earth because researchers must be able to recognise life, to know what life looks like, Anbar says.
The study focuses on a microbe found on Earth.
However, the announcement of a press conference to discuss it, which did not disclose the details of the find, generated widespread speculation on the Internet that the report could disclose the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
That was not the case.
The discovery "shows that, in other planetary environments, organisms might be able to use other elements to actuate biochemistry and that the "standard" set of elements, which we deem absolutely necessary for life, might not be fixed," commented Charles Cockell, professor at the Institute for Planetary and Spacial Scientific Research, Open University, Milton Keynes, United Kingdom.
Cockell wasn't part of the research team.
"This work is new because it shows the substitution of one element in fundamental biochemistry and biochemistry," Cockell added.
It wasn't an accidental discovery.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA's Institute of Astrology, which led the study, has chosen Mono Lake because it has high levels of arsenic.
Arsenic and phosphorus are chemically similar; thus, it speculated that a microbe exposed to both substances may be able to replace each other.
"Arsenic is toxic mainly because its chemical behavior is really similar to that of phosphorus."
"As a result, organisms find it difficult to distinguish these elements separately."
“But arsenic is different enough that it doesn’t work exactly like phosphorus; it penetrates inside and, in a way, like the work of our biochemical machine,” Anbar explained.
The researchers collected the bacterium known as GFAJ-1 and exposed it to increasing arsenic concentrations to which it was able to adapt and grow.
The microbe grows better with phosphorus but the fact that it can live with arsenic instead raises the possibility that a form of life using arsenic can occur naturally, either elsewhere on Earth or on another planet or moon where arsenic is more common.
Jamie S. Foster, an assistant professor of microbiology at the University of Florida, said the idea that arsenic could be replaced with phosphorus wasn’t new; but nothing has ever shown that it can work.
Arsenic was more common at the beginning of Earth's existence, she says; thus, the researchers hypothesized that the first forms of life could have used it.
This suggests that there could be other ways of forming life, not just the way life on Earth was formed at first,” said Foster, who wasn’t part of Wolfe-Simon’s research team.
The research was funded by NASA, the Ministry of Energy and the National Institute of Health.
The last two stolen exhibition dogs have been found in Compton
The other twick dogs out of four flights from outside a motel to Bellflower were found, Los Angeles County sheriffs announced Thursday.
The four exhibition dogs — two akitas and two Welsh Pembroke corgis — disappeared on Tuesday when someone stole the Chevrolet Express pickup truck in which they had spent the night outside a Bellflower motel 6, authorities said.
Two of the dogs were recovered Wednesday afternoon and the other two were found late in the evening on Wednesday, Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department spokesman Steve Whitmore said.
"The good news is that dogs are okay," Whitmore said.
"They're in good shape."
"They have been returned to their rightful owners."
Whitmore refused to say precisely where the dogs had been found, in which neighborhood and what were the circumstances of their discovery.
“An investigation is ongoing.”
"We don't want to say," Whitmore said.
"We're following orders."
Dog owners Kristina Rickard and David Peek couldn’t be reached to provide a comment Thursday.
They were reunited Wednesday afternoon with their two Akitas at an animal shelter in Downey.
The sheriff's deputies tracked down the van and the two dogs separately in Compton.
The Akitas - Trace, a 3-year-old male, and Didi, a 10-month-old female - were safe, authorities said.
Bunny and Peter, the corgis that Rickard and Peek had planned to show at regional competitions this week, on behalf of the owners of the Japanese dogs, were missing until their discovery on Wednesday night.
Peek and Rickard, who live in Redding, are in town this weekend as part of Long Beach’s National Championship “AKC/Eukanuba”, where they planned to present Trace.
The other dogs were expected to participate in competitions of other events scheduled in the days prior to the "AKC/Eukanuba" exhibition, Peek told the Times on Wednesday.
Southern California is one of the stops of the couple’s nearly-a-month-long canine exhibition tour.
On Monday night, Peek and Rickard checked into the motel.
They often stay in the motels and park their van outside, right next to their bedroom window.
They took three other dogs — a Boxer puppy, a Parson Russell burrow, and a Breton spaniel — who stayed with them in the motel’s room.
But they left the Akitas and the corgis, with the thicker fur, in the van where it would be fresher.
They opened the windows a bit, so the dogs could look fresh.
Peek and Rickard reported the theft of the van at 6 a.m. Tuesday.
Peek said they would offer a reward to anyone who could take them to the corgis, equipped with an implanted microchip to identify them.
It is not clear whether an award was given on Thursday.
The lion is ready to blush again, the restructuring of MGM awaits only the final green light of the court
After 18 months of turbulence and uncertainty, MGM finally got the green light to start from scratch.
A New York federal bankruptcy judge on Thursday officially praised the “pre-packaged” plan by creditors that about US$5 billion in debt would be erased; paving the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company to focus its efforts on the production and distribution of movies and TV shows.
With Thursday’s decision that the plan meets the requirements of the U.S. Bankruptcy Code, there is no legal hurdles remaining ahead of MGM to get afloat under the tutelage of the founders of Entertainment Spyglass, Gary Barber and Roger Birnbaum, who will become the managing directors of what will be a lean studio.
The staff will be reduced to about 320 out of 400 at departure.
MGM will not actually emerge from Chapter 11 until JPMorgan Chase enters into a US$500-million-dollar credit facility, planned to finance MGM’s new operations and productions.
It is currently licensed by the investment bank, which itself faces US$75 million, and is expected to be funded around mid-December.
The process of restructuring the studio began in May 2009 when investment bank Moelis & Co. was hired to advise management on how to invest its finances given that it could not afford to pay interest amounting to US$300 million annually.
In August, general manager Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former CEO of Enron and a turnaround expert known for his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Moelis and Cooper’s attempts to solve MGM’s financial difficulties and satisfy the studio’s more than 140 creditors took much longer than expected.
After several months of discussions, the parties agreed at the end of 2009 to put the company for sale.
However, the auction attracted few bidders, none of whom were willing to pay the sum of 2 billion more whose creditors were looking.
In the spring of this year, it was necessary to redo everything when the MGM team had begun to work on a restructuring plan that, after months of internal disagreements and debates, was finally approved by creditors at the end of October.
When MGM officially emerges from bankruptcy in the following weeks, Moelis and Coopers received multimillion-dollar premiums for their work.
Moelis & Co., which has already secured US$1.2 million, will receive a payment of US$9.5 million.
Cooper's companies, which raised $375 000 per month for their company management services, will receive a "success premium" of US$4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum plans to relaunch the production of MGM, which has released only one film this year, and set up an annual list of seven or eight films that would be released from 2012.
They also intend to co-finance two films based on "The Hobbit", with Warner Bros
The studio must make a separate loan between $265 000 and $275 million to finance its share in these two films, which must be directed by Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who owns about 18% of MGM’s debt, has attempted to carry out a merger of the studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, where he is the main shareholder.
However, the recent discussions between the two companies did not lead to an agreement.
Tech toys to keep your gift budget in balance
Although the main contestant for this year’s most wonderful toy is IPAD, followed, perhaps, by Microsoft’s video game system, Kinect, both can make a significant breach within your gift budget.
Consider that, for the price of an iPad, you can buy five $99 BMX bikes and that a Kinect game costs the same price as 10 classic bead jump ropes, or about 48 boxes of Play-Doh.
But there are ways to keep your gift budge balanced.
Recently, I visited three national toy sellers – Wal-Mart, Toys “R” Us and BJ Wholesale Club – to see the electronic toys I could buy for less than $80.
It’s clear that Moore’s Law has evolved, this year’s technology toy is likely to have more strength; while costing the same price if not a little less, compared to last year.
Furthermore, while there are generally fewer new toys associated with websites, two of the most well-knowned of this type, Webkinz and Club Penguin, have more offers.
And button-powered toys are increasing, as well as rechargeable battery toys.
Here are a few recommendations.
(All prices are from Toys "R" Us, unless otherwise specified.)
Sing-a-ma-jigs (Mattel, $13 for 3 years and older) are stuffed toys the size of a guinea pig that sing a note when you squeeze them on your stomach.
A pressure is equal to a note, and a second one makes it possible to compose a song.
If you buy several, they will sing in harmony.
Caution: Once you start pressing, you can no longer stop.
To combine singing with dancing, Dance Mickey Star (Fisher-Price, $60 for 2 years and older) is the best choice.
Drawing inspiration from last year's Elmo Live technology, the 14-inch animatronic doll plays games in turn like Simon Says and can do lunar dancing thanks to its innovative hinged feet with wheel blocking.
The children who tried the toy uses Mickey as a dance instructor while their parents complain about a lack of volume control.
If you’re an 80’s child, you’ll remember Alphie, one of the first computerized educational toys.
It’s back (Hasbro / Playskool, $40 for 3 years and older), with a backpack filled with questionnaire game cards designed to turn a car ride into a session with self-correction and valve workbook.
If you’re wary when your child holds your smartphone, here are three stand-alone alternatives.
VTech’s Mobigo ($60, for 3 to 8 year olds) is the cheapest and easiest to use, along with a qwerty keyboard, although games don’t educate as efficiently as the Explorer Leapster ($70, for 3 to 12-year-olds), it’s the first major overhaul of the Leapster range, with a newer and brighter screen.
No versions are compatible with old cartridges.
Fisher-Price's iXL ($90, for 3 to 7 year olds) uses an iTunes-like system to offer games and e-books and is equipped with an SD card input that displays family photos.
Finally, Scrabble Flash ($30, Hasbro, for ages 8 and over) is a set of five blocks barely larger than a matchbox, each with its own LED screen.
The goal is to form words by placing the blocks online before time runs out, it's ideal for an impromptu spelling contest.
More and more, toys have some kind of online connection.
Based only on their presence in toy stores, the twicariest are Webkinz and Club Penguin.
To buy your child a Club Penguin online pet, for example, you can get a Penguin Disney Club 2"Mix 'N Match Figure Pack, $5, for ages 6 to 12) that includes a coin marked with a hidden code.
When you redeem the code online, your child receives enough credits at the Club Penguin store for two items of choice.
They can be roller skates or a Puffle toy pet.
Slightly larger than the hair at the end of your toothbrush, Hexbug Nano ($10, for 5 years and older) is the last button-piled micro-robots.
The fake critter moves quickly on 12 vibrating silicone pastes in random directions.
It’s fun to make a labyrinth of blocks to see if she can find her way through.
It's also solid.
Ours withstood a fall from the kitchen table and even a momentary passage through our dog’s mouth.
Bring a milder side to policing in China
Tang Shenbin surveyed a square in the city like an urban sergeant in training by severely inspecting his nervous loads, issuing orders with a military authority in a low voice.
He wanted chengguan’s female members—ignoring officials of public order in China, feared and despised for their arbitrary repression and penchant for violence—to pass on some impression to a group of spectators.
"Stand right!"
"Be piercing!"
Show them, he said, "what pretty girls look like!"
Four daughters having barely spent adolescence in white gloves, dressed in identical jackets and trousers of olive color, froze at attention.
Four pairs of heeled black shoes, lined up to the ruler.
Four neat hats perfectly perched at the top of hair fastened by a striped knot in blue and white.
“Personally, I think they have an average appearance,” Tang said, with disdain.
"The models are pretty."
More than one government has tried to refresh the image of urban inspectors in China.
One city demanded that all new recruits have a graduate degree.
Guangdong province has traded the grey-green of uniforms for a supposedly more engaging blue.
Wuhan, in Central China, replaced their eyes by the strong way: in 2009, a report reported that 50 officers had encircled an uncontrollable snack cart by throwing black glances, firmly for half an hour, until the hawker repackaged his belongings and left.
Xindu, an urban district of 680 000 inhabitants in Chengdu, has chosen to do a major image operation.
Since 2003, the district has supplemented its urban street policing of 13 women, specially chosen for their appearance, curve and young age.
The idea is to give the police a softer, more feminine side.
Unfortunately, even Scarlett Johansson may find it difficult to draw attention to China’s underground urban brigade
And for good reason, the critics will say.
Unlike the police, these officers are only authorized to enforce municipal ordinances by imposing fines or other administrative penalties.
But the Chinese media depict the news of a different reality.
In January 2008, inspectors in Hubei province beat an observer to death after he used his mobile phone to film them dispersing a protest against a dump of waste.
Last year, a training manual for Beijing’s inspectors, stolen and uploaded, describes how to effectively defeat offenders without emitting blood.
This year in Shanghai, a watermelon peddler was abandoned with a brain injury after a fight with five officers.
A video game imbued with violence, available by online download, shows Chinese inspectors trained in the onslaught of street vendors.
“Chengguan marked the government,” China Daily, a national publication, last year lasted, after yet another polemic on tactics.
The diary demanded a "real complete cleaning."
Skeptics say the approach is far from it.
After the district made an announcement in October to find eight new female recruits, an editorial, published in The Beijing Evening News, questioned whether women had real tasks or whether they were just tourist entertainment.
The answer seems to be a bit of both.
The announcement of the district required female candidates, aged between 18 to 22, to have a beautiful silhouette and "the five facial features in the right order."
They must be off-average size, either 1.6 meters or 5 feet, 2 1⁄2 inches.
Retirement at the age of 26 is compulsory.
Officers said the work was physically too burdensome for women over the age of 25.
“It’s their image that’s important,” a certain district officer told Rednet.com, a quasi-governmental website.
"Primarily, the external qualities of the candidates will determinate whether they are suitable; these are the size, weight, facial features, etc."
Then come the disposition and the "inner qualities."
Chengguan’s wives are like flowering vases,” he said, adding: “In addition to being vases, they’ll have other responsibilities.
Zheng Lihua, deputy director of the City District Management Office, does not adhere to this description.
But he noted that height requirements were the standard standard in many Chinese job vacancies for both sexes.
This is the case of the requirement relating to the order of the facial features.
How beautiful this means is a topic of debate among the Chinese.
Admittedly, people with disabilities or disfigured should not apply.
"We can't leat a person boxing or a hunchback coming to serve here," Zheng said.
"He wouldn't give a good picture."
Liu Yi, who patrols Baoguang Square near a monastery, is 22 years old and has appled cheeks with a finely curved mouth.
She doesn’t see the enhancement of her appearance to be sexist, she said.
"Do you think I look sexy in this uniform?" she asked with a disillusioned air.
Xu Yang, his 21-year-old dimple colleague, says, “Our job is to present the image of the city.”
They do not object to having their mandate limited, they said, because they harbor greater career ambitions than to simply drive sellers into the alleys where they are supposed to limit their activities.
Every morning, the team confronts a dozen street vendors who move quickly on foot or by bike, trying to sell as many buns or tofu bowls as possible before fleeing.
"Master Wang, you have to leave."
“We’ve already told you several times!” said Ms. Xu as a salesman fled on foot, temporarily abandoning her noodle cart bike.
Agents describe their duties as monotonous rather than daunting.
“It’s pretty much the same every day,” said Huang Jing, a 20-year-old who studies post-work marketing.
"Great routine."
One of the reasons is that female agents don't have the power of their male counterparts to confiscate sellers' property.
They can only threaten to report offenders to their male superiors.
This tends to protect them from sudden public exposures of animosity against bureaucracy which are common throughout China.
This year, hundreds of citizens in Kunming, the capital of Yunnan province, revolted after false rumors spread that chengguan agents had killed a vendor.
In one night, more than a dozen chengguan’s officers were injured, 14 government vehicles were overturned or set on fire.
Xindu has so far escaped such violence.
Calm is hardly guaranteed.
Just two blocks from Placidic Baoguang Square, where female officers were patrolling that morning, more than 50 people gathered around the corner.
Officers confiscated a motorcycle that was being repaired on the sidewalk rather than in a store, as required by regulations.
The owner of the motorcycle was screaming scandal.
A 15-minute standoff ensued before the sinister-faced officers made their walks towards their vehicle and left at full speed with the motorcycle and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a coordinator attached to the men's team, stayed behind, his white badge returned to hide his name.
Like female officers, coordinators - men aged 40 years or older - play purely a supporting role.
Contrary to them, they are not trained in order to maintain a wax image.
"Sometimes we fight verbally."
"Sometimes we physically fight," said Mr. Li said in a neutral tone.
"Most of the time, it's the audience that's starting."
Prosecutors won't charge Senator Ensign
Prosecutors at the Department of Justice have lifted the criminal charges against Senator John Ensign, stemming from a case related to an assistant to a former campaign and his efforts to gain lobbying for the woman's husband, the senator's lawyers said Wednesday.
The move puts an end to the most serious legal threat confronted by Mr. Ensign, the Nevada Republican who was considered a possible Republican presidential candidate in 2012 until the scandal around his case made national headlines last year and hurt his career.
Ensign's office said he was "very happy" with the decision, the result of a one-year investigation.
The senator could still be exposed to disciplinary action taken by the Senate ethics committee.
Ensign's lawyers stated that the Department of Justice had "informed that Senator Ensign was no longer under investigation and that he did not intend to maintain any charges against him in this case."
The Department of Justice has refrained from commenting on this matter.
The announcement came as a sort of surprise for many ethics lawyers outside the Department of Justice that there is significant evidence that Ensign has conspired to violate a federal ban on lobbying.
Interviews, e-mails and other documents, first released in October 2009 in the New York Times, indicated that Mr. Ensign had helped Douglas Hampton, who was one of his assistants, to obtain lucrative lobbying work to gain silence from Mr. Hampton and his wife, Cynthia, about the senator's case with her.
Ms. Hampton once served as Treasurer during an Ensign campaign.
Mr. Ensign contacted a number of political funders to find work for Mr. Hampton and then defended their rights after they hired Mr. Hampton to lobby for them; in accordance with documents and interviews.
Since Mr. Hampton had recently been an employee of Mr. Ensign, he was prohibited for one year by law from lobbying his former employer or the Senate as a whole.
Ethical experts have argued that the unusual agreement between Mr. Ensign and Mr. Hampton, who were close friends prior to the case, appears to be in contradiction with the so-called ban on lobbying.
“There appears to be prima facie evidence of a conspiracy to rape and a substantial violation of revolving door law,” said Stanley Brand, a Washington lawyer who specializes in defending clients in an investigation by the government.
Although the department's judge declined to explain the reasoning that led to the Decision in the Ensign case, Mr. Brand and other ethical lawyers said that the department appeared to be wary of political prosecution after a number of high-level setbacks by his Public Integrity Section, including last year's decision to drop charges against former Senator Ted Stevens accused of failing suit.
Federal prosecutors are clearly afraid to take up arms, said Melanie Sloan, the general manager of citizens for accountability and ethics in Washington, a defense group that has received several complaints against Mr. Ensign in the case.
"This say high-ranking government officials now assumes the laws don't apples to them", she added.
Paul Coggins, Ensign's senior lawyer, said in an interview that he received a phone call on Monday from a prosecutor of the Department of Justice and a follow-up the next day, telling him that the department was not seeking charges against his client.
Ensign's team of lawyers had cooperated with prosecutors in an attempt to persuade them that the senator, as his office reaffirmed Wednesday in a statement, "acted in accordance with the law."
Mr Coggins refused to clarify the legal arguments he had made to the public prosecutor's office.
“I’m not comfortable getting into the details of my discussions with the Department of Justice, except to say that we think they’ve made the right decision,” he said.
Last month, the Federal Election Commission decided to abandon an investigation following an independent complaint by the ethics group, arguing that the payment of $96,000 by Mr. Ensign's parents to the Hampton violated federal electoral law.
The electoral commission said it did not have enough evidence to prove that the money was anything but a gift.
NV Energy, a major Nevada company that has long supported the senator, is a company for which Mr. Ensign has helped Mr. Hawton find lobbying work.
An e-mail sent in December 2008 showed that, after the company greeted Mr. Hampton as a lobbyist, Mr. Ensign's chief of staff John Lopez contacted Mr. Hampton and told him that he was "hammering" the Ministry of the Interior on an issue in dispute.
Mr. Ensign also participated in a luncheon with Mr. Hampton and another client of Nevada, Allegiant Air, and asked the Transportation Secretary to schedule a meeting on behalf of the airline after committing Mr. Hampton to lobby.
When news of the case surfaced last year, Ensign apologized to his constituents and resigned his position as the fourth-ranked Republican leader in the Senate.
After maintaining a low profile for several months, Mr. Ensign said two weeks ago that he intended to run for a third term in the Senate.
In announcing that Ensign was no longer the target of a criminal investigation, his spokeswoman, Jennifer Cooper, said the senator hoped that the ethics committee would quickly follow the Justice Department's example and end its investigation.
Senator Ensign is pleased to continue his hard work on behalf of the people of Nevada, she said.
Cheshire's call will point to Twitter
Half a dozen of them were typing in the rows of the courtroom.
They sent reports of horrific testimonies every minute via Twitter.
At the end, the defense said there were more than 140 000 messages about the trial of the rape case and the children tied to their bed in a house that was set on fire.
On Thursday, Steven J. Hayes, who was convicted of the murder of three members of the Cheshire family in Connecticut, is expected to result in an official end where the judge would impose the death penalty, voted by the jury.
But Mr. Hayes' lawyers have already tabled evidence in the court that outlines appellate arguments, likely to occupy the courts for years.
What’s provocative is that the intense reporting on the trial – as well as the widespread and extensive use by Twitter journalists – created a “circus atmosphere”, so widespread and instantaneous saturation with inflammatory details that the jury was misinfused by the public’s passions.
The request could prompt appellate judges to discuss whether new technologies require new rules in courtrooms.
"I experienced it in this trial; I think there's a real danger, the introduction of new technologies in courtrooms violates human rights to a fair trial," said Thomas J. Ullmann, a lawyer for Mr. Hayes, chief lawyer.
Trial Judge Jon C. Bleue of the State Superior Court laconically dismissed the claims in last week's ruling, saying the media was "carefully controlled" and there was no evidence that the jury was animated by passion.
But that doesn’t seem to be the last word on the issue.
Courts have recognized for decades that media coverage of well-known cases can raise questions about the fairness of a trial.
In a 1966 ruling, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the conviction of a Cleveland osteopath, Dr. Sam Sheppard, for the murder of his wife, arguing in his ruling that there was an "atmosphere of carnival at trial" because of the media.
The crime is considered by some to have been the inspiration for the television show and the movie "The Fugitive".
Some lawyers have stated that the appeals tribunals seized to review Mr. Hayes' sentencing half a century later may be curious given the argument that new rules may be necessary to manage the means of communication in courtrooms, unimaginable at the time of Dr. Sheppard's case.
“There’s a general reason to worry about the impact of the new medias” in far-reaching trials, said John J. Donohue III, a professor at Stanford Law School who studied the death penalty in Connecticut and wrote a report that it was arbitrarily applied.
Professor Donohue argues that some appellate judges could be intrigued by the idea that restricting instantaneous communication from courtrooms could allow for fairer trials.
Twitter's case is just one of many described by Mr. Hayes' attorneys, who may be discussed for years, delaying the execution.
Defence lawyers also say, for example, that Mr. Hayes suffered harm when Justice Blue refused to allow them to test the reactions of potential jurors by showing them gruesome photos of criminal scenes during jury selection.
Some of the jurors said they were horrified by photos of the victims, Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48, and her daughters, Hayley, 17, and Michaela, 11.
Defense lawyers say they will also challenge the constitutionality of the death penalty and argue that the judge unfairly expelled one of the jurors, who had been critical to prosecutors, and that he abusively put pressure for a verdict by making the unusual decision to get the jury to deliberate in one weekend.
Jurors have not been kidnapped.
But their arguments about the effect of the intense media coverage of the trial could be the greatest source of interest.
They noted in a protest against last month’s verdict that the case dominated the media scene in Connecticut and that it was being discussed during the election campaign by candidates for governor and the U.S. Senate.
They maintained that the computer media continued to stoke the flames of the emotion of the case.
"The daily scramble of media tweets, cameras and journalists in front of the courthouse," the lawyers wrote, "were irrelevant to nothing that had been seen before here, in New Haven, and perhaps even nationwide."
Their focus on the coverage of the Twitter system is controversial.
In the interviews, some reporters said that the claim, that instant news was more incendiary that press releases delivered more slowly, was a Luddistic view that would impoverish the Court’s policy governing media coverage of future trials.
But some lawyers said appeals courts were often drawn to arguments that social and technological changes required court adaptation.
Mr. Hayes' lawyers insist on this claim.
In their case, they make a reference to the 1966 Supreme Court ruling that overthrew Dr. Sheppard's conviction because of media coverage of the trial and clarified that they would invoke the need for new rules for the new media.
"This case," they wrote, represents that of Sheppard v. Maxwell of the 21st century.
Can civility bring cleavages closer together?
Following an invective and negative campaign, a minimum of civility erupted in Washington this week.
Is it a warning sign of genuine bipartisan cooperation forward or an illusory moment before the rapid return to the partisan struggle in the capital?
Tuesday's meeting between President Obama and congressional leaders produced no breakthrough on the major issues to be addressed during the "lame duck" session.
But obviously, there was at least a polite discussion about the differences of the two sides as well as the recognition of their shared responsibilities to govern, rather than another episode where each side wanted to score points.
Wednesday's public meeting of the committee on the president's deficit has been equally civilized, despite the political differences that remain between the members.
It seems clear that the close interaction of several months has created a mutual respect that transcends the ideological gap; but which, however, still divides members.
This is hardly enough to declare that a new era of cooperation has arrived.
Both sides are now in a period of trial and mutual testing for signs of cooperation and compromise, while trying to assess the limits of public opinion and the patience of voters for a renewed partisan war.
This is why Tuesday’s meeting and subsequent comments from participants disclosed a minimum of demands or accusations.
The philosophical differences between Obama and Republicans are too great and too deep to suggest a rapid climate change that has existed over the past two years.
Whether it’s taxation, spending, health care or rights, both parties stay miles away.
However, the November election moderated Washington, at least in the short term.
“There’s no doubt that things have changed in tone,” said Pete Wehner, an official in the Bush administration, now alongside the Center for Ethical and Public Policy.
"It's inevitable after elections.
Elections arouse partisan passions, and then they fade.
That's good."
On certain issues, including those on the agenda of the "lame duck" session, Republicans and Democrats have no choice but to find a solution.
Bush’s tax cuts will expire at the end of the year, and no one is in favor of that.
Both parties want to find a way to preserve some or all of them, permanently or temporarily.
Likewise, a compromise is possible on the extension of unemployment benefits, if only because Republicans cannot afford to give the impression that they are in favor of tax cuts for the rich, while denying unemployment benefits to unemployed workers.
After Tuesday's meeting in the White House, more optimism hangs over a possible agreement on the issues raised during the session relating to the "laminog duck", with the ratification of the "New START" pact.
But William Galston, of the Brookings Institution, warns that fruitful cooperation on the most pressing issues of the "lame duck" session will not necessarily lead to a better understanding between the two parties when the 112th Congress meets in January.
Instead, he says, a period of disagreement is likely or even unavoidable.
“Once the things that need to be done will, psychology in the short term will return to one of the confrontations,” he said.
Wehner also expressed his widely shared skepticism that civility would lead to genuine cooperation.
“I see most of what’s happening between today and 2012 as a pre-show or shadow-boxing compared to the main event of 2012, he said.
The reasons are closely related to the composition of the new republican majority in the House and the voters present in tea who contributed to its creation.
However, Republican leaders are aware of the dangers if they go too far, they know they have to try to live up to their promises to cut public spending and repeal the new health care law.
These partisan positions are already clear in the first reactions to the recommendations of the Deficit Committee, which include reforming the tax code and increasing certain taxes, raising the retirement age and cutting spending.
Co-Chairs Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have chosen one of Washington’s toughest tasks and have, in their opinion, imposed the issue of the deficit on the public agenda in a way that cannot be ignored.
But they need 14 votes on the 18-member commission to issue their plan as an official recommendation, and they struggled to reach that number.
The greatest resistance remains among the elected representatives of the group; which suggests that there will be on tax issues, at least today, few motives or willingness to compromise.
Galston predicts two phases in the fighting between the White House and Congressional Republicans.
The first will come next year in the form of partisan clashes, given that Republicans are challenging Obama when it comes to budgeting, taxes and health care; and then followed by a period of greater bipartisan cooperation.
What would be the cause of the change?
public opinion.
It is widely proven that, despite the partisan spirit of the recent election, a majority of Americans still want to see both parties working together.
If the confrontation leads to an impasse, as in 1995, then both parties will carefully observe the reaction of the public.
Whoever will be judged in loss will have to change course and find compromises.
However, that would be an optimistic point of view.
The partisan spirit and polarization are greater today than they were in the middle of the 1990’s, and there seems to be a greater ideological gap between Obama and Congressional Republicans then between President Bill Clinton and the Republicans.
At the same time, activists from both parties see 2010 as a mere prelude to a larger election in 2012.
Given this reality, the civility exposed this week could be appreciated by many Americans.
But it will take much more effort on the part of both sides to find common ground on the issues that still divide them.
Leaked U.S. documents describe Moscow as a haven of corruption
Control of the city is in the hands of a "Kleptocracy" and pass to the Kremlin some of the bribes and the protective money it collects, the U.S. Embassy in Russia reported in a note in February.
The police and the Federal Security Service are making huge rackets that help explain the high cost of living in Moscow, he said.
They collect money not only from legitimate companies but also from organized criminal groups.
Each layer of the bureaucracy – what Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has called the “vertical of power” — takes its part as the money travels up the ladder.
The memo, which was sent to Washington as Ambassador John Beyrle, was posted by Wikileaks on its website on Wednesday.
The note is based on sources whose names have been removed.
It was drafted while Yuri Luzhkov was still mayor of Moscow; and he is largely held responsible for corruption.
He was fired in October by President Dmitry Medvedev, but there have since been little traces of clean-up.
Lujkov and his wife, Elena Baturina, who owns a leading construction company, have always denied the corruption charges over the years; and he has won several defamation lawsuits on these charges.
In September, he said the following to a Russian television channel, when asked about a documentary suggesting he was corrupt: "It's crazy, it's to throw away, it's anything."
The Russians believe, by an overwhelming majority, that their society is corrupt; according to polls.
But it's unusual to find the information outlined in an embassy report; and it's unheard-of to conceive that it'd be to diplomats from a foreign country to do so.
The direct link between the government of the city of Moscow and crime has led some people to call it "dysfunctional" and claim that the government functions more like a kleptocracy than a government, according to the embassy's note.
"Criminal elements enjoy a "krysha" (term used in the criminal world/mafia which literally means "roof" or protection) that passes through the police, the Federal Security Service (FSB), the Ministry of the Interior (MVD) and the Prosecutor's Office, as well as throughout the government bureaucracy of the city of Moscow.
“Analysts to identify a three-level structure in Moscow’s criminal world.”
"Luzhkov's at the top."
"The FSB, the MVD and the militias are on the second level."
"Finally, ordinary criminals and corrupt inspectors find themselves at the lowest level."
“This is an inefficient system in which criminal groups fill a vacuum in some areas where the city does not provide certain services.”
The report states that the FSB brews the money of the largest companies and that the police target the small.
A source, he said, “explained that Moscow’s business owners understood that it was better to get protection from the MVD and FSB (rather than organized crime groups) because not only they have more weapons, more means and more power than criminal groups but they are also protected by law.”
“That’s why the demand for protection by criminal gangs isn’t as high anymore.”
The note points out that, although the collection of money is extended, the protection itself can be uneven; even those who pay are sometimes subjected to arrest.
Those who don’t pay quickly see their belongings close under one pretext or another.
Medvedev spoke several times about the fight against corruption, but he admitted little progress.
The note of the embassy transmits reports of men who bring suitcases, probably filled with money, to the Kremlin.
“In his fight against corruption, Medvedev depends on the bureaucrats,” Gueorgui Satarov, director of Moscow’s think tank Indem, said in a recent interview.
"But he's part of this bureaucracy."
"He's not part of a political class because he doesn't exist in Russia."
Without politics, without opposition, without separation of powers, he said, corruption is inevitable.
According to Indem, the costs of corruption amount to more than 300 billion dollars per year across Russia.
The country was ranked 154th in a recent poll on the overall perception of corruption by the non-governmental organization Transparency International (countries are ranked from least to most corrupt).
The disclosure of the U.S. embassy's note, which was realized only yesterday, late in the evening Moscow time, will stir with certainty discontent within the Russian government; although Luzhkov can be blamed, to a certain extent, for Moscow's failures, this could be an opportunity for the Kremlin to show that he is solving the problem.
Interviewed by the CNN by Larry King, Putin hypothesized that WikiLeaks documents could be a fabrication and reacted angriely to what U.S. diplomats had leaked in by calling him Batman and bringing Medvedev closer to Robin.
"The problem lies in the fact that this is about our interaction, an important factor in domestic policy in this country," he said.
“But to be honest with you, we didn’t suspect that it would be achieved with such arrogance, such pressure and, you know, in opposition to ethics.”
Khmer Rouge veterans forced to face the past
In a poorly lit classroom with low-quality decrepit concrete walls, Anlong Veng's high school principal referred to the man who had built him, the last leader of the Khmer Rouge, Ta Mok.
"Everyone here loves Ta Mok."
“He was a good leader; and he cared about his people,” said Sreng Kor Ma, 42.
Known as the "butcher" for his brutality, when he was at the head of the Khmer Rouge, this commander remains popular in this isolated bastion of former Khmer Rouge in north-western Cambodia where he built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure; and where thousands of former soldiers of the organization still live today.
But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge surrendered to the government, long-standing loyalty is finally questioned to Anlong Veng.
In April, a local truth and reconciliation forum allowed the victims to publicly deal with the people who had participated in the regime.
In June, the government distributed a high school textbook which, for the first time, teaches the history of the Khmer Rouge to the children of its former soldiers.
And, in July, a Cambodian court, headed jointly with the United Nations, handed down its first sentence for a former Khmer Rouge member, sentencing 35-year prison, the former head of Tuol Sleng’s infamous torture center, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as the “Duch.”
Faced with this evolution of the situation, anxiety has increased among the Khmer Rouge veterans of Anlong Veng; complicating reconciliation efforts and their willingness to reintegrate into Cambodian society.
“There are resentments and fear among the ancient Khmer Rouge but they are powerless to do anything,” said Chhang Youk, director of Cambodia’s Documentation Center, an independent body that conducts research on the regime.
"For them, life under the Khmers Rouge was glorious but the regime became a symbol of evil."
"The situation generates divisions within families."
During the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia from 1975 to 1979, about 1.7 million people were executed or died of starvation, disease or overwork.
When the Vietnamese invaded and overthrew Pol Pot’s government in 1979, the rest of the regime and his army fled to Cambodia’s border with Thailand.
There, they launched an insurgency that lasted until the last of the movements surrendered in December 1998.
As Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary ran the guerrillas of their base located in Cambodia’s Western Mountains and in the jungle, Ta Mok was preparing a sequel to Anlong Veng.
But in the middle of the 1990s, after a UN-sponsored peace agreement led to the country's first democratic elections in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began defecting to the government, culminating with the capitulations of Ieng Sary in 1996 and Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan in 1998.
Pol Pot died of natural causes in 1998 and Ta Mok, who had stood up, was captured by government forces in 1999 in the mountains near Dangrek.
He died in a prison in Phnom Penh in 2006 awaiting trial.
Without their benefactors, most Khmer Rouge veterans have descended into black misery, common to the rural areas of Cambodia; and many remain nostalgic for the movement.
Although a few elite Khmer Rouge officers kept their positions as local leaders in exchange for laying down their arms, they are unskilled farmers in the low rank and file.
“These people received very little follow-up after surrender,” said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, which organizes public forums across Cambodia, including in Anlong Veng.
"They are imbued with the agrarian utopian ideology of the regime."
“They were raised in a framework where doing business is a foreign concept.”
The 55-year-old Sor Lim, who joined the Khmer Rouge as a teenager in 1974, fought in the jungle around Anlong Veng before settling in 1998 as a poor rice grower.
"Life under the Khmer Rouge was good," he said.
"Your Mok feeds everyone."
But now life is difficult because we have to support ourselves.”
The ongoing Khmer Rouge court has also caused problems here.
The court is expected, early next year, to begin the judgment of Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another Khmer Red Minister.
The court's mandate is to try senior leaders and the main perpetrators of the crimes, but he did not say whether he was continuing beyond these four cases.
We'd need more to calm Anlong Veng's fears.
The latest media speculation turned to Im Chaem, a 64-year-old former district chief in Banteay Meanchey province during the Khmer Rouge rule in the 1970s.
In 2007, she told researchers at the Cambodia Documentation Center that she had overseen the construction of the Trapeang Thma dam, a project where thousands of forced labourers reportedly died.
Recently, during a steamy evening, Im Chaem returned from work in the field located near his wooden house on stilts, on a dirt road outside Anlong Veng.
As the sun cast long shadows on the dry grass, Im Chaem refused to discuss his past within the Khmer Rouge.
If the court summoned her, she said, she wouldn't go.
"Cambodia is at peace and stable," she said.
"If there's more prosecution, there'll be war."
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who defected the Khmer Rouge in 1978, has also repeatedly warned that instability will occur if the court pursues several suspects; although experts and historians dispute this.
"Cambodo must dig a hole and bury the past," he said.
But facing the past is all that Cambodia has to do to move forward, says Youk Chhang, of the documentation center.
“Reconciliation in Khmer terms is to reconnect broken pieces,” he said.
"We have to put these pieces together so we can understand."
The center produced the first government-approved handbook on the Khmers Rouge, "A 75-page history of Democratic Kampuchea."
He distributed it to Anlong Veng in June as a supplement to the history textbooks of the Ministry of Education, designed for high schools, which contained less than four pages on the Khmer Rouge.
As in most of Cambodia, the young people of Anlong Veng know only a few details about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city’s links to the regime.
Touch Valeak, a 19-year-old student at Anlong Veng University, said the new textbook helps students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted with skepticism when he came back with the book at home to study.
“My family rejects many parts of the handbook and the court,” he said.
"They're suspicious because they don't know exactly how many people the court will pursue."
Such resistance has made the reconciliation process complex and difficult, Sok Leang said.
But public forums, the handbook, and the court are starting to make an impact, he said.
However, the Khmer Rouge retain a powerful appeal here.
In the heights of the Dangrek Mountains, a path, invaded by weeds and dotted with plastic bags, leads to a rectangle of black soot, covered with a rusty sheet metal roof.
Pol Pot’s body was burned here on a pile of tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, a 31-year-old mobile phone saleswoman, said she didn’t know much about the man who was cremated there.
"But he has a great spirit; and many locals go there to pray," she said.
It was Nuom Sothea's birthday, and later that day she planned to walk to Pol Pot's last resting place where she would leave a bunch of ripe bananas in the hope of bringing luck.
Belarus agrees to relinquish its stockpile of military-grade uranium
The government of Belarus announced Wednesday that it will abandon its stockpile of highly enriched uranium, an essential component for nuclear weapons; this decision is part of a surprise victory of President Obama’s campaign to secure nuclear materials around the world
The deal was leaked in a joint statement issued after Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton met here with Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov on the sidelines of a security summit.
The U.S. government had tried for years to convince Belarus to give up highly enriched uranium and appeared to have cleared the horizon last spring.
But U.S. officials told the Washington Post that some classified operations have been carried out with Belarus over the past two months to eliminate 187 pounds of military-grade uranium from a research center in Belarus.
This paved the way for an agreement on Wednesday to eliminate the rest of the matter by about 500 pounds, according to an official who raised the condition of anonymity because of the sensitivity of the issue.
The deal seems to be one of the most important outcomes of the 47-nation Nuclear Security Summit, held at Obama’s initiative; where Belarus was excluded.
Apparently, it was profitably providing an invitation to the former Soviet republic in the wake of the summit in 2012.
“I’m really fascinated by the fact that they want headquarters at the 2012 Nuclear Nuclear Nuclear Security Summit.”
“It proves to me that this process works,” said Ken Luongo, president of the Global Security Partnership, a nuclear security advocacy group.
Clinton called the deal “a very significant, important step” carried out by Belarus.
Obama pledged in Prague in April 2009 to secure all vulnerable nuclear material around the world over the next four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of all their highly enriched uranium.
Belarus would be seventh.
A dozen countries have done so under the administration of George W. Bush.
At the time of Obama’s Nuclear Security Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko said the nation would never give up its uranium.
"We have kept highly enriched uranium, hundreds of kilograms of what is essentially military-grade uranium and low-enriched uranium," Lukashenko said.
"It's our product ... We're not going to make dirty bombs, and we're not going to sell it to anyone."
"We're going to use it for research purposes, that's all."
Belarus approached the U.S. a few months ago; however, showing interest in reaching an agreement.
The first implementation to remove uranium from Belarus in October was particularly complicated because the equipment had been slightly irradiated, officials said.
It has been loaded on a special train that has traveled more than 1,300 miles to a facility in Russia to be stored and disposed of, officials said.
The second operation was completed on Monday and was carried out by air, officials said.
The U.S. government spent US$13 million in its efforts, largely to replace Belarusian’s research facilities with low-enriched uranium; unsuitable for weapons.
"Meticulous coordination and organization have been implemented in order to build confidence," leading to the agreement signed by Clinton, said Andrew Bieniawski, an head of the Department of Energy specializing in Global Threat Reduction.
According to Estimates of the James Martin Non-Proliferation Study Center, this year Belarus had at least 88 pounds of military-grade uranium, as well as hundreds of kilograms of high-enriched uranium.
Belarus seeks to dispose of its stocks by 2012, according to a joint statement.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been strained for years, mainly due to Lukashenko's harsh crackdown on human rights.
But Wednesday’s statement suggested potential renewal.
“While welcoming progress on these issues of global security, the United States and Belarus have recognized that increased respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains essential for improving bilateral relations; and indispensable for the progress of the country and its citizens,” she said.
The government has raised no objections to CIA flights
The government has raised no objections to CIA thefts with Taliban and Al Qaeda prisoners towards Guantánamo across Spanish territory, according to a telegram from the U.S. ambassador to Madrid.
This is demonstrated by Wikileaks’ filtered (cable) documents to the daily El País, which adds that the first vice-president of the government of that time, María Teresa Fernández de la Vega, had requested that the Spanish Executive be informed to “be able to demonstrate that they exercised adequate vigilance on the foreign planes crossing Spain.”
In June of 2006, after a report by the Council of Europe accusing complicity in practices contrary to human rights to a dozen countries, including Spain, took place a meeting between former North American Ambassador Eduardo Aguirre and Fernández de la Vega.
The Vice-President acknowledged that the Council's report "surprised Zapatero's Government offside" and insisted that Spain had nothing to hide.
"De la Vega pointed out that Spain has no objection with regard to intelligence theft over Spanish territory."
"They only want to be informed" to avoid being "taking them off guard," Aguirre said in a cable.
The holder of the External Ministry of that time, Miguel Ángel Moratinos, informed the ambassador that the Spanish Executive "wanted to give this case the lowest profile possible, even if, as it was in the hands of the judges, the Government had a limited capacity for influence".
In turn, in January 2007, prosecutor Vicente González forwarded to the Embassy the fact that he was not going to block the judge's initiative to request the declassification of documents from the National Center for Intelligence (CNI) and the Ministry of Defence on CIA flights.
"These reports do not contain any incriminating evidence, no sensitive information," the embassy said after speaking with the prosecutor.
The United States still showed concern for "appropriate coordination between the Spanish judge and German prosecutors" more than the declassified information of CNI o of the Ministry of Defense.
"This coordination between independent investigators will complicate our efforts to maneuver the case discreetly between the Governments," Aguirre said.
In March of 2007 during a lunch between the U.S. ambassador and the Minister of Internes, Alfredo Pérez Rubalcaba told him that "even if the Spanish judges are madly jealous of his independence and will do what they consider appropriate", he had given instructions to his collaborators "so that they avoid the comments of igniting rhetoric, trying to lower the temperature".
The Ambassador also expresses his concern for the PP's work in this matter.
“Despite its pro-U.S. general orientation, our experience suggests that PP certainly will take advantage of any indication that Zapatero’s Government has tolerated or deliberately ignored U.S. actions that could be interpreted as possible human rights violations, even if those revelations have negative consequences for the U.S. Government,” she said.
The parricide that passed to confessions complained in network of the "overwhelming" of being a mother
The girl was talking about the child when he was already dead and she had a blog: "Galician Terror"
Mónica Juanatey Fernández is "Muki" in the network.
