China Maintains Goals to Reduce Greenhouse Gases
The country’s representative in COP16 assured that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the trace of coal and has detailed that it maintains its offer to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions up to 2020 until 2020.
China maintains its offer to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions between 40 and 45 per cent before 2020, said Su Wei, the country’s representative at COP16.
In its first public appearance in the 16th Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP16), Su Wei assured that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the trace of coal and convert its industry.
In fact, he explained, the last four years his country has inverted US$245 million in efforts to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
Nevertheless, he made it clear that China will not change its objectives, but it will continue to work together with the international community in this area.
He mentioned that China's climate change strategy is divided into four areas: dialogue; capacity-building; institution-building and pilot programme support.
There has been progress in all of these areas, including last year's approval of a new Energy Law, which recognizes the objectives of reducing greenhouse gases, he said.
On the other hand, he stressed that in China, federal authorities have worked in a coordinated manner with governments and locals to achieve energy savings targets, efficient energy use and emission reductions.
In addition, he said, there are various multi-year plans underway or completed, both funded by the Chinese government as well as the governments of other countries.
For example, he mentioned, the Efficient Energy Utilization Programme, which has been implemented over the past five years and has received investments from the United Nations (United Nations), the government and the private initiative.
There is also a provincial climate change project, another for the change of incandescent lamps by thrifty lamps, and another for mitigation and adaptation, he said.
At the same table, Martin Krol, the country's government's scientific adviser on climate change, assured that China's efforts have been greater than expected.
For example, he explained, the per capita emission of greenhouse gases in China is three tonnes per person, while in the United States it is 100 tonnes; China is responsible for the 9% of the historical contribution in contamination.
Similarly, it has made unprecedented efforts in the use of energy, how to increase energy efficiency by 20% over the past five years and plans to turn it into another similar towards 2020, he said.
Espino Legal Expulsion: César Nava
The national leader of PAN detailed that the former panistic leader has the right to address the Commission of National Order to claim what right.
The national leader of PAN, César Nava Vázquez assured that the expulsion of Manuel Espino Barrientos was legal and that the former panist leader has the right to apply to the Commission of National Order to claim what right.
In the interview prior to Gabino Cué Monteagudo’s protest, he said that in any case, Espino’s eviction is a decision adopted at the local level.
"This is an autonomous decision by the Sonora Order Commission."
"Mr. Espino has the right to address herself, if necessary, by making a complaint to the Commission d'Order of the National Council, so that it is a decision taken within a competent body," he said.
On the other hand, he enlightened that this is not the time to think about 2012 and there has never been on the negotiating table an alliance for the presidential candidacy.
Nava Vázquez was convinced that Acción Nacional would be stronger after the internal elections of the next 4 and 5 December where a successor should be elected.
"This (an alliance in 2012) is an aspect that has never been on the negotiating table, at least for now, it has been an approach for local elections that has been successful and has achieved its goal," he said.
The panistic leader mentioned that PAN is backed by 71 years of dialogue, which is why "internal competition doesn't scare us; on the contrary "we savor it and make us stronger," he said.
He said that probably the council will elegirate the best leader Acción Nacional needs for the following three years, so he would give the note "to the person determined by the council."
“I’m completely quiet and open, I’ll be proud to leave the party president’s office to any of the five.”
He predicted that the relaunch recorded by the party in the past elections of July 4 will be reflected on the next dispute, even if "this is not yet the time to think about 2012."
"For the moment it's about accompanying Governor Gabino Cué in this task."
In this context, he stressed that the three governments emanating from alliances in Oaxaca, Puebla and Sinaloa will be the flag and will be a reason of observation throughout the country, so that there have been "many hopes for them, and I am sure that Gabino will live up to expectations".
When asked about PRI's criticism against President Felipe Calderón because of his speech during his four years of government, César Nava asked priists to reflect on the six years of former presidents Ernesto Zedillo and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
"If the PRI thinks the governments of Zedillo y Salinas have been better than Fox y Calderón; that they are beginning to defend them; that they're coming out of the 'club," he said.
He stated that the holder of the Federal Executive can make expressions such as those he made in his speech in the National Auditory, because in the country there is no gag, much less censorship.
Bamba promotes the law in Twitter for undocumented students
"If you agree with the approval of the Dream Act, call you senators and ask them to approve this legislation," he tweeted in the popular social network.
President Barack Obama met today at the National Mobilization to support the Dream Act, to legalize undocumented students, with a message in his personal account of the social network Twitter.
“If you agree with the approval of the Dream Act, call you senators and ask them to approve this legislation,” Obama said.
His short message keeps open a cybernetic link with a broader document that asks the public to immediately contact senators, as voting on legislation is imminent.
"With a vote we're hoping soon, now is the time to listen to your voice," he said.
The message was leaked after the 42 Republican senators threatened to block the vote of any legislation if the extension of Bush-era tax cuts and government funding continue.
Such a blocking, announced in a letter signed by the 42 Republican senators, could affect the passage of the Dream Act and other projects such as the ratification of the new START treaty with Russia against the proliferation of nuclear weapons.
Asked about the Republican blocking of the Dream Act and other projects, presidential spokesman Robert Gibbs said he is confidant that once the tax problem is resolved we will be able to set an agent for the rest of the projects.
"I think we've got a lot of time..."
"We all agree that we must solve this tax problem before the end of the year..."
“I think we can get a deal at some point in this direction and then we will be able to plan the sequence of the rest,” he said.
Senate Democratic Majority Leader Harry Reid announced on Tuesday that he was going to call for a "closing vote of debate" for the Dream Act, which is why the initiative to legalize undocumented students could be passed starting next Thursday in the Senate, even though the most likely would be next week.
The senatorial version of the Dream Act or the U.S. Dream Act would legalize 800 thousand young people who arrived in the United States before the age of 16, they have remained in that country for the last five years and they have been accepted by an institution of higher education or by the Army.
But supporters require 60 votes to approve the initiative, which is why they require the assistance of a large number of Republicans, at times when the partisan organizations of the project protagonists intense lobbying to guarantee the approval of the Law.
The Democrats, the majority of whom support the Dream Act, have 58 places.
Nevertheless, at least one of them, Senator Ben Nelson of Nebraska, said he would vote against and only a Republican expressed his support for the law, Richard Lugar of Indiana.
That’s why supporters of the Dream Act need the vote of several Republicans if other Democrats decide to vote against the initiative.
Among the most courted Republicans are Senators Maine, Olympia Snowe and Susan Collins, and outgoing Senator George Lemieus of Florida, who completed Senator Mel Martínez.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch of Utah, and Kay Bailey Hutchinson of Texas, but they said they weren’t going to support him on another occasion.
Another Republican who supported him, John McCain, hasn’t defined his posture so far.
Even though the House of Representatives has a different version, analysts agree that the larger obstacles in the Dream Act are in the Senate.
With the literature we put our finger on the wound
The second stage of the Pleasures of the Language was a walk in which the participants displayed the narration
"Sex in the tongue, I understand, but... the finger on the wound? what do we expect of me?" asked writer Luisa Valenzuela invited to open the second stage of the Pleasures of the language, a cycle that celebrates literature in Spanish and this time used the subtitle of his question (during another fair the round table was dedicated to Sex in the language).
The finger on the wound "...puts us embarrassed", gently launched the Argentine writer to the public, most of the young people, who had gathered at the International Book Fair of Guadalajara (FIL) to listen to this table moderated by Marisol Shultz and in which Valenzuela, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Montero and Luis García Montero participated.
Luisa Valenzuela read a text dedicated to Gonzalo Celorio that Shultz defined after as a great story, with Luisa in the first person as the protagonist who seeks to know what is the "play", she goes from one doctor to another, without getting an answer, only leads in the same narrative thread.
From the infectiologist she emerges convinced: "patience is not my forte, it may be my wound."
In the end, after a frustrated approach with the traumatologist who took his breath away in the middle of the hallway, but who explains to him that wound is not a scientific term; it is the psychoanalyst reveals the mystery: "the wound is what is open in reality, symbolism is the scar."
The protagonist sighed relieved and concluded: "On the wound I was only able to know that it is open in reality and that it does not exist", but convinced of the fact that the pleasures and traps of the language must be very linked between them.
Valenzuela, the writer, played with both.
The Nicaraguan Sergio Ramírez has also exhibited a delightful narrative that he has entitled Élogue à l'invention, where the finger on the wound is not numbered but at some point is discovered.
With touches of magical realism, he speaks of a famous bewitcher who touched the clouds, made people who had gone away or repaired the virtues that a damoiselle had lost, a narrative that turns to speak America afterwards, the great news that had from the beginning its own chroniclers and quoted Bernal Díaz del Castillo who criticizes the excess of the narratives
“Cervants who invented us all [writers] mocked the false witnesses who appeared in the stories presented as real, but in another way we would never have told the history of America with an imaginary that even lacked modesty.”
Nevertheless, he stressed, "crazies and liars have the best imagination" and here reality converges with falsehood, we no longer know what part of the narration belongs to one band or another, where fictional literature reaches its peak.
This is actually referred to as ‘Cervantes tradition’.
"It's because of his legacy that we're what we're talking about."
In turn, writer Mayra Montyero annotated and applauded the variety of interpretations provoked by the invitation of the FIL with his round table The Dute on the Sorrow.
The Puerto Rican-Cuban has put hers on the great plague of social violence that her country is going through - and Latin America shares - produces disputes about drug trafficking and the sale of drugs.
"I think that good literature, good poetry the good songs contain a tacit commitment that is not of course premeditated," she said, after quoting that the reality of literature is communication and the dimension of commitment that is established between the author and readers, and that it is a "chemical reaction" of the latter.
The evening is finished with the intervention of the Spanish poet Luis García Montero who also stressed "it must on the wound of the committed writer".
He considered that in a world that lives in high speed also with regard to intellectual habits and the control of consciences that turns to the advantage of dogmatism, "behind each affirmation there is a small no and behind each negation there is a little yes".
"To be the masters of our own feelings and be the masters of reality, we have to nuance: and nuancer means many times putting their fingers on the wound and disturbing people who feel very convenient in thinking about caricature, white and black, established ideas and without nuance and also disturbing the powers that prefer a world in white and black," he said
Romano hunting his first title
Rubén Omar Romano had three defeats in finals he had ups and downs in his career, but he managed to reach the decisive series three times.
With the duel of Santos Laguna and Monterrey in the finale, there are also two different styles that confront each other, marked by their technicians, who find between them a contrast marked by the results.
Rubén Omar Romano had three defeats in finals, he had ups and downs in his career, but he was able to reach the decisive series three times, even if so far he has not put in the mile and that's why this occasion is very special for him.
The Argentinian ended up comforting himself with the medal of sub-champion, he remained a step behind the winners and curiously with the same team he played consecutive finals.
The Santist strategist arrived at his first final as a coach in the 2002 Opening Tournament, when he held the reins of Morelia with whom he lost with an overall result of 4-2 against Toluca; later, in the Closure 2003 he also arrived at the decisive duel and lost again with the overall result of 3-1 and this was precisely against Rayados.
With Santos Laguna already, in the 2010 Bicentennial Tournament, again against the Diablos, Rubén suffered a loss again, as his team made an equal match of 2-2 against the Choriceros after 210 minutes of play, but in the series of penalties he got an advantage for 3-1 with two opportunities to get the title and finally three of his players missed 11 meters and he suffered the jump in the result of 4-3.
Rubén has lost two finals with the Diablos Rojos and one with Monterrey, his rival at the tournament, so he doesn't want to have the same fate, he's trying to detach himself from the evil to be able to celebrate a title for the first time, even though he claims that for him the institution is more important.
"I want to be champion, I don't want to break any statistics, it's for a club, not for a passion I have and therefore I'll get the title, but what I mentioned before is more important," he said when asked what his weaknesses would be in the finals.
He remembers a lot about the last final, from which he assures that he has learned a lot, "the only lesson that we can learn is that until the referee whistles the final, we can't celebrate, we're prepared in the line, the players too, because the last time, with two penalties for shooting we were already prepared to run and I believe this is a lesson before the end of the game we can't
He knows that this series could be defined by penalties, which does not worry him, because he confides in his players: "We are never trying to get to the series of penalties, we try to finish within the regular deadline, but sometimes we only have to prepare, the previous tournament we have been prepared, unfortunately the destiny has made us a bad trick, sometimes it's the emotions or the anguish to hold the title in our hands
Romano now has the opportunity to no longer be an underchampion technician and taste for the first time the glory offered by the titles.
The European Union is changing the rules for obtaining a visa
Individuals must go to the Visa Seeker Service Centres prior to their interview in the Consular Section; the cost of waiting time will be reduced.
As of January 10, 2011 the visa processing procedure at the U.S. Embassion and Consulates will change.
With the new procedure, the majority of applicants will have to report to Visa Seekers Service Centres (VSAs) prior to their interview in the Consular Section.
CSA staff will take biometric information from the applicant, which will be reviewed by the Consular Section prior to the interview.
The new procedure will have certain advantages for aspirants, including the following:
The total cost to process the visa will be lower, it will no longer be necessary to make a payment to obtain an appointment, another payment for the processing of the visa and another for the mail service to deliver the document.
The aspirant will make a single payment that will cover all three services.
The current tariff will remain the same: the equivalent of US$140 for a tourist visa, US$150 in the case of visas with application (including visas for temporary workers) and US$390 for merchant and investor visas.
Individuals who wish to renew their visa will not have to attend an interview with the Consular Representative, they will only present themselves to the nearest CSA where they will present the documents and take their fingerprints.
Due to the fact that biometric information will be included in the CSA, applicants who want a second appointment to be interviewed in the Consular Section will remain less time than before.
Similarly, visa applicants at the Consulates of Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer pay the extra service rate of US$26.
For further information on the new procedure to apply for the visa, visit the following page http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201_Visas-FAQs.html
NASA to Announce Extraterrestrial Life Discovery
The U.S. Space Agency quoted the press to present tomorrow a discovery that will have an impact on the search for living things in space.
The U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) will release an astrobiological discovery tomorrow Thursday, which could prove the theory of the existence of extraterrestrial life.
The discovery will be presented in a press conference tomorrow Thursday, at 13:00 a.m. at NASA headquarters in Washington for experts in astrobiology, the science that studies the origin, evolution, distribution and future of life in the Universe.
When she cited the press, NASA said it would present who will have an impact on the search for extraterrestrial life, which has sparked speculation from experts from other countries.
The discovery could demonstrate the theory that there are creatures similar to us and in hostiolous atmospheres that were thought to be uninhabited and put on the table the possibility of becoming intelligent beings like humans if their living conditions are improved.
It seems that we are talking about a microbe discovered at the bottom of the Mono Lake in Yosemite National Park, California, which lives in an atmosphere that was thought too venomous for any form of life, because the lake is rich in arsenics.
Of all forms, the creature uses arsenics as a way of living and this ability makes us think that a similar life could exist on other planets that do not have our volunteer atmosphere.
According to astrobiologists, if these organisms use arsenic in their metabolism, it proves that "there is another form of life, a second form of life, different from that we know."
The space agency will communicate the true scope of the findings in tomorrow's press conference on Thursday.
At the conference will participate in the geobiology Pamela Conrad, which studies the possibility of life on Mars; and biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists who studies Titan (Saturn's largest satellite), from the perspective that its chemistry is similar to that of Earth.
Germe PRD, PT, Convergencia and Patrón 'bloc' opponent
With reforms to the Organic Law of the Congress, we make possible the arrival of 'bipartisanship', we denounce.
The 60th had just settled when in the Congress appeared an opposing block led by deputies of PRD, PT, Convergencia and Jesús Manuel Patrón Montalvo, who declared himself independent and the four questioned one thing: the arrival of 'bipartisanship' in the Chambers.
Before initiating the installation session of the 60th Legislature, Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez of Convergencia; Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of PT; Javier Corvera Quevedo of PRD; and Patrón Montalvo 'thundered' against the reform of the Organic Law
With this reformation, they have reiterated, 'bipartisanship' is initiated because now there are two people, of the same number of parties, who control Congress.
These four deputies, alongside Gloria Margarita Santos Aguilar, coordinator of PRD, were the only to vote against the Executive Board chaired by PANiste Salvador López Brito and Luis Antonio Cárdenas Fonseca, as Vice-President.
Corvera Quevedo mentioned that with these reforms in an institutional way the political marginality on the part of PRI and PAN is constituted.
He mentioned that in the following days they would speak to the representatives of PAN and PRI to change the pattern starting from a political will and if they do not, they will again be enslaved by both parties and in this way will end the opposing coalition.
"So far has arrived (the opposing coalition), that's what I'm looking at, because we're excluded, from any decision, they don't even have the political deference to consider the opinion of the rest."
Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez, de Convergencia mentioned that with PRD, PT and Patrón there are coincidences and that's why they form a plural group.
It cannot be permissible, he continued, that Congress, after having been monopolistic, is now "duopolistic".
“It’s not the spirit of reforming the law that there’s an eternal bipartisanship in Congress.”
To begin with, this group starts with 5 legislators and they are now talking to other colleagues so that they are more numerous, the Convergent has abounded.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of PT explained that now the Congress has turned into a 'loot' between two, referring to PAN and PRI.
“Before there was only one party, now they divide it between two.”
Patrón Montalvo asked to respect the personality of the deputy, to give him his place at the negotiating tables.
"That they do not leave by cupular agreements outside to 5 which will probably be 9 deputies, now to the five that we are seen, separated in different fractions, we have decided to unite to look for a place in the negotiating table, this is the problem, that in the reform they have done, that they have decided, we are outside and we do not agree with this reform."
$200 million more for "the supplier"
Mazatlán's Town Hall pays another cheque to the questioned owner of the electrical equipment company.
Another cheque of about 200 miles pesos favors Lorena Guadalupe Camacho Palazuelos was issued by the Town Hall.
With it, the amount paid this second semester to the owner of the electrical equipment company amounts to 1 million 200 miles pesos.
According to Municipe's transparency page, this last cheque number 9258447 was issued the past 16 November for the amount of 200 mil 144.56 pesos, in the concept of "account liquidation providers".
Treasurer Luis Miguel López Moreno had said that the Commune would pay other amounts to this supplier, which was observed by the State's Senior Audit to sell at a higher price.
What has not been reported is the total debt and, therefore, how many cheques will still be issued to Camacho Palazuelos.
"We didn't buy anything, we're simply paying him what we owe him, like the rest of the suppliers," he said in an interview.
The last cheques "reappeared" in the last quarter of the year: September 24, two on October 15, October 19, October 29 and the latter on November 16.
Although the supplier has not yet published in the Official Journal of the Crown, according to the provisions of the Act when a company is questioned by ASE, to prevent other governments from using its services, the Treasurer has assured that they have not used its services again.
All this, after July 25, Noroeste had published a revision of the cheques that the Town Hall issues to its suppliers, revealing that in the meantime of March 2008 - July 2010, in the triennial of Mayor Jorge Abel López Sánchez, the Municipe to pay Camacho Palazuelos more than 10 million pesos, through 42 checks.
He also carried out transactions with Jumapam, cashing cheques of more than 3 million pesos.
The tourist sites must be cared for
Tourist entrepreneurs regret the accident of the foreign tourist because of the poor conditions of the S�ánchez Taboada roundabout in Mazatlán.
After a foreign tourist fell in a trough at Sánchez Taboada roundabout, Mazatlán's tourism sector asked the authorities to pay attention to areas and sites that are in poor conditions and project a bad image of the port.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, president of the Zona Dorada Merchants Association, recalled that they have repeatedly asked that the image of the destination be neat and new activities must replace others that tourists find bad.
He said that the poor state of the streets and sidewalks has affected because tourists come there to walk and they see disorder and avoid nearby shops.
“Politicians don’t know what it means to close a business one day and they don’t know the fortune you’re losing, elsewhere they don’t do the work for so long and without proper planning.”
Hotelier José Ramón Manguart Sánchez said that the fall of the tourist exposed the lack of prevention and maintenance by the Town Hall.
"The municipe will have to assume its responsibility and prevent it from passing again, how to do it, so through preventive maintenance and only make the fix," he said.
"We want to enjoy a clean city, because not only because it is a tourist area must be maintained, but as inhabitants who deserve a clean place to live there and the Town Hall must change its strategy to properly maintain the city."
There are other sites that are neglected and incomplete, he said, such as Escudo and Venadito de Mazatlán.
"Today, because the tourist comes, it only depends on the promotion, there must be a good product, a city that has cleanliness in its beaches, boulevards, permanent maintenance of tourist attractions," he said.
"We didn't give them the attention they need and this type of facts underlines something that's notorious for all, maintenance is necessary."
Raúl Llera Martínez, sub-secrietor of Tourism Promotion, said that the recovery of Mazatlán tourist sites is urgent, as they are not used properly and project a bad image.
He spoke of a series of places that have to put themselves online together with the municipe and be a priority to have the city up-to-date, clean, ordained and attractive because it has a tourist vocation.
"Fortunately Mazatlán offers a lot of historical and cultural sites and places that we should first order, clean and put us online to make them attractive to be able to add to the inventory that we have to promote this destination."
IP requests results from Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, President of CCE, proposed that MEPs assume the great responsibility of being an example for a better Sinaloa because of the various provocations confronted.
Mazatlán's business sector calls for a dignified and agreement-free working legislation between the parties and the Government Executive, as Sinaloa is convulsed and faces major problems.
Yesterday we installed the 60th legislature formed by seven political forces, 19 deputies of PRI, 13 of PAN, three of the New Alliance Party, two of PRD, one from PT, one from Convergencia and one from PVEM, in total 40 popular representatives.
The list of expectations that the new officials of the Governmental Congress is a clean and committed management with society and the different sectors that need responsible legislative work and that the same models of party spirit or paternalism do not repeat themselves.
Eduardo Dávalos, President of CCE, proposed that MEPs assume the great responsibility of being an example for a better Sinaloa because of the various provocations confronted.
"We hope that MPs will really exercise their rights, free of agreements between the parties because the custom is that they make pacts to look after their own interests and they turn into a carnival masquerade of the rulers and leaders of parliamentary factions."
That's why I'm asking that they certify the commitment to establish the necessary bridges that the current governess is advancing.
“We’re going to ensure that they’re doing a dignified job that’s, above all, committed with society and the sectors because the people of Sinaloa have given them their vote of confidence in past elections.
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, president of the Tres Islas Hotel Association, considered that one of the great provocations of the 40 popular representatives is that of pushing and adding forces by seeking the economic growth of the state, because now the economic situation is very complicated.
“The most important thing is that they drive economic growth, that they generate incentives for investment and infrastructure, and that in a way will stimulate the generation of jobs.”
With regard to tourism activity, he called for new legislators to give it the importance it has in the government budget to consolidate the Secretariat of Government Tourism.
"I understood that it is the second Secretariat of the State with a smaller budget and it is incongruous as an important economic activity and generating foreign exchange and investment and which competes directly with agribusiness."
Apart from the need to classify resources to detonating tourism projects that help give security to the Crown and update the Alcohol Act that previous legislatures have not been able to resolve.
We're looking for King Mage
Dolls, cars and bicycles, among other toys, are the wishes of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family.
For their parents, the next day of the Wides will be difficult to accomplish their illusions, because their home is formed by neither more nor less than 12 people and a goat.
However, Adelaida, Isabel, Jesús, José Alfredo, María Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not lose the hope that Melchior, Balthazar and Gaspard will wear them the toys and clothes they desire so much.
The family lives in a humble home of Colonia Salvador Allende and they live day to day because their father does not have a stable job.
"Sometimes it's very difficult for us, because my husband doesn't have a job, but we sell tangerines and potatoes...and in the afternoon I go out with the 'plebes' for sale in the street," says Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, the mother of children.
Adelaida, 4 years old, wants a doll or a bicycle, and her sister Isabel, 3 years old, would want a Barbie doll.
In turn, 10-year-old José Alfredo has long aspired to have a 'monster truck' and for his 8-month-old younger brother Jesús, Cayetana suggests waist-cut clothes for a year.
"He told me (José Alfredo), 'I'd like to have this monster,' but I told him 'we'll see, my son,' because he was very dear," Cayetana recalls.
For older children, who were at school, clothes and shoes would be ideal.
María Guadalupe, 11 years old, puts on size 12; Isaías, of 10 years old, puts on the size 10, while Miguel, 8 years old, puts on the waist eight.
José Juan, 14, needs a pair of sports shoes, so a peer of size five-and-a-half or six would help him a lot.
If you want to help to make reality the dream of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family you can participate in the On Seeking King Mage campaign, by giving a toy or money at the reception of Noroeste.
14 suspected drug traffickers arrested
A dozen searches were carried out in Estrie on Wednesday as part of the dismantling of a network of production and sale of cannabis.
Fourteen people have been arrested and will face various charges.
The searches took place in Sherbrooke, Orford and Stoke, mostly in places allegedly used for drug production.
"Approximately 750 cannabis plants, four kilos of hashish, money and equipment used for production have been seized," said Louis-Philippe Ruel, spokesman for the Sûreté du Québec.
In all, eleven men and three women were arrested, including the two alleged leaders of this network, two men from Sherbrooke.
Those arrested were scheduled to appear at the Sherbrooke courthouse within hours of their arrest.
They could face charges of producing cannabis, possessing them in order to traffic it, conspiracy and the theft of services, among others.
These searches took place following a long-term investigation, which lasted nearly a year.
"It is information received from the public that allowed us to make these arrests," Ruel said.
Some 70 police officers of the regional investigation module of the Estrie de la Sûreté du Québec, the Sherbrooke Police Service and the Memphrémagog Police Board took part in this police operation.
Illegal migration can continue without problems
The Conservative bill, which, according to the government, is expected to solve the problem of boats loaded with illegal migrants, will obviously be defeated while the three opposition parties said on Wednesday that they could not support it.
"I rely heavily on the Charter."
"I'm standing with a party that created the granite under your feet," Liberal leader Michael Ignatieff said as he was surrounded by MPs after the caucus.
The Liberals claim that the bill as proposed, which would make it possible to detain for up to one year anyone from a ship identified as carrying illegal immigrants, would violate the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.
The leader of the Bloc Québécois, Gilles Duceppe, also indicated that he could not support the bill, because he attacks the wrong target.
"We're against it and it seems that all opposition parties are against, so we'll see what the government does," Duceppe said.
NDP Immigration Critics Olivia Chow has dismissed criticism that her party is not firm enough regarding illegal immigrants.
"The New Democrats agree to take action against smugglers and that's what we're doing through another bill, the C-35," Chow said.
"We don't support the Conservative bill because it attacks refugees and imprisons children for at least one year," Chow said.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney has accused opposition parties of playing politics and ignoring the will of Canadians.
"Canadians overwhelmingly tell MPs that they don't tolerate this kind of clandestine migration, these criminal operations that target Canada and treat the country like a doormat."
"They expect Canada to take firm action," Kenney said.
The government did not announce a vote of confidence on its bill, which could have led the country to elections in the event of its rejection.
However, one Conservative told QMI that the government was going to ask for a vote so that eacher MP who opposes then explains himself before his constituents.
The new unity is not unanimous
Despite the announcement of the possible creation of a permanent unit to fight corruption and collusion by Jean Charest, many observers continue to call for the creation of a public commission of inquiry on the subject.
The Minister of Public Safety, Robert Dutil, argues that this idea does not date from yesterday: Quebec City would have consulted with the Department of Investigation in New York on this subject in January.
The metropolis has a permanent unit over corruption since 1870.
Tuesday’s announcement surprised more than one.
It must be said that during a meeting of the Permanent Council on Police Services of Quebec, on October 29, this measure was never discussed despite the presence of Robert Dutil.
It did not take more so that the PQ critic in terms of public security, Stéphane Bergeron, calls this announcement a "bunny out of a hat" and a "tinkered solution on the corner of a table".
Minister Dutil, meanwhile, responded to those who fear that political power would have too much influence on the possible future unity.
"You're going to see the result when we're going to announce it," he said.
"We're aware of that problem."
For the president of the CSQ, Réjean Parent, nothing can replace the establishment of a public inquiry.
"The more Mr. Charest persists in not wanting to create this commission of inquiry, the more people think like you and me that he has something to hide," he said.
"The only way to have a commission of inquiry is the resignation of Jean Charest," explains Yves Boisvert, professor of ethics and governance at the National School of Public Administration.
To break this impasse, he therefore suggests that Quebec give a special mandate to the Auditor General to investigate collusion and corruption.
"We must be able to expand the mandate of the Auditor General," he said.
He also believes that Quebec City could give the auditor the same powers as a commissioner, thus allowing him to compel people to testify.
The creation of a permanent squad is not the only project in the cards of the Charest government.
Quebec City would also ask Ottawa to amend the Criminal Code in order to be better adapted to this type of crime.
This request would emanate from the public prosecutor of Operation Marteau, who informed the Minister of Justice Jean-Marc Fournier that the jurisprudence is not explicit in such investigations.
Federal Public Safety Minister Vic Toews confirmed that his Justice colleague Rob Nicholson is currently considering some amendments related to this issue.
During Operation Spring 2001, which struck the groups of bikers, even if the Criminal Code was not adapted, 131 people were arrested.
Ottawa subsequently passed the Gangsterism Act.
Rivers under high surveillance
Several streams remain under high surveillance due to heavy rainfall in several parts of the province since Tuesday.
A landslide occurred on Wednesday afternoon in Shawinigan, Mauricie.
Two houses were evacuated pre-emptively.
In the evening, another slide would have occurred in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, Lanaudière.
A road collapsed.
From Tuesday to Wednesday evening, heavy rainfall fell on several regions, from 40 to 70 millimeters of rain.
An additional 20 to 40 millimeters of rain was expected by Thursday evening, according to the regions.
Areas north of the river and in the Outaouais valley were the most affected.
They could receive up to 100 millimeters of rain in total.
Since it is necessary to add this precipitation to the melting snow already accumulated in some areas, there are fears of the overflowing of several watercourses.
Flood warnings were issued Wednesday evening by the Civil Security of Quebec for the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in Lanaudière.
The rivers of the North, in Saint-Jérôme, in the Laurentians, and the Chêne, in Lotbinière, were the subject of flood watches and remained under surveillance.
In the Lanaudière region, heavy rainfall has led to the overflowing of some lakes in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare and Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, a temporary bridge was washed away due to heavy rains.
Ten residences had to be evacuated as a preventive measure.
In the Outaouais, the overflowing of some lakes resulted in the closure of several roads, notably in the municipality of Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
The Civil Security remains on alert and monitors the situation closely, said Annik Bouchard, Security Advisor.
For its part, the Quebec Flood Prediction Centre issued warnings of heavy floods for several rivers, including Maskinongé, L'Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and du Loup.
Dupont partially blamed
Quebec radio host Stéphane Dupont has suffered a partial setback before the Canadian Broadcasting Standards Council (CBSC) for controversial remarks on social assistance and Haitians as well as his coarse on-air language.
The CCNR considers that Mr. Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team had the right to express their opinions, but that some comments contained "inaccurate information on social assistance, unduly negative representations of Haitians and coarse language that is not suitable for the programs broadcast during the day".
The Commission also concluded that a discussion on suicide, including the dissemination of the names of two people who committed suicide, did not violate broadcasting codes.
The CBSC had received a number of complaints about comments made during programs broadcast between 2008 and 2010.
The first decision was rendered following a complaint by the Common Front of Socially Assisted Persons of Quebec.
The group complained that the amounts quoted in the issue were inaccurate.
Facilitators need to provide sound rather than misleading information.
Regarding suicide, the host Stéphane Dupont had felt that suicide is a gesture of coward.
The majority of the members of the Committee concluded that the message conveyed was that suicide should not be seen as a solution to life's problems.
"The majority of the Committee considers that the implicated episodes of "Dupont le midi" have addressed a sensitive issue in a fair and appropriate manner."
So no blame has been held against him.
The last dispute was about statements that followed the earthquake in Haiti.
Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort had then engaged in a face-to-face media that had provoked a lot of reactions.
CHOI's facilitator said he didn't donate to help Haitians because we couldn't know for sure that needy people would really receive the money.
The Committee believes that these comments were not abusive, but that their cumulative effect constitutes an unduly negative representation of Haitians.
“I’ve already served my three-day suspension on it.”
“My file has been blank for a year and a half,” the host said.
In all decisions, the Committee denounces the dissemination of coarse language.
In each case, broadcaster CHOI-FM collaborated "beyond its obligation".
The consequences are minimal for the station, which must, among other things, announce these decisions once during prime time within three days.
A king of kung-fu in Montreal
The Montrealer of Algerian origin Nacereddine Zemmal has an extremely special background.
The athlete who excels in "sanda" (kung-fu "full contact"), kickboxing and Thai boxing rides on the tatamis and rings of the world in search of new challenges.
"I started practicing martial arts such as kung-fu and kickboxing 12 years ago in Algeria," said Zemmal, who has been living in the metropolis since 2008 to evolve among professionals.
"In my case, I'm looking for fights everywhere, since for those who don't like the ground game present in mixed martial arts, competitions are rare in North America."
Recently, he who works as a security officer and as a clerk for the UPS company delivered bad news to his rivals, winning two gold medals at the World Karate and Kickboxing Association (WKA) World Amateur World Championship (WKA) from October 23 to 30, in Edinburgh, Scotland.
"I've won full-contact kickboxing and Thai boxing categories," he said.
"Currently, I'm getting ready to go to China for the San Shou World Cup."
"Fighters who finished among the top four at the last world championship in each category were invited."
"I won bronze in 2009 in Toronto."
"In 2005, in Vietnam, I finished eighth."
“In 2007, in China, I finished the tournament fifth, when I came third in Toronto in 2009, he added.”
"What's interesting is that I've progressed in every competition."
"At this rate I'll be first this time or next year!"
The "san shou" or "sanda" is a discipline originating from China where punches and kicks, as well as projections on the ground, are allowed.
Duels take place originally on a platform and more rarely in a ring.
The most renowned pugilist from this form of combat is the Vietnamese-American Cung Le, well-renowned in the world of martial arts.
If Nacer Zemmal only has to remember one event in his career, he will certainly respond to his presence at the "King of Sanda", the Kung-fu Super Bowl.
"This competition is the strongest and most prestigious in China," explained the sympathetic guy.
"That's kind of the equivalent of Lumpinee Stadium (the biggest stadium in Thailand)."
"It's been a great experience."
Zemmal won his first fight in the semi-finals of his category (80 kg), before losing in the final.
"Whoever defeated me was a real pro," he analysed.
"There are four fighters per category (80 kg, 85 kg, 90 kg and 90 kg)."
"The winners of the semi-finals compete in the finals of their category."
"Then the winners of each category face each other in order to crown a single champion, the king of kung-fu."
450 million in non-tendering contracts
Hydro-Quebec has awarded 450 million dollars in contracts to engineering firms for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydroelectric project.
At the heart of this avalanche of more than 100 contracts, it is a consortium made up of BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor that obtained the most important, that of the management of the project, for a sum of 295 million dollars without tenders.
This consortium called Stewardship was established in 2004 concurrently with Hydro-Québec's choice to entrust the private sector with its responsibility to manage this important 5 billion-dollar hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005, the Energy Stewardship consortium won the lucrative contract without a public tender.
For the Parti Québécois, the use of consortias of companies poses a serious ethical problem that risks eliminating competition and causing higher prices.
"It's the new way of collusioning and breaking down the competition."
"That is to say that people, instead of doing this in a secret way, now do it in an open way," PQ MP Sylvain Simard told reporters yesterday.
Earlier in the morning, Mr. Simard raised the issue in the National Assembly because he said it was difficult to see how the interest of the public could be served in this way.
The Minister of Natural Resources, Nathalie Normandeau, replied in the afternoon, vigorously denying that there was collusion for this contract.
"I'm starting to have my journey," she said, arguing that the PQ had been "dishonest" and had "failed in rigour."
She said that "it'd be the leasest thing" that the PQ apologises.
Consortia formula is a common practice around the world.
It is usually a time-limited alliance that allows two or more companies to consolidate their respective expertise in order to respond to a tender for a complex project.
The Eastmain project has a high level of complexity given the scale of the work.
The project is ongoing and is expected until 2012.
However, the contract was awarded without the competition acting; there was negotiation.
Also, the consortial formula is no longer an exception in Quebec, even for relatively simple projects.
As revealed last week Le Devoir, the formula of consortials in the consulting engineering sector is being investigated by the government.
A quick overview of Quebec's corporate register allows more than 200 consortia, some of which appear to be permanent.
For example, Dessau works in partnership with most of its main competitors, including CIMA+, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin and others Roche and Tecsult.
Each combination, and there are dozens, leads back to one and the same legal person, Dessau.
With regard to the Energy stewardship consortium, Minister Normandeau pointed out that "the territory of James Bay responds to particular workforce dynamics and that Hydro-Quebec has responded in a particular way since the 70's".
The great boss of the Eastmain project, Réal Laporte of the James Bay Energy Corporation (JBES), gave an explanation that raised eyebrows on the fact that the stewardship contract for the project was granted over-the-counter.
"If you go to tender and there are few bidders, your prices are going to be higher."
"You have two choices: whether you're taking the people that's in that deal and you're saying: we're going to negotiate a satisfying deal with a good price."
"But the idea is that it's not the price that's important, it's people's skills," Laporte said.
This contract awarded to the Energy Management consortium is part of a long list of contracts of more than $50 000 obtained by the Parti Québécois.
The contracts have been awarded by Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
After verification, Le Devoir had 109 consulting engineering contracts awarded between 2002 and March 2010 to firms or their subsidiaries, including soil laboratories.
SNC-Lavalin won 22 contracts, Génivar obtained 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, not to mention its participation in Energy Stewardship.
All contracts have been negotiated.
They total $449 783,344, including the $295 131,13564 stewardship contract.
Sylvain Simard suggested that the four firms that make up the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) are "well known to the Quebec Liberal Party".
Minister Normandeau dropped that the PQ is also linked to these firms who "fund it", before specifying that they were rather its leaders.
Responsible consumption barometer - The "green" label does not inspire confidence
Greener, but frankly skeptical.
Quebec consumers have very little confidence in sustainable development players, but also fair brands and environmental certifications to guide their choices.
Worse, even if he bathes in a media universe where the messages in favor of a more responsible consumption swarm, he deplores a credible lack of information, a deficiency that he even qualifies as a brake on his commitment, says the Barometer of responsible consumption, whose first reading was delivered yesterday.
Even if they increased the consumption of so-called socially responsible products in 2010, Quebecers seem to give very little credit to those trying to sell them.
Two-thirds of them say they don't believe the environmental commitment of companies as well as the green advertising of companies, reveals the measurement tool developed by the Observatory of responsible consumption of Sherbrooke University.
Another observation: half of the consumers doubt the reliability of the official certifications affixed to the so-called fair trade products, but also of the credibility of the products and brands displaying themselves as eco-responsible, says the Barometer.
In the same vein, the discourse of politicians on this theme is taken seriously by only 14.6% of Quebecers.
However, the information on responsible consumption seems essential to stimulate this part of the consumption currently animated by almost a third of Quebec consumers.
Indeed, the emptiness and dubious nature of information would calm the ardours of 46% of consumers.
This is the second obstacle identified by the Barometer, after the higher price of these products.
Moreover, a third of Quebecers say they are equally wary about the alleged performance of green products or services that we are trying to put in their hands.
On another note, consumers were almost half in 2010 to have changed their habit and especially changed the brand of one or more products to meet their conviction.
Up to 61%, they have also favored purchases from merchants in their neighborhood and favored the purchase of food products grown in their region, says the Barometer.
Greener and more responsible - the Responsible Consumption Index has reached 64 out of a maximum of 100 for this year - the Homo consumus of Quebec likes to present itself as the king of recycling who seeks to reduce its environmental footprint by worrying about the protection of animals.
However, his social and ecological conscience seems to remain outside his car, according to the Barometer: in 2010, barely 28% of Quebecers said they had used public transit or advocated carpooling to defend their green interests.
Walking and cycling were also supported by 39.1% of the participants in this vast behavioral survey, which has just taken its first shot.
A state loan to buy eXcentris
The Government of Quebec, which has been asked to commit financially to the acquisition of the eXcentris cinema complex by the Angus Company and the Parallèle, agrees to collaborate, but with only one loan.
The Société de développement Angus et le Parallèle, which hoped for a donation of seven million dollars from the Ministry of Culture to buy Daniel Langlois the eXcentris film complex on Saint-Laurent Boulevard by giving it its full cinephilic vocation, do not get what they demanded.
The government agrees to get involved in the matter, but on its own terms.
Rather than the expected donation, the Government of Quebec proposes a loan that would be less than the initial expectations, which could constitute a viable solution on advantageous terms, at least in the opinion of the sources from the state's offices.
A first action plan and a 12.8 million request had already been rejected by Quebec last spring, but requests have since been revised downwards.
A proposal and an action plan were sent to Minister St-Pierre by the president of the Society for Cultural Business Development (SODEC), François Macerola, who leads the eXcentris file.
He sent her a final report about ten days ago.
But the firm’s counter-proposal reached SODEC on Tuesday.
Parallel and Angus have until December 31 to respond.
It seems, however, that the assumption of the gift of the state must really be dismissed.
Recall that the Association of Owners of Cinemas and Cineparks of Quebec was opposed to a direct investment by the State to support the acquisition of eXcentris, arguing that the Ministry of Culture does not have to finance real estate projects, which it has not done for other cinemas.
On the contrary, on the contrary, several stakeholders in the community demanded substantial economic aid.
Daniel Langlois, the master of ceans, for ten years carried at arm's length the author's cinema in his premises of the complex after having financed its construction himself, which could justify a derogation on the part of the Treasury Board.
The lack of cinemas for independent films is glaring in Montreal, and eXcentris was able to retain its customers.
Angus Society and Parallel, headed by Christian Yaccarini, are piloting a private financing campaign, hoping to raise $700 000 to push the wheel of the acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, only one of the three screens, Le Parallèle, continues to broadcast films at eXcentris.
The goal of the future buyers is to reopen the three theaters on February 27 by screening the beautiful film of Xavier Beauvois Des hommes et des Dieus.
But Christian Yaccarini did not recall Le Devoir and we do not know what he and his group are thinking of the downward proposals made to them.
The veil raised on a completed Canada...
U.S. diplomatic cables leaked by WikiLeaks lift the veil on what the U.S. sees as Canadians’ inferiority complex towards Americans, but at the same time they betray their sense of superiority towards Canada.
For example, U.S. diplomats are surprised that not every Canadian voter has Canada-U.S. relations in mind by going to vote and believe that President Obama's visit helped the Conservative government pass its budget in 2009!
The four new communications between the U.S. embassy in Ottawa and Washington, spread out in the public square yesterday, provide more crispy details than substantial content to put under the tooth.
A cable dated September 22, 2008, in the midst of the federal election campaign, recounts the extent to which the United States is not among the issues discussed by political leaders.
To the astonishment of the U.S. ambassador stationed in Ottawa, David Wilkins.
Mr. Wilkins offers some explanation for this situation.
"An almost inherent Canadian inferiority complex may affect Canadian political leaders talking about everyday domestic issues rather than focusing this election on the United States (such as during the 1988 free trade campaign)," he wrote.
The United States is therefore, he concludes, "the proverbial elephant of this election: omnipresent, but potentially too threatening to talk about it."
The ambassador understands all the less this "deafening silence" that the two countries have an intense political and economic relationship.
“Logically,” he said, “the ability of a candidate, a party and even more than one party leader to manage this essential relationship should be a key factor that voters have in mind in voting.”
The other expression of this sense of importance can be reflected in the cable sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, Chargé d'affaires in Ottawa, preparing President Barack Obama for his first visit to Ottawa.
"Your trip will help ensure the survival of the government during the vote of confidence on the federal budget at the beginning of February, by which Canada will post its first deficit in more than a decade because of a recovery plan of 30 to 40 billion dollars," Breese wrote.
Nowhere is there any mention that the government had to extend Parliament before Christmas to avoid being overthrown by the coalition and that the opposition parties made its survival conditional on the quality of the proposed recovery plan.
Another communication, dated January 2008 and signed by Ambassador Wilkins, warns Washington of the increasingly negative image that the drama series broadcast on CBC are returning from the United States.
The ambassador is concerned about the programs Border, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which we observe unsubtle references to Maher Arar, the "No-Fly-List" or the kidnappings for muscular interrogations of the CIA.
Another cable reports that, during their first bilateral meeting, Hillary Clinton asked her French counterpart Bernard Kouchner if Europe was ready to welcome some prisoners from Guantánamo.
Mr Kouchner said yes, but on the basis of the "case by case".
At the end of the meeting, it reads, "he handed over a paper to the Secretary of State about Omar Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin."
"The secretary has agreed to review the file."
Finally, it should be noted that the spirits are warming about the WikiLeaks website.
Tom Flanagan, a former adviser very close to Stephen Harper, said on CBC that the head of this massive leak should be "murdered".
He echoed Mike Huckabee in the United States.
A Global Plan to Renovate Lighting
The U.N. proposed yesterday in Cancún a plan for the renovation of domestic, commercial and industrial lighting in 100 countries with a view to obtaining a reduction in greenhouse gases (GHGs) that corresponds to almost one-third of the additional reduction that must be made to stabilize the global climate by 2020.
This ambitious plan lies in the wake of the major concrete projects that the UN hopes to pass in Cancún to compensate for the lack of binding reduction commitments by the industrialized countries.
According to the plan filed yesterday, if incandescent bulbs that still monopolize 50 to 70% of the market were replaced by compact fluorescent bulbs or, better, light-emitting diode (DEL or "LED" bulbs), most of the 1.8 billion tonnes (Gt) of GHGs emitted on the planet to illuminate humans would be avoided.
Lighting is responsible for 8% of the world’s emissions.
That’s the equivalent of 70% of global fleet emissions.
In Copenhagen last year, major emitting countries, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, have embarked on voluntary reduction plans that are expected to increase overall GHG emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
But it will be necessary to subtract from this balance sheet another 5 Gt in order to stabilise by 2020 the rise of the climate to 2 degrees centigrade in order to prevent it from becoming out of control.
In incandescent bulbs, 95% of the energy dissipates in the form of heat and the rest, i.e. 5%, produces light.
With compact fluorescent bulbs, the energy expenditure is reduced by 75% and with the new LED bulbs, we reduce this need for energy by twice again.
Compact fluorescent light bulbs last on average from 6000 to 10 000 hours.
The new LED light bulbs, such as those launched by the manufacturer Philips this week - this company participated in the UN study - have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consume 80% less energy than an incandescent bulb, which gives an idea of the possible savings with a replacement plan that would apply to 100 countries.
These brand new light bulbs can also work with rheostats in addition to emit very little heat.
They do not contain any mercury.
Several specialists have shown reservations to compact fluorescent light bulbs because they contain mercury and must be disposed of as hazardous wastes, which is not always possible in developing countries, unless manufacturers have instructions in place.
But, according to the report, the main source of mercury emission on the planet is not the compact fluorescent bulb, but rather coal-fired power plants, which emit a lot of mercury to produce electricity wasted in incandescent light bulbs.
In the United States alone, these plants emit 25 000 tonnes of mercury per year.
The report details the gains that each country would make through this international programme.
For example, Indonesia would thus spare one billion dollars per year in electricity purchases and eight million tonnes of CO2, the equivalent of 3.5 thermal power plants, the construction of which would cost it 2.5 billion.
Côte d'Ivoire expects the announcement of the electoral results
UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has called for the publication "without delay" of the results of the presidential election in Côte d'Ivoire, following the expiry of the deadlines.
"The Secretary-General emphasizes the need for the Independent Electoral Commission to complete its work and to announce the provisional results without further delay," said a spokesman for the head of the United Nations.
"It calls on all Ivorian parties and institutions to exercise responsibility and to refrain from any initiative until the announcement of the provisional results."
Despite the pressure exerted by some foreign governments, the electoral commission has missed the deadline of Wednesday midnight without publishing the results, three days after Sunday's election.
Approximately forty minutes before the expiry of the deadline, the president of the CIS had said on television that the commission "continued to work" to announce the results.
Calls multiplied Wednesday in the world for the CIS to finally proclaim the name of the winner of the second round between the outgoing Laurent Gbagbo and the opponent Alassane Ouattara.
But Camp Gbagbo counts on the cancellation by the Constitutional Council of "fraudulent" votes in the north, held by the ex-Rebellion of the Forces nouvelles (FN) since the failed coup in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, Ivorian president's campaign manager, said that voter bullying maneuvers practiced by the FN in the north of the country had deterred supporters of the outgoing president from going to vote.
The entire election could be annulled if Gbagbo's complaints are withheld by the Constitutional Council.
Gbagbo's opponents believe that the country's highest court is not neutral because it is led by Paul Yao N'Dré, a close political ally of Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, a man of the North who denies any connection with the ex-rebels, considered "unacceptable" the delay in the announcement of the results and asked Gbagbo to respect them, but he did not go so far as to claim victory.
His allies claim that Gbagbo is blocking the release of the results because he knows that he has lost and that filing a complaint before the Constitutional Council, having prevented the electoral commission from releasing the results, would be illegal.
The second round of Sunday's presidential election was supposed to crown the process of reunification and reconciliation of a country that remained split in half for years.
The United Nations warned the Ivorian political class on Wednesday that it could be held responsible for possible violence.
Laurent Gbagbo had finished in the lead of the first round, on 31 October, with 38% of the vote against 32% in Alassane Ouattara, who received public support from former President Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round).
The unemployment rate remained stable in France in the 3rd quarter
The unemployment rate in France within the meaning of the International Labour Office (ILO) has remained stable at 9.3% in metropolitan France (9.7% by including the overseas departments), show the seasonally corrected data (CVS) provisional published Thursday by INSEE.
The number of unemployed people stands at 2.631 million in metropolitan France.
Data for the second quarter were confirmed at 9.3% of the working population in metropolitan France and 9.7% with the overseas departments.
Over one year, the unemployment rate increased by 0.1 percentage points.
More broadly, 3.4 million people in metropolitan France did not work in the third quarter but wanted to work, whether or not they were available within two weeks to work and whether or not they were looking for a job.
The situation of young people on the labour market has deteriorated further, with a further increase in the unemployment rate, increased to 24.2% in metropolis (+ 0.9 points over three months, +0.5 point over a year) and 25% by including the overseas departments.
In the third quarter, 637,000 young people between the ages of 15 and 24 were unemployed within the meaning of the ILO.
For their part, seniors experienced a slight increase in unemployment, at 6.4% in metropolitan France (+0.2 points over three months as well as over a year), i.e. 482,000 people aged 50 or over, and 6.6% with the overseas departments.
Only 25-49-year-olds benefited from unemployment stability in the third quarter, to 8.3% in metropolitan France (-0.1 point over three months, +0.1 points over one year) and 8.8% throughout France.
INSEE no longer publishes monthly unemployment rates since 2006, after a controversy over its calculation method.
On the other hand, Eurostat, the statistical agency of the European Union, continues to do so and announced Tuesday a rate of 9.8% for France (DOM included) in October after 9.9% in September.
Alexandre Guérini indicted and detained
Alexandre Guérini, 53 years old, manager of waste treatment companies and brother of the PS president of the general council of Bouches-du-Rhône, was indicted and imprisoned at the Luynes prison, near Aix-en-Provence as part of an investigation into alleged fraudulent public procurement.
Alexandre Guérini has been indicted for abuse of social property, embezzlement of funds and public property, concealment, active corruption, trading of influence and possession of ammunition, says his lawyer.
"Alexandre Guérini is combative and wishes to explain himself on all the questions that the magistrate will ask him, and not fantasies."
"It's easy to prosecute him for a multitude of reprehensible leaders, it's something else to prove them," his lawyer, Florence Rault, told AFP before his client's hearing.
Mr. Guérini, brother of the strongman of the PS in the department, Jean-Noël Guérini, tried, through his lawyers, to pass to the judge before his hearing a memo to explain himself.
One of his relatives, Philippe Rapezzi, was also indicted Wednesday night and jailed.
According to his lawyer, Mr. Lucien Simon, his client has never done anything other than to operate the discharges he had to manage.
With regard to the awarding of contracts or the destination of funds related to their operation, he has never been aware of them, said Me Simon.
In this case, Daniel Pinna, former director general of services of the agglomeration of Aubagne, was indicted Wednesday for embezzlement of public funds and complicity, and left free under judicial control with prohibition to meet the protagonists of the file.
"My client knew how to provide explanations to the facts against him."
"The investigating magistrate took this into account by not depriving him of his freedom," said his lawyer, Yann Arnoux.
Four other people had been indicted and released in the night from Tuesday to Wednesday, with placement under judicial supervision for two of them, said the deputy prosecutor of the Republic Michel Raffin.
Michel Karabadjakian, Deputy Director-General for Cleanliness of Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), is being prosecuted for "trafficking in influence and passive corruption".
Two officials from the agglomeration of Aubagne, Michèle Duval and Christophe Bringuier, are indicted one for "subtraction and embezzlement of public property by a person in charge of a public service mission", the other for "complicity of embezzlement of public funds".
Finally, Alexandre Guérini's companion, Jeannie Peretti, was indicted for "concellation of abuses of social property".
Among the people who were confronted by Mr. Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, recently imprisoned in a parallel case of fraudulent public procurement relating to Corsica.
Investigators suspect his companies were used to launder money from big banditry.
The investigation is carried out in the context of several judicial information opened against X in April 2009 following anonymous letters, for alleged fraud in public procurement, in particular in the waste sector.
From a source close to the investigation, the investigations focus in particular on the conditions of allocation and operation of a landfill of La Ciotat, which collects waste from Aubagne as well as some cities in the urban community of Marseille (MPM).
Clotilde Reiss case: the Elysée exaggerated Syria's mediation
For more than ten months, from July 2009 to May 2010, the fate of Clotilde Reiss, the young French researcher arrested in Iran for "espionage", held the public in suspense in France, put diplomacy on alert, and accentuated the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
All under the watchful eyes of the diplomats of the United States.
U.S. documents obtained by WikiLeaks and consulted by Le Monde reveal some below this crisis.
They describe how the Elysée explained its strategy to the Americans, going so far as to give them advice for the treatment of their own "hostage" crises in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show how French officials sought to convince Washington that the release of the young Frenchwoman did not result in the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian detained in France and wanted by the U.S. judiciary for trafficking materials for the benefit of Iran's nuclear program.
Finally, they place in a harsh light the way the French presidency sought to manipulate the media to accredit the idea that Syrian President Bashar Al-Assad had played a central role in the liberation of Clotilde Reiss.
In reality, it was not.
A diplomatic telegram written on August 12, 2009 in Paris is entitled "Iran: French comments on how to deal with hostage cases".
The Elysée's advisor for strategic affairs, François Richier, accompanied by the director of the Quai d'Orsay for North Africa and the Middle East, Patrice Paoli, talks with American diplomat Kathleen Allegrone.
He explains the French method.
They advise Americans to do the same to repatriate their own forcibly held nationals in Iran.
"Make noise", they say, "hammer constantly" in the media the words "fundamental human rights", "innocence", "immediate liberation".
It is necessary in order to embarrass the Iranian regime, sensitive to its image.
"Do it all the more as the Iranians ask you not to do it."
Because, past the attempts of discussions through discrete channels, "silence will not solve anything", the Iranians are "masters in delaying maneuvers".
Richier adds that the arrest of foreigners is "a familiar Iranian tactic: hostage-taking for political blackmail."
He announces that Clotilde Reiss' prison release (which will take place on 16 August, the day it takes refuge at the Embassy of France) "will necessitate the payment to Iranians of a large amount of money" (230,000 euros of bail).
And he says that the Elysée anticipates other hostage crises, a phenomenon that "will worsen".
The Iranians could "in the near future arrest another of the 1,800 French citizens living in Iran," he said.
28 August 2009.
Clotilde Reiss has been out of prison for twelve days, but remains stranded in Iran.
According to information: Iranian Majid Kakavand, whose U.S. is calling for extradition for illegal trafficking of sensitive electronic equipment to Iran, is released in Paris, by decision of a judge.
The U.S. embassy, like many observers, is questioning this troubling coincidence.
For Washington, which hunts down Iran’s clandestine networks to acquire equipment for the nuclear program, Majid Khakavand’s expansion is bad news.
Has France accepted a bargain?
The liberation of the Iranian against that of the Frenchwoman?
During a new interview with Kathleen Allegrone on September 4, François Richier assures that this is not the case.
The Elysée, he said, was not only "completely taken by surprise by the judge's decision", but sought to oppose it.
"There have been lively exchanges between the French presidency and the guardian of the seals" (at the time, Michèle Alliot-Marie), but "the French government can not reverse the judicial decision", he observes.
Mr. Richier also wants to demonstrate that Majid Kakavand remains under the "narrow" surveillance of the French intelligence services.
The adviser of the Elysée composes a telephone number, hangs up, then informs the American diplomat that the Iranian "is currently located in buildings in Paris managed by the Iranian embassy".
During the detention of Clotilde Reiss in Iran, the Elysée spares no effort, by means of communiqués and "leaks" organized in the media, to make believe that Syrian President Bashar al-Assad plays an "important" and "influentive" role of "mediator".
The Americans observe this activity not without irony, and note that the manipulation bears fruit since several press articles pass on the official French version.
A telegram dated August 25, 2009 refers to "the 'blitz' media of French officials".
On which so much "gratitude" expressed towards Mr. Assad, wonders the embassy in Paris.
"Despite their statements full of praise, the French officials privately acknowledge that they have only a vague notion of what the Syrians have actually done."
The document then examines the reasons that prompted the Elysée to pour into this fiction.
The intense communication effort "aims to validate Sarkozy's policy of openness to Syria", he analyzes.
A policy launched in 2008, which "was then perceived, by many critical voices in France and abroad, as premature and undeserved".
By "exaggerating the Syrian role", Paris seeks to "demonstrate to Syria what praises it can attract by playing a constructive role in the region".
The goal is also to "sow seeds of discord between Syria and Iran".
The U.S. Embassy knows this from a good source.
On August 11, François Richier told him: "Of course, we don't know if the Syrians did anything."
"But we thanked them anyway."
"This should at least cause confusion among Iranians."
Clotilde Reiss returned to France on May 16, 2010.
Nicolas Sarkozy receives her at the Elysée.
In a statement, he thanked the presidents of Brazil, Senegal, and Syria for their "active role in promoting the release of our compatriot".
No Online Games for Young Koreans Past Midnight
The South Korean government will pass a so-called "Cindrillon" law that will prohibit teenagers from playing online games after midnight by blocking their access, government officials said.
A bill will be submitted to Parliament in December to ask online gaming companies to block access to these games after midnight, to young people under the age of 16, said representatives of the ministries of culture and the family.
“Many children play these games throughout the night and then have difficulty studying at school and having a normal life during the day.”
"We believe that this law is necessary to guarantee their health and their right to sleep," Jo Rin, the minister in charge of the bill, told AFP.
Online games will be available again at 6 a.m.
Companies will have a year to prepare for law enforcement, he said.
The government is also considering asking companies to limit the amount of daily access to these games for young people whose parents request them, the official added.
It will also make available to people who demand it free software that can limit the time spent on the Internet.
South Korea is one of the most "connected" countries and newspapers regularly report miscellaneous facts related to heavy Internet use.
The country is also the undisputed champion of global online gaming competitions.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals at the World Cyber Games against 58 other nations.
2018-2022 World Cup: FIFA designates the organizing countries
In the midst of turmoil due to suspicions of corruption, FIFA's executive committee on Thursday designates the organizing countries of the 2018 and 2022 World Cups, an eminently political choice where struggles of influence and lobbying may take precedence over technical criteria.
One thing is for sure, the 2018 edition of the queen competition of FIFA will take place in Europe, traditional bastion of the discipline (4 candidates: England, Russia, Spain-Portugal, Netherlands-Belgium).
That of 2022 will be much more exotic, the International Federation hoping to conquer new territories (Qatar, Australia), after a first on the African continent in 2010, or continue its establishment in the United States or the Far East (Japan, South Korea).
The list of personalities being part of delegations (David Cameron, Prince William, José Luis Rodriguez Zapatero, José Socrates, Bill Clinton, the Emir of Qatar, Belgian and Dutch heads of government, Zidane, Beckham, Cruyff) gives the measurement of the economic and political stakes of a World Cup.
Behind-the-scenes negotiations are therefore likely to last until the last minute.
For the rest, it is difficult to establish a hierarchy between the candidates.
According to FIFA’s evaluation report, the English, Spanish-Portuguese (2018) and U.S. (2022) files have better arguments than their competitors.
But these considerations will not weigh very heavily on the 22 members of the Executive Committee at the time of the vote.
The Sunday Times, at the origin of the revelations that resulted in the suspension for corruption of two members of the CE (Reynald Temarii and Amos Adamu), could thus have ruined the chances of his country, the cradle of football that hosted the World Cup only once, in 1966.
The BBC handed over a layer on Monday by releasing a documentary that pins three influential CE members, Ricardo Teixeira, Nicolas Leoz and Issa Hayatou.
Enough to displease FIFA and embarrass English officials, who have embarked on intense lobbying in recent weeks with David Cameron on the front lines.
The head of government hopes to repeat the successful coup by his predecessor Tony Blair, which won the organisation of the 2012 Olympics in 2005 for London.
Will this intense activity be enough to restore England's coat of arms to the EC?
Nothing is less safe because the Spain-Portugal duo has no shortage of assets with its stadiums already ready, its expertise (Mondial-82 in Spain, OJ-92 in Barcelona, Euro-2004 in Portugal) and its football culture.
But the bleak economic and financial prospects of the two countries could shower their ambitions.
Possible reprisals against the English and doubts about the solvency of Spain and Portugal are only increasing Russia's rating, which has become an underweight outsider despite the shortcomings of its file (stadiums, hotels, transportation).
But Prime Minister Vladimir Putin decided not to make the trip to Zurich and denounced an "unfair competition in the preparation of the vote", in a thinly veiled allusion to accusations by the English press against EC members.
"We're covering them with mud, we're trying to jeopardize them," Putin said.
For 2022, the United States, part of the 1994 experiment, appears as the logical favorites.
The world's largest economic power, the headquarters of FIFA's main sponsors, the country seems to be one step ahead of Japan and South Korea, which have separately built a very nice project.
But these new applications appear premature for two countries having co-organized the test in 2002.
Another threat: the granting of the World Cup-2026 to China, ardently desired by FIFA, which would in fact nullify the Asian chances for 2022.
With Qatar, which has released the big game by offering futuristic stadiums, and Australia, FIFA would continue to expand into new countries, but these two countries present more risks than certainties.
Australia, such as Japan and Korea, could also suffer from the weakness of TV fees paid (due to jet lag) by European countries, the nerve of war for FIFA.
Second term: the obvious
De Gaulle was really hesitant.
Mitterrand made it look like he was hesitating.
Valéry Giscard d'Estaing and Jacques Chirac did not have any hesitation.
And Nicolas Sarkozy even less than them.
A mandate at the Elysée appeals another.
So the current head of state will be a candidate in 2012.
But the evidence does not mix well with the share of mystery attached to the presidential office.
You have to act as if the doubt still exists.
In one way or the other.
In 2006, even without believing it, Jacques Chirac's friends claimed that a third term was not excluded.
On November 15, with a serious air, Nicolas Sarkozy assured that he would make his decision "somely in the autumn of 2011".
That he evokes in front of parliamentarians UMP, Tuesday evening at the Elysée, the prospect of "two mandates" has therefore sowed a bit of excitement.
Was this the official announcement of his candidacy?
We're a long way from it.
The president only recalled that the constitutional revision of 2008 limited the number of five-years to two.
He made an observation, not an announcement.
This is not the first time he has mentioned the presidential election, in the tone of humour, in such meetings.
“At worst, or at best, you’re still seven and a half years with me,” he said in July 2009.
Rebelote in May 2010: "Don't believe I'm not thinking about the next stage."
His pre-yester words do not make him more candidate than the day before; they make him no less.
If they get comments, that's for a double reason.
Acceleration of the candidacy race for the PS, on the one hand.
But this is not the entry of Ségolène Royal who pushes Nicolas Sarkozy to reveal himself.
On the contrary, it is because his candidacy in 2012 is obvious that many in the PS are calling for an earlier-than-expected order of battle.
Bad polls, on the other hand.
For the first time, right-wing voters would prefer the candidacy of François Fillon to that of Nicolas Sarkozy (46% against 44%, according to BVA-L'Express).
"We have a candidate and there is no discussion at the UMP about it," the Prime Minister's entourage reacted immediately and cautiously.
"If he believes the defeat possible, he will not be a candidate," says Jean-Pierre Raffarin.
"He will always believe he can win", corrects Alain Juppé, resigned obviously.
Euro: ECB forced to maintain purchases of government securities
The last meeting of the Governing Council of the European Central Bank (ECB) of the year should be an opportunity to specify the modalities of exit from the crisis or, at least, to announce the cessation of many of the non-conventional monetary policy operations to be completed at the end of the year.
European sovereign debt crisis obliges, the Frankfurt issuing institute will probably not be able to afford to be as peremptory under penalty of exacerbating a more than tense situation today in European bond markets.
The political cost would be even higher.
Yesterday, at a press conference, the French Minister of Finance, Christine Lagarde, praised the active role played by the ECB in promoting the resolution of the government debt crisis in Europe.
It did not want to say whether it felt that the central bank needed to do more, for example by increasing its sovereign bond purchasing program.
"I reserve my reflection to my European partners (...)".
The day before, in Brussels, during his hearing by the Committee on Economic and Monetary Affairs of the European Parliament, the President of the ECB, Jean-Claude Trichet, did not show himself any more as to his intentions, merely indicating that the programme for the purchase of state securities on the secondary market "continued" and that "we will see what we will decide" at Thursday's meeting.
Only certainty: the ECB will keep its main policy rate unchanged at 1%, its lowest historical level since May 2009.
In fact, purchases of Treasury bonds by the ECB doubled last week to nearly 1.35 billion euros.
And, according to indiscretions by market operators, it made new purchases of Irish and Portuguese securities yesterday.
In total, the central banks of the Eurosystem have bought 67 billion euros since May 2010.
There is no doubt about the expectations of a continuation of purchases among market operators (see page 29).
Economists also do not expect a noticeable change of course.
If loan operations for more than three months have already disappeared from the ECB’s arsenal, it still carries out unlimited liquidity allocations on generous terms and for durations ranging from one week to three months.
Economists today expect that this device will be extended by a few months.
“I can’t imagine that she’s significantly changing her program.”
"Otherwise it would take the risk of provoking new tensions in the short term," said Michael Schubert, an economist at Commerzbank.
"A gradual exit announced during the first quarter of 2011 seems more likely," said Norbert Bräms, an economist at Sal.
Oppenheim.
In terms of its interventions on the public debt market, "the ECB should maintain a purchase volume of these bonds to animate a sluggish market", adds Norbert Bräms.
In view of the importance of European banks' exposures to euro area sovereign debt markets, the issuing institution has no choice but to maintain its market presence.
"It must at all costs replace the lack of investors in the market and act in such a way as to prevent a run-up of yields paid by the most fragile states," said a major bond manager yesterday.
Because if interest rates soar, states will be all the more embarrassed to reduce their indebtedness, if not stabilize it.
Safeguarding the euro is at that price.
In Italy, the Berlusconi government is making a round back
After a worrying start to the week for Italy, the situation was somewhat relaxed, yesterday, with the decline of 10-year bond rates.
A return to precarious calm.
At the end of a meeting with the Minister of the Economy, the presidents of the region said they had heard Giulio Tremonti describe the situation as "indecipherable" and expected an "unpredictable" weekend, which he then denied.
On Tuesday in any case, the gap with German long rates had reached the record of 210 basis points and the price of hedging contracts against the risk of bankruptcy (CDS) had soared to 263 points, leading to some panic in Rome.
In the Council of Ministers, Silvio Berlusconi pointed out that in Spain, the gap with German rates was more than 400 basis points.
But his right-hand man, Gianni Letta, said he was "strongly preoccupied" with "market turbulence" and possible contagion from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the third-largest economy in the eurozone, is not like Greece or Ireland."
“The bad news is that it’s like Portugal,” says Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
Clearly, its problem is the lack of growth and the atonia of tax revenues that goes with it, making the state run the risk of no longer being able to cover its financing needs.
The latest forecast from Brussels has been a cold shower for the government.
Italian growth is now expected at 1.1% in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012... while the budget under consideration in the Senate expects 1.3% and 2% respectively.
Under these conditions, calculates the European Commission, the public deficit will still represent 4.3% of GDP next year (compared to 3.9% foreseen by the government) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
Hence the rumor of a new budgetary collective that could be put in place after the holidays, for an amount of 7 billion euros.
"If necessary, new austerity measures will have to be taken," Economic Affairs Commissioner Olli Rehn said.
For the time being, Italy is receiving support from the international institutions.
"The fundamentals of its economy are in order and its accounts are good, we have been repeating it for a long time," said Arrigo Sadun, Italy's administrator at the International Monetary Fund in charge of Italy at the IMF.
"There is no problem in the immediate future, neither in the short nor in the long term, because in spite of its significant debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, editor's note), the stabilization efforts demanded of Italy are less than those required of other countries," observes Pier Carlo Padoan, OECD Chief Economist.
There remains the issue of political instability.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a motion of censure in the Chamber of Deputies, following which early elections could be held at the end of March.
There would then be an unsuitable period for decision-making in the event of a serious crisis.
Europeans are thinking about strengthening anti-crisis devices
Christine Lagarde hammered it again yesterday as part of a press conference in Paris: "The Europeans, I believe that it was again demonstrated Sunday in the day and in the night, are obviously in solidarity, determined, committed to defending their currency and their monetary zone."
Although all relative, the détente recorded yesterday on the rates of European government bonds has brought water to the mill of the governments and the ECB.
But we can't yet talk about the beginning of normalization.
The rating agency Standard & Poor's has announced that it could degrade Portugal's debt rating, the most targeted target today by speculation.
"As in May, there is a real lack of clarity and political visibility, which weighs in particular on banking systems," say the economists of Exane BNP Paribas.
“Political time is much slower than the time of the markets.”
Aware of this hiatus and the dangers it conceals, European countries are stepping up discussions to strengthen existing anti-crisis mechanisms.
Informal consultations between the most directly concerned finance ministers and, more broadly, with their G20 peers are well underway.
And this also in view of the meetings of the Eurogroup and the Ecofin at the beginning of next week.
The EBC is meeting today.
On the menu, the fate of non-conventional measures to support the countries of the euro area in the market collimator (see above).
As for the European Financial Stability Fund (ESFF), its Executive Director, Klaus Regling, began a tour of Asia in Singapore on Tuesday to inform Far-Eastern investors of the developments of the crisis in Europe.
If the objective of these visits is not to raise funds, he certainly prepares the ground.
EFSF’s first securities issues are scheduled for next month.
Yesterday, a U.S. official told Reuters that the U.S. would support a possible increase in funds mobilized by an additional direct commitment from the IMF.
The countries on the front line, Portugal and Spain, meanwhile, are preparing to adopt structural reforms, in particular with regard to the organisation of the labour market.
Remedies in addition to austerity measures with a closer effect.
Lisbon and Madrid want to take a different path from Greece and Ireland.
Botth of them are keen to avoid asking for international aid before putting in place their own budgetary corrections and deciding on the structural reforms necessary to restore lasting confidence.
However, European donors are talking about the options available to accelerate the process of normalization of sovereign bond markets.
While in Paris on 24 November, Axel Weber had the first step forward the idea that if the 750 billion euros mobilized by Europe and the International Monetary Fund were not enough, the Union would not hesitate to hand over to the portfolio.
The economists of Barclays Capital, a leading player in the European sovereign debt market, consider that an extension of about 100 billion euros would suffice, "preferably" in the form of a direct injection of liquidity.
The vehicle exists: it is the European Financial Stabilisation Mechanism with 60 billion euros to date.
A council that has every chance to be carefully examined in European capitals.
Especially if market conditions get worse.
Snow and ice: 24 departments in orange vigilance
Snowy episodes still create traffic difficulties, especially on the secondary network in Brittany, and may disrupt certain economic activities.
Twenty-four departments are now on orange due to the risk of snow and ice.
L'Eure, Eure-et-Loir, Deux-Sèvres and Vienne, were added to the 20 departments already in orange vigilance, 18 since Wednesday afternoon and two, the Dordogne and the Gironde, later in the night.
According to Meteo France, sustained snow showers affect the north of the Gironde and the Charentes and it snows weakly on the Ile-de-France.
Layers of snow ranging from 2 to 5 cm, punctually from 7 to 10 cm, are expected for the departments concerned, and from 1 to 3 cm, even up to 5 cm for the Paris region.
The departments concerned are those of Charente, Charente-Maritime, Côtes d'Armor, Finistère, Gironde, Dordogne, Poitou and those of the Lower Normandy, Ile-de-France and Limousin regions.
Snowy episodes are noticeablely noticeable enough to create traffic difficulties and disrupt certain economic activities, warns Météo France.
In Côtes d'Armor, several departments have been banned to heavy trucks of more than 7.5 tonnes, including Guingamp-Lannion and Saint-Brieuc-Loudéac.
The traffic on the secondary network in Brittany is very difficult according to the gendarmes who recommend taking his vehicle only in case of necessity and the circulation of some TER buses has been suspended for the day.
School transport was banned for Thursday in Picardy.
No specific measures were envisaged Wednesday evening to restrict the movement of trucks.
The prefecture "invites all motorists to the utmost caution on the roads and to adapt their driving accordingly".
The Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGAC) has asked the airlines to cancel Thursday 25% of their flights at the Paris airport of Roissy and 10% in Orly due to upcoming snowfall.
DGAC recommends that air passengers contact their airline.
In Britain, Gatwick Airport, south of London, and that of Edinburgh, Scotland, are closed until Thursday morning.
Metro Grand Paris: first financial sketches
Since the debate around the modernization of transport in Ile-de-France is in full swing, the billions are raining.
In the discussion at least.
In particular, 23 billion is mentioned for the Grand Paris metro that the Société du Grand Paris must build.
To start this SGP, the State has announced a capital endowment of 4 billion euros.
In the theoretical scheme, it is this sum that will then allow the SGP to borrow on the markets a share of the financing of its dual loop.
Today we finally come to the first additions.
The Amending Finance Act 2010, examined in the Finance Committee of the National Assembly this morning, contains the mechanisms planned to find the beginning of this money.
There are two: the creation of a special equipment tax and an increase in the tax on offices in Ile-de-France.
The first, we read in the text of the bill, article 15, is "for the benefit of the public establishment Société du Grand Paris".
It is quantified: "The product of this tax is 117 million euros per year".
The second is a reorganization of an office tax that already exists "in order to modernize it, to increase the product and to allocate this increase to the Société du Grand Paris".
How much would it pay?
Mystery.
It's not encrypted in the text.
We're far from 4 billion.
"It is specified that these fiscal resources are in addition to the 4 billion euros of the capital endowment that will be mobilized by the State and brought to the Société du Grand Paris as the project proceeds," reads the statement of reasons.
At the SGP, we can't find this system shocking.
"From the moment we create revenues (the two taxes), we can cover the studies over three years and then type in the 4 billion later, it is explained."
"This will be included in the budget and drawn as you go along."
The SGP estimates that the 4 billion will begin to be entered in 2013 for the 2014 budget.
This device, in which the promised 4 billion are still for absent subscribers, is likely to provoke a somewhat agitated parliamentary discussion.
First open session on 7 December.
Avenged hackers and spies in diligence
The WikiLeaks case has dual value.
On the one hand, it proves to be an apparent scandal, a scandal that appears as scandal only in the face of the hypocrisy that governs the relations between states, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it heralds profound changes at the international level, and foreshadows a future dominated by regression.
Let's proceed in order.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is the confirmation that each file constituted by a secret service (of any nation) is composed exclusively of press clippings.
Berlusconi’s “extraordinary” revelations about Berlusconi’s sexual habits only relate what for months could be read in any diary (except those that Berlusconi owns), and Khadafi’s sinteringly caricatural profile had long been for cabaret sketch artists.
The rule that secret files should only be composed of already known short stories is essential to the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
If you go to a library devoted to esoteric publications, you will see that each book repeats (on the Grail, the mystery of Rennes-le-Château, the Templars or the Rose-Cross) exactly what was already written in the previous books.
And this not only because the author of occult texts does not like to do unpublished research (nor does it know where to look for news about the non-existent), but because those who dedicate themselves to occultism believe only what they already know, and who reconfirms what they had already learned.
That's Dan Brown's mechanism of success.
Same for the secret files.
The informant is lazy, and lazy (or limited-spirit) the head of the secret services (otherwise he could be, what do I know, editor at Libération), who retains as true only what he acknowledges.
The top-secret information about Berlusconi that the U.S. Embassy in Rome sent to the State Department was the same that Newsweeek published the week before.
So why did the revelations on these files make so much noise?
On the one hand, they say what any educated person already knows, namely that embassies, at least since the end of the Second World War, and since the heads of state can call themselves or take a plane to meet at dinner, have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small performance exercises, have turned into espionage centers.
Any viewer of investigative films knows this very well, and it's only out of hypocrisy that we're pretending to ignore it.
However, repeating it publicly violates the duty of hypocrisy, and serves to place American diplomacy in a bad light.
Secondly, the idea that any hacker could capture the most secret secrets of the world's most powerful country deals a significant blow to the prestige of the State Department.
So the scandal does not put the victims into crisis so much as the "executives".
But let’s come to the deep nature of what happened.
Formerly in Orwell's time, one could conceive of any power like a Big Brother who controlled every gesture of his subjects.
The Orwellian prophecy had been completely revealed since that, being able to control every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction carried out, the hotel visited, the highway used and so on, the citizen became the total victim of the eye of power.
But when we demonstrate, as it happens now, that even the crypts of the secrets of power cannot escape the control of a hacker, the control report ceases to be unidirectional and becomes circular.
Power controls every citizen, but every citizen, or at least the hacker – elected as avenger of the citizen – can know all the secrets of power.
How can a power that no longer has the opportunity to keep its own secrets?
It is true, Georg Simmel already said, that a real secret is an empty secret (and empty secret can never be revealed); it is true, also, that knowing everything about the character of Berlusconi or Merkel is indeed an empty secret of secrecy, because belonging to the public domain; but revealing, as WikiLeaks did, that Hillary Clinton’s secrets were empty secrets means removing all power from him.
WikiLeaks did no harm to Sarkozy or Merkel, but made it too big for Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this wound inflicted on very powerful power?
It is obvious that in the future, states will no longer be able to put online any reserved information - this would be tantamount to publishing it on a poster glued around the corner.
But it is equally obvious that with current technologies, it is futile to hope to be able to maintain confidential reports by telephone.
There's nothing easier than finding out if and when a head of state flew and contacted one of his colleagues.
How in the future can private and reserved reports be maintained?
I know well that, for the moment, my prediction is science fiction and is therefore romantic, but I am obliged to imagine government agents who move discreetly in diligences with uncontrollable itineraries, carrying only messages learned by heart or, at most, by hiding the rare information written in the heel of a shoe.
The information will be kept as a one-size-fits-all copy in locked drawers: at the bottom, Watergate’s spying attempt was less successful than WikiLeaks.
“I’ve had the opportunity to write that technology is now progressing in crab, that is, backwards.
A century after the wireless telegraph revolutionized communications, the Internet re-established a telegraph on wire (telephonics).
(Analog) videotapes had allowed film researchers to explore a step-by-step film, going back and forth and discovering all the secrets of editing, while now (digital) CDs only make it possible to jump from chapter to chapter, i.e. by macroportions.
With high-speed trains, we go from Rome to Milan in three hours, while by plane, and the trips it includes, it takes three and a half hours.
It is therefore not extraordinary that the politics and communication techniques go back to cars on horseback.
One last observation.
In the past, the press was trying to figure out what goes through the secret of the embassies.
Now it is the embassies that request confidential information from the press.
Climate: how France hacks its promises of funding
A year after the Copenhagen Agreement, it is time to review the fulfilment of the promises of rapidly unlocked (so-called fast-start or early-stage funding) made by developed countries.
And the big French hacks are revealed.
In the Copenhagen agreement, developed countries pledged US$30 billion between 2010 and 2012.
The European Union has promised 7.2 billion euros over three years and France 1.26 billion euros (420 million per year).
At the opening of COP16, the EU released the big game to persuade its partners of the reality of its commitment to fast-start financing: report detailing the action of its Member States, press conference, large side event, distribution of brochures...
Transparency is appreciable and appreciated: developing countries and civil society would like all developed countries to do the same with the same level.
The EU announces that it has released 2.2 billion euros in 2010, which puts it on the right path to meet its commitment of 7.2 billion in 2012.
But when you look in detail, things are more complicated.
This money must, according to the Copenhagen agreement, be "new and additional", in order to ensure that an additional effort is made without being at the expense of Official Development Assistance.
Unfortunately, there is no agreement on what "new and additional" means.
While the European Commission had proposed harmonisation, some Member States whose procedure France blocked.
Today, everyone chooses the reference that best suits them.
For example, the European Commission has seen as a reference for the year 2009: international funding is maintained, and adds 150 million over three years for fast start financing.
Other countries have chosen similar references in terms of the year or in terms of the level of official development assistance.
On the other hand, France believes that the financing of "all new projects" of 2010 is "new and additional".
In other words, there is no reference to the expenditures of previous years or to the overall level of Official Development Assistance.
Concretely, the total amount of France's support to developing countries can remain stable (or even decrease), it claims to meet its commitment in terms of "new and additional" financing...
Another crucial element is the distribution between donations and loans.
Again, there is no rule and everyone chooses his or her calculation method.
Some, such as France, consider that 1 € on loan is equivalent to 1 € given.
Of course, this is hardly justifiable: the effort is not comparable.
If the use of loans can be justified for certain investments that are profitable (often projects related to the mitigation of emissions that rely on more efficient technologies), it is misleading to present a euro that will be recovered as an equivalent to one euro that we give... At most, should be counted under its commitments only the cost of the loan for the lending country (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France has nothing to do with such a reasoning: it "respects" its commitment of 420 million euros by making 91.5% of loans (389.8 million euros) and 8.5% donations (36.1 million euros) - see the EU document to verify for yourself.
It is the only country in the Union to use the loan so massively.
Germany requires 46%, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia (in others) respect their commitments with 100% donations.
In other words, when the vast majority of the European Union put 1 € to satisfy its commitment, France puts only 10 cents and considers that the effort is comparable.
Nice pass tour!
In summary, in its fast start financing, France operates a dual scam: on the one hand on the evolution of the action over time and on the other hand on the reality of the financial effort.
By spending only 36.1 million euros, it manages to make believe that it spends 420 million "new and additional" in coherence with its promises.
The manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (because it seems that it was Bercy who carried out the arbitration) could have a double negative impact, both within the EU (why the other Member States would continue to give if it is enough to lend?) and in the negotiations since the developing countries are not completely fooled...
The chapter of early funding is only a short-term, relative issue in relation to all climate discussions.
But it could be one more grain of sand in the huge machine that is the UNFCCC.
Let’s hope that won’t.
In any case, it is essential to draw lessons from this first year of early funding.
First of all, France could announce a change in its method of accounting for the years 2011 and 2012, in a spirit of respect for its commitments and equity with the other countries of the European Union.
But above all, France could advance discussions on long-term financing issues (another crucial chapter currently under discussion): we will not escape the question of "additionality" or the mode of accounting for loans.
France could change its position to become as progressive on these issues as it is on others.
There is therefore hope for the new Minister of the Environment, Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
A surprise announcement about France’s fast start and its vision of “additionality” and the use of loans is likely to increase the level of international confidence needed to move forward in the discussions.
Pesticide alert ingested by children
A 10-year-old ingests 81 different chemical substances in a day, according to a study published this Wednesday by the association Générations Futures (formerly the Movement for rights and respect for future generations).
The association, which warns that its results "do not have significant statistical value in view of the small number of samples analysed, but are illustrative of the problem treated", bought between July and September the food generally consumed in four meals and a snack in one day by a child of about ten years, in the supermarkets of Oise and Paris.
These typical meals, including official recommendations - five fresh fruits and vegetables, three dairy products and 1.5 liters of water per day - as well as treats, have been examined by laboratories looking for chemicals, pesticides, heavy metals and other pollutants.
The assessment of the plate is overwhelming: 81 chemicals, 42 of which are classified as carcinogenic possible or probable and five substances classified as carcinogenic as well as 37 substances likely to act as endocrine disruptors (EPs), according to studies by several institutions such as the International Agency for Research on Cancer.
"Although, even if, in almost all cases, the legal limits for each chemical substance taken individually are not exceeded, we can clearly see in our survey that the reality of consumer exposure to potentially carcinogenic and/or PE contaminants is worrying," the association notes.
For breakfast, butter and milk tea alone contain more than a dozen possible carcinogenic residues and three proven as carcinogens certain as well as nearly twenty residues likely to disrupt the hormonal system.
The apple, imported from Brazil, planned as a snack by the study, shows traces of six chemicals including a fungicide yet banned in France.
Same goes for green beans from Kenya, included in lunch, on which the study has detected traces of a powerful insecticide banned in the EU.
In addition, chopped steak, canned tuna, and even bread baguette and chewing gum were full of pesticides and other chemicals.
In tap water the analyses revealed the presence of nitrates and chloroform.
For dinner, the salmon steak was the most "rich" with 34 chemical residues detected and even the examination of the plastic plate used to rehease the meal in the microwave was not exempt.
The final risk to the consumer of this "contaminant cocktails" "is probably underestimated", according to the study.
The Alliance Europe Ecologie-Les Verts (EE-LV), for its part, proposed "facing the urgency of the situation" to establish a moratorium on the use of pesticides, in order to stop "the increase in diseases of civilization".
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Agency (EFSA) reported the presence of residues of 365 different pesticides identified in fruits and vegetables consumed in Europe, including 76 in cereals.
